/* Seviye sınavları ve çalışma bölümü (beta). tools/sinav/build.py ile üretilir; elle düzenleme. */
window.SINAV={"exams":[{"id":"a2-1","t":"Sınav 1","sec":{"R":{"min":40,"parts":[{"k":"R1","nm":"Okuma 1 · Kısa metinler","ti":"Reading Part 1","ins":"Her kısa metni oku ve metnin ne söylediğini en iyi anlatan seçeneği bul.","ty":"mc","q":[{"q":"What does the notice say?","o":["You must pay if you bring books back late.","The library is closed every Friday afternoon.","You have to pay 20p a day to borrow a book."],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Geç getirilen kitaplar için günde 20 peni ödenir.","x":"LIBRARY\nPlease return books by Friday.\nLate books cost 20p a day."},{"q":"What is Ella telling her mum?","o":["She wants her mum to collect her at 8.","She will eat dinner at Sara's house.","She is going to cook pizza tonight."],"a":1,"tg":["purpose","will-going-to"],"e":"\"Don't cook for me – we're having pizza\" = akşam yemeğini Sara'larda yiyecek. Onu Sara'nın babası getirecek.","x":"Mum,\nI'm at Sara's house. Her dad will drive me home at 8. Don't cook for me – we're having pizza!\nElla"},{"q":"What does the sign say?","o":["The café closes every Monday in August.","You can go to the café on 15th August.","The café is closed for the whole of August."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Kafe 1–14 Ağustos kapalı; 15 Ağustos pazartesi yeniden açık.","x":"CAFÉ\nClosed for holidays 1st – 14th August.\nOpen again on Monday 15th."},{"q":"What has changed?","o":["the time of the match","the day of the match","the place of the match"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Maç cumartesiden pazara alındı; saat aynı.","x":"Hi Tom,\nThe football match is now on Sunday, not Saturday, because the pitch is too wet. Same time – 10 o'clock.\nCoach Ben"},{"q":"What does the notice say?","o":["Students don't need to take a drink.","Students will be given a free lunch at the zoo.","Students can buy food at the zoo."],"a":0,"tg":["inference","must-have-to-should"],"e":"\"Drinks will be provided\" = içecek verilecek, getirmeye gerek yok. Öğle yemeğini öğrenciler kendisi getirmeli.","x":"SCHOOL TRIP TO THE ZOO\nStudents must bring a packed lunch.\nDrinks will be provided."},{"q":"Why is the person selling the bike?","o":["It has some problems and needs repairs.","It is too small for their child.","Their son has already got a new one."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"Too small for my son now\" = oğluna küçük geliyor.","x":"FOR SALE\nBoy's bike, 2 years old, good condition. Too small for my son now. £40.\nCall 07700 900123 after 6 p.m."}]},{"k":"R2","nm":"Okuma 2 · Kişi eşleştirme","ti":"Reading Part 2","ins":"Üç metni oku. Her soru için doğru kişiyi (A, B ya da C) seç. Bir kişi birden fazla kez seçilebilir.","ty":"match","pl":"Tatil yazıları","opts":[["A","Lucy","Every summer my family goes camping by a lake in the mountains. We sleep in a tent, and I share it with my older sister. In the mornings we go swimming, even though the water is really cold! There's no internet there, which I didn't like at first, but now I enjoy reading books instead. My favourite part is cooking dinner on a fire in the evening."],["B","Mark","Last year we went to Rome for a week. We stayed in a hotel in the city centre, so we walked everywhere. I loved the old buildings and the food – I ate ice cream every day! It was very hot, so in the afternoons we went back to the hotel and slept. I'd like to go back one day, but first I want to learn some Italian."],["C","Ana","My grandparents live on a farm, and I spend every August with them. I help my grandfather with the animals – we've got cows, chickens and two horses. I learned to ride a horse this year. My friends think it's boring, but I love it because it's so quiet. The only thing I don't like is getting up at six o'clock every morning!"]],"q":[{"q":"Who shares a tent with a family member?","a":"A","tg":["detail"],"e":"Lucy çadırı ablasıyla paylaşıyor."},{"q":"Who learned to do something new this year?","a":"C","tg":["detail","past-simple"],"e":"Ana bu yıl ata binmeyi öğrendi."},{"q":"Who couldn't use the internet on holiday?","a":"A","tg":["detail"],"e":"Lucy: \"There's no internet there\"."},{"q":"Who wants to study a language?","a":"B","tg":["detail"],"e":"Mark önce biraz İtalyanca öğrenmek istiyor."},{"q":"Who has to get up very early?","a":"C","tg":["detail"],"e":"Ana her sabah 6'da kalkıyor."},{"q":"Who went everywhere on foot?","a":"B","tg":["inference"],"e":"Mark otel şehir merkezinde olduğu için her yere yürüdü."},{"q":"Who says that other people don't like this kind of holiday?","a":"C","tg":["attitude"],"e":"Ana: \"My friends think it's boring\"."}]},{"k":"R3","nm":"Okuma 3 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading Part 3","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","ty":"mc","title":"My brother the chef","p":["My brother Daniel is twenty-two and he works as a chef in a busy restaurant in Manchester. When he was at school, he wasn't very interested in cooking. He wanted to be a footballer, and he played for the school team every weekend.","Everything changed when he was sixteen. He broke his leg in a match and had to stay at home for two months. He was bored, so he started watching cooking programmes on TV. Then he began making dinner for the family. At first his food wasn't very good, but he got better quickly.","After school, Daniel went to college and studied cooking for two years. It was hard work, and he often had to start at seven in the morning. But he loved it, and his teachers said he was one of the best students in his class.","Now Daniel works five days a week, usually in the evenings. He doesn't have much free time, but he says he's never been happier. His dream is to open his own small restaurant by the sea. I hope he does – then I can eat there for free!"],"q":[{"q":"What did Daniel want to be when he was at school?","o":["a famous chef","a footballer","a PE teacher"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"He wanted to be a footballer\"."},{"q":"Why did Daniel start cooking?","o":["He had nothing to do at home.","His mother asked him to help.","He wanted to be on TV."],"a":0,"tg":["inference"],"e":"Bacağı kırıldı, evde canı sıkıldı; yemek programları izleyip yemek yapmaya başladı."},{"q":"What does the writer say about Daniel's first meals?","o":["They were excellent.","Nobody wanted to eat them.","They weren't very good."],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"At first his food wasn't very good\"."},{"q":"What was Daniel's college course like?","o":["It was difficult, but he enjoyed it.","It finished at seven every evening.","It was easy for him because he could already cook."],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"hard work ... But he loved it\". Saat 7 başlama saati, bitiş değil."},{"q":"What would Daniel like to do in the future?","o":["work fewer days","open a restaurant","cook for his brother"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"His dream is to open his own small restaurant by the sea\"."}]},{"k":"R4","nm":"Okuma 4 · Çoktan seçmeli boşluk","ti":"Reading Part 4","ins":"Metni oku ve her boşluk için doğru kelimeyi seç.","ty":"cloze","title":"The giant panda","p":"Giant pandas live in the mountains of China. They (1) ______ most of the day eating bamboo – up to 14 hours! A baby panda is very small when it is born, about the (2) ______ size as a mouse. Pandas usually live (3) ______ their own, but mothers stay with their babies for about two years. There (4) ______ only about 1,800 pandas in the wild today, but the number is growing. Scientists (5) ______ hard to protect them, and many people visit zoos (6) ______ see these beautiful animals.","o":[[["use","spend","take"],1,["collocation"]],[["like","equal","same"],2,["vocab"]],[["on","by","in"],0,["vocab"]],[["is","are","has"],1,["there-is-are"]],[["are working","works","is work"],0,["present-continuous"]],[["for","so","to"],2,["gerund-infinitive"]]],"e":["spend time doing: zaman geçirmek.","the same size as: ile aynı boyda.","live on their own: kendi başlarına yaşamak.","There are + çoğul (pandas).","Şu sıralar süren eylem: are working.","Amaç: to + fiil (to see)."]},{"k":"R5","nm":"Okuma 5 · Açık boşluk","ti":"Reading Part 5","ins":"Metni oku ve her boşluğa TEK kelime yaz.","ty":"open","one":true,"title":"Hi Sam","p":"Hi Sam,\nThanks (1) ______ your message. I'm sorry I didn't reply yesterday – I was very busy. My cousins came to stay with us (2) ______ the weekend. We went to the beach on Saturday and it (3) ______ really sunny. On Sunday we visited a museum. It was (4) ______ interesting than I thought! Are you free next week? (5) ______ you like to come to the cinema with me? There's a new film that I really want (6) ______ see.\nLove,\nMia","a":[["for"],["for","at","over"],["was"],["more"],["would"],["to"]],"tg":[["vocab"],["prepositions-in-on-at"],["past-simple"],["comparatives-superlatives"],["question-words"],["gerund-infinitive"]],"e":["Thanks for: ... için teşekkürler.","for / at / over the weekend: hafta sonu için / boyunca.","Geçmiş zaman: it was sunny.","Uzun sıfat karşılaştırma: more interesting than.","Kibar davet: Would you like to ...?","want to + fiil."]}]},"L":{"min":30,"parts":[{"k":"L1","nm":"Dinleme 1 · Kısa konuşmalar","ti":"Listening Part 1","ins":"Beş kısa konuşma dinleyeceksin. Her soru için doğru cevabı seç.","ty":"mc","q":[{"id":"l1a","sc":[["F","Shall we go to the cinema tonight? The film starts at seven fifteen."],["M","Seven fifteen? I don't finish work until seven. Is there a later one?"],["F","Yes, there's one at eight forty-five."],["M","OK, let's go to that one."]],"q":"What time will they see the film?","o":["7.15","7.00","8.45"],"a":2,"tg":["spelling","detail"],"e":"7.15 erken; 8.45'teki seansa gidecekler."},{"id":"l1b","sc":[["F","I need a present for my brother. He likes music, but he's got lots of CDs."],["M","What about a T-shirt?"],["F","He's got lots of those too. Oh, I know – headphones! His old ones are broken."]],"q":"What will the girl buy for her brother?","o":["headphones","some new CDs","a T-shirt"],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"CD ve tişörtü çok var; kulaklık alacak."},{"id":"l1c","sc":[["M","Sorry I'm late. My car wouldn't start this morning."],["F","Oh no. Did you take the bus?"],["M","No, it was too crowded, so I walked. It took forty minutes!"]],"q":"How did the man get to work today?","o":["on foot","in his car","by bus"],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Araba çalışmadı, otobüs kalabalıktı; yürüdü."},{"id":"l1d","sc":[["M","Mum, have you seen my phone? It's not in my bag."],["F","Did you look on the kitchen table?"],["M","Yes, it's not there."],["F","Oh, here it is. It's on the sofa, under your jacket."]],"q":"Where is the boy's phone?","o":["in his bag","on the sofa","on the kitchen table"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Telefon kanepede, ceketin altında."},{"id":"l1e","sc":[["F","It's raining again today. What about tomorrow?"],["M","The forecast says it'll be cloudy in the morning, but sunny in the afternoon."],["F","Good, we can play tennis after lunch then."]],"q":"What will the weather be like tomorrow afternoon?","o":["rainy","sunny","cloudy"],"a":1,"tg":["detail","will-going-to"],"e":"Sabah bulutlu, öğleden sonra güneşli."}]},{"k":"L2","nm":"Dinleme 2 · Not tamamlama","ti":"Listening Part 2","ins":"Bir kayıt dinleyeceksin. Her boşluğa bir kelime, sayı ya da tarih yaz.","ty":"open","id":"l2","title":"Notlar","sc":[["F","Hello, this is Riverside Sports Centre. Thank you for asking about our children's swimming lessons. The lessons are on Wednesdays. We used to have them on Mondays, but not any more. They start at half past four and finish at a quarter past five. The teacher is Mrs Barker – that's B, A, R, K, E, R. She's very friendly and she has taught swimming for twenty years. Ten lessons cost forty-five pounds. Please remember that children must bring a towel and a swimming hat. Thank you for calling."]],"p":"Riverside Sports Centre – children's swimming lessons\nDay: (1) ______\nTime: 4.30 to (2) ______ p.m.\nTeacher's name: Mrs (3) ______\nPrice for 10 lessons: £(4) ______\nBring: a towel and a swimming (5) ______","a":[["Wednesday","Wednesdays"],["5.15","5:15","quarter past five","a quarter past five"],["Barker"],["45","forty-five","forty five"],["hat"]],"tg":[["detail"],["spelling"],["spelling"],["spelling"],["detail"]],"e":["Dersler çarşamba; pazartesi eskiden yapılıyordu.","\"finish at a quarter past five\" = 5.15.","Harf harf söylendi: B-A-R-K-E-R.","\"Ten lessons cost forty-five pounds\".","Havlu ve yüzme bonesi (swimming hat)."]},{"k":"L3","nm":"Dinleme 3 · Uzun konuşma","ti":"Listening Part 3","ins":"İki kişinin konuşmasını dinle ve soruları cevapla.","ty":"mc","id":"l3","sc":[["F","Tom, it's Dad's fiftieth birthday next Saturday. Shall we plan a surprise party for him?"],["M","Great idea, Kate. Where shall we have it? At home?"],["F","Our house is too small for everyone. What about the Italian restaurant in town? Dad loves it."],["M","Hmm, it's very expensive. Grandma's garden is big, and it's free."],["F","You're right. Let's ask Grandma. How many people shall we invite?"],["M","Well, there's the family – that's about fifteen people – and some of Dad's friends from work. Maybe thirty altogether."],["F","OK. And what about food? I can make a cake."],["M","You always make chocolate cake. Dad prefers lemon."],["F","Fine, I'll make a lemon cake. You can buy the drinks."],["M","OK. And Mum can bring some sandwiches. What present shall we get him?"],["F","He wants a new watch, but they cost too much. How about a book about fishing? He's just started going fishing."],["M","Perfect. I'll buy it tomorrow."]],"q":[{"q":"Where will the party be?","o":["at their house","at the Italian restaurant in town","in their grandmother's garden"],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Ev küçük, restoran pahalı; büyükannenin bahçesi."},{"q":"How many people will come to the party?","o":["about fifteen","about thirty","about fifty"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Aile yaklaşık 15 kişi, iş arkadaşlarıyla toplam 30. Elli, babanın yaşı."},{"q":"What kind of cake will Kate make?","o":["lemon","chocolate","strawberry"],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Babaları limonlu pastayı tercih ediyor."},{"q":"What will Tom bring to the party?","o":["sandwiches","the drinks","the cake"],"a":1,"tg":["detail","will-going-to"],"e":"\"You can buy the drinks\" – içecekleri Tom alacak. Sandviçleri anne getirecek."},{"q":"What present will they buy?","o":["a book about fishing","an expensive new watch","a fishing rod"],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Saat çok pahalı; balıkçılık kitabı alacaklar."}]},{"k":"L4","nm":"Dinleme 4 · Kısa kayıtlar","ti":"Listening Part 4","ins":"Beş kısa kayıt dinleyeceksin. Her soru için doğru cevabı seç.","ty":"mc","q":[{"id":"l4a","sc":[["F","Hello, this is Anna Cole. I bought a lamp from your shop on Saturday, but when I got home, I found it didn't work. Can I bring it back and change it for a new one?"]],"q":"Why is the woman calling?","o":["to buy a lamp","to change something she bought","to ask when the shop opens on Saturday"],"a":1,"tg":["purpose"],"e":"Aldığı lamba çalışmıyor; yenisiyle değiştirmek istiyor."},{"id":"l4b","sc":[["M","Everyone said the trip to the castle would be boring, but I thought it was brilliant. The guide told us lots of funny stories, and we dressed up in old clothes."]],"q":"How did the boy feel about the school trip?","o":["He thought it was boring.","He enjoyed it a lot.","He thought it was too long."],"a":1,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"\"I thought it was brilliant\" – çok beğendi. Sıkıcı diyenler başkaları."},{"id":"l4c","sc":[["F","OK, everyone, before you go home, please remember that the history homework is due on Thursday, not Friday. And don't forget to bring your sports clothes tomorrow."]],"q":"What is the teacher mainly talking about?","o":["when to give in homework","a history test on Friday","a new sports club"],"a":0,"tg":["gist"],"e":"Tarih ödevinin perşembe teslim edileceğini hatırlatıyor."},{"id":"l4d","sc":[["M","My new flat isn't very big, and the kitchen is quite old. But from the living room window, I can see the whole city and the river. That's the best thing about it."]],"q":"What does the man like most about his new flat?","o":["the size","the kitchen","the view"],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"That's the best thing about it\" = manzara."},{"id":"l4e","sc":[["M","Can you see the screen OK?"],["F","Yes, but the people in front are talking. And this popcorn is too salty!"],["M","Shh, it's starting."]],"q":"Where are the speakers?","o":["at a cinema","in a classroom","at a restaurant"],"a":0,"tg":["inference"],"e":"Perde (screen), patlamış mısır, \"it's starting\": sinema."}]},{"k":"L5","nm":"Dinleme 5 · Eşleştirme","ti":"Listening Part 5","ins":"Bir konuşma dinleyeceksin. Her kişiyi doğru seçenekle (A–H) eşleştir. Üç seçenek fazladır.","ty":"match","id":"l5","sc":[["F","So, Ben, what are your friends doing in the holidays?"],["M","Well, Sam's going to work in his uncle's shop. He wants to save money for a new bike."],["F","Good for him. What about Lily? Is she going camping again?"],["M","No, not this year. She's going to stay with her grandparents in Scotland."],["F","That's nice. And Josh?"],["M","Josh is doing a cooking course. He wants to be a chef one day."],["F","Really? And Emma? She loves the beach, doesn't she?"],["M","Yes, but she's broken her arm, so she can't swim. She's going to paint her bedroom instead. Her mum said she can choose the colours!"],["F","With one arm? And Ryan?"],["M","Ryan's having tennis lessons every morning. He's really good already."]],"opts":[["A","going camping"],["B","doing a cooking course"],["C","visiting family"],["D","working in a shop"],["E","learning to swim"],["F","painting"],["G","playing tennis"],["H","going to the beach"]],"q":[{"q":"Sam","a":"D","tg":["detail"],"e":"Amcasının dükkânında çalışacak."},{"q":"Lily","a":"C","tg":["detail"],"e":"Bu yıl kamp yok; İskoçya'da büyükanne ve büyükbabasında kalacak."},{"q":"Josh","a":"B","tg":["detail"],"e":"Aşçılık kursuna gidiyor."},{"q":"Emma","a":"F","tg":["detail","inference"],"e":"Kolu kırık, yüzemiyor; odasını boyayacak."},{"q":"Ryan","a":"G","tg":["detail"],"e":"Her sabah tenis dersi alıyor."}]}]},"W":{"min":20,"tasks":[{"id":"a2x-w1","part":6,"t":"Part 6 · E-posta: Parka gitmek","in":"You want to go to the park with your English friend Alex on Saturday. Write an email to Alex. In your email:\n• suggest going to the park\n• say what time to meet\n• say what you can do there.\nWrite 25 words or more.","mail":"To: Alex\nSubject: Saturday","pts":[["Parka gitmeyi öner",["park","shall we","let's","would you like","do you want"]],["Buluşma saati söyle",["o'clock","at 1","at 2","at 3","at 4","at 9","at 10","at 11","half past","meet at","a.m","p.m"]],["Orada ne yapabileceğinizi söyle",["play","football","picnic","ride","walk","frisbee","swim","tennis","basketball"]]],"min":25,"max":60,"model":"Hi Alex,\nWould you like to go to the park with me on Saturday? Let's meet at the park gate at 11 o'clock. We can play football and then have a picnic by the lake.\nSee you,\nEmre","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı fikirlerle","Hi Alex,\nThe weather is going to be sunny on Saturday, so shall we go to Central Park? We can meet at the café near the big gate at 2 o'clock. I'll bring my bike, so we can ride around the lake. After that we can have an ice cream.\nBye,\nZeynep"],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (A2 üstü)","Hi Alex,\nAre you free on Saturday? I've got a great idea – let's go to the park by the river! I think we should meet at half past ten at the bus stop opposite my school, because it's easy for both of us. There's a new skate park there, so we can try it, and if we get hungry, we can have a picnic under the trees.\nSee you,\nBerk"]]},{"id":"a2x-w2","part":7,"kind":"story","t":"Part 7 · Resimli hikâye: Kırık cam","in":"Look at the three pictures. Write the story shown in the pictures.\nWrite 35 words or more.","mail":"Picture 1: 👦⚽🌳  (Bir çocuk parkta futbol oynuyor.)\nPicture 2: ⚽💥🪟  (Top bir evin camına çarpıp kırıyor.)\nPicture 3: 👦🙇👵🍪  (Çocuk özür diliyor; yaşlı kadın gülümseyip ona kurabiye veriyor.)","pts":[["Çocuğun futbol oynadığını anlat",["football","ball","played","playing","kicked"]],["Camın kırıldığını anlat",["window","broke","broken","smashed"]],["Özrü ve sonucu anlat",["sorry","apologised","cookie","biscuit","smiled","gave"]],["Geçmiş zaman kullan",["was","went","played","kicked","broke","said","gave"]]],"min":35,"max":80,"model":"Last Saturday, Leo was playing football in the park near his house. He kicked the ball very hard and it flew over the fence. It broke the window of an old woman's house! Leo felt terrible, so he knocked on her door and said sorry. The woman smiled and gave him a cookie.","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı fikirlerle","On Sunday afternoon, Tom was playing football with his friends in the park. He kicked the ball very hard, but it went over the trees and hit a window. The glass broke! Tom was very scared. He went to the house and said sorry to the old woman. She wasn't angry. She smiled and gave him a cookie."],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (A2 üstü)","Last summer, Emir was practising football in the park next to his grandmother's street. He wanted to score a great goal, so he kicked the ball as hard as he could. Unfortunately, it flew into a garden and smashed a window! Emir felt terrible. He knocked on the door and apologised to the old lady who lived there. To his surprise, she laughed and said, \"Don't worry!\" Then she gave him a cookie and a glass of lemonade."]]}]},"S":{"parts":[{"id":"a2x-s1","p":1,"t":"Part 1 · Tanışma","ins":"Sınav görevlisi seninle ilgili sorular soruyor. Kısa ama tam cümlelerle cevap ver; son sorularda biraz daha uzun konuş.","q":[["What's your name? How old are you?",15,["my name is","I'm","years old"],["My name is Elif and I'm fifteen years old. My birthday is in May.","Hello, my name's Burak. I'm sixteen, and I'm in the tenth grade at school."]],["Where do you live?",15,["I live in","it's"],["I live in Bursa. It's a big city in the north-west of Turkey, near the mountains.","I live in a small town near Izmir. It's quiet, but it's close to the sea."]],["Tell me about your family.",30,["there are","I have","I've got","my mother","my father","brother","sister"],["There are four people in my family: my mother, my father, my little brother and me. My mother is a nurse and she works in a big hospital. My father works in a bank. My brother is eight and he loves football. We also have a cat called Pamuk.","I have a big family. I live with my parents, my two sisters and my grandmother. My older sister is at university in Ankara, so she only comes home in the holidays. My younger sister is ten. My grandmother cooks for us every day, and her food is delicious."]],["What do you like doing at the weekend?",30,["I like","I usually","with my friends","because"],["At the weekend I usually meet my friends. We go to the shopping centre or watch films at the cinema. I also like playing basketball in the park, because it's fun and good for my health. On Sunday evenings I do my homework.","I like sleeping late on Saturday mornings! After that, I often help my dad in the garden. In the afternoon I play computer games or read comics. On Sundays we sometimes visit my grandparents in their village."]],["Please tell me about your favourite place in your town.",30,["my favourite","because","there is","you can"],["My favourite place is the big park near my house. There is a lake with ducks and lots of trees. I go there with my dog every evening, because it's quiet and beautiful. In summer there are concerts there too.","My favourite place in my town is a small café near the sea. They make the best hot chocolate! I go there with my friends after school, and we sit by the window and watch the boats."]]]},{"id":"a2x-s2","p":2,"t":"Part 2 · Resimlerle konuşma","ins":"Gerçek sınavda bu bölüm bir partnerle yapılır. Burada resimlerdeki etkinlikler hakkında kendi görüşünü söyle.","opts":["🏊 swimming","🎮 video games","📚 reading","🚲 cycling","🎸 playing music"],"q":[["Here are some pictures of different things to do in your free time. Do you like these different things? Say why or why not.",60,["I like","I don't like","because","I think","it's"],["Let me look at the pictures. I really like swimming because it's good exercise and I feel relaxed after it. I go to the pool every Saturday. Video games are fun, but I don't play them very often because my eyes get tired. I don't like reading very much – it's a bit boring for me. Cycling is great! I go cycling with my dad on Sundays. Playing music is interesting, but I can't play any instruments. I'd like to learn the guitar one day.","I don't like swimming very much, because the water is often cold. But I love video games – I play with my friends online every evening. Reading is OK. I like comics and short stories. I think cycling is dangerous in my city because there are too many cars. Playing music is my favourite! I play the piano, and I practise every day for thirty minutes."]],["Which of these things do you like best? Why?",30,["I like ... best","my favourite","because"],["I like cycling best, because I can do it outside with my friends and see lots of different places. It's also cheap and good for the environment. Last summer we rode to a beautiful lake near my town and had a picnic there.","I like playing music best. When I play the piano, I feel happy and calm, and I can forget about school. My dream is to play in a concert one day."]],["Do you prefer doing things alone or with friends? Why?",30,["I prefer","because","with my friends","alone"],["I prefer doing things with my friends, because it's more fun and we can talk and laugh together. Alone, I sometimes get bored. For example, going to the cinema is much better with friends, because we can talk about the film afterwards.","I prefer doing things alone, because I can decide everything myself. For example, I like reading in my room in the evening. With friends, we always have to agree about what to do, and that can be difficult."]],["Do you think it's important to do sport? Why?",30,["I think","yes","because","healthy"],["Yes, I think it's very important, because sport keeps you healthy and you feel happier. It's also a good way to make friends. I play basketball at school, and my best friends are in my team.","Yes, I think so. Many young people sit at their computers all day. Doing sport helps your body and your mind, and you sleep better. I go swimming twice a week, and I always feel great afterwards."]]]}]}},"lv":"A2","name":"A2 Key"},{"id":"b1-1","t":"Sınav 1","sec":{"R":{"min":45,"parts":[{"k":"R1","nm":"Okuma 1 · Kısa metinler","ti":"Reading Part 1","ins":"Her kısa metni oku ve metnin ne söylediğini en iyi anlatan seçeneği bul.","ty":"mc","q":[{"x":"NOTICE\nSwimming pool closed for cleaning on Monday morning. Lessons will start at 2 p.m. instead of 10 a.m.","q":"What does the notice say?","o":["Swimming lessons have been cancelled for the whole of Monday.","Monday's swimming lessons will take place later than usual.","The pool will be cleaned every Monday morning."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Dersler iptal edilmiyor, sabah 10 yerine öğleden sonra 2'de başlıyor: yani her zamankinden geç."},{"x":"Hi Tom,\nI've left the keys with Mrs Hill next door because I'll be back late tonight. Please don't forget to feed the cat!\nAnna","q":"Why has Anna written this message?","o":["to tell Tom where he can find the keys","to ask Tom to collect the cat from Mrs Hill","to explain why she forgot the keys"],"a":0,"tg":["purpose","present-perfect"],"e":"\"I've left the keys with Mrs Hill\" anahtarların nerede olduğunu söylüyor. Kedi Mrs Hill'de değil; Tom'dan kediyi beslemesi isteniyor."},{"x":"SALE\nAll winter coats half price this week only. Cards accepted, but not for items under £5.","q":"What does this sign say?","o":["You can only pay in cash for sale items this week.","Winter coats are cheaper for a short time.","Coats under £5 are not in the sale."],"a":1,"tg":["detail","vocab"],"e":"\"half price this week only\" = kısa bir süre için yarı fiyat. Kart yalnızca 5 sterlinin altındaki ürünlerde geçmiyor."},{"x":"To: All students\nThe trip to the science museum has been moved from Friday to next Tuesday because the bus company had a problem. Please bring your forms by Monday.","q":"What should students do?","o":["give in their forms before the trip","contact the bus company about the problem","go to the museum on Monday"],"a":0,"tg":["detail","passive-advanced"],"e":"Gezi salıya alındı; formlar pazartesiye kadar, yani geziden önce teslim edilmeli."},{"x":"Lucy, Ben called. He can't meet you at the cinema at 7, but he'll be there by 7.30 if you can wait. Text him to let him know. Mum","q":"What does Ben want Lucy to do?","o":["change the cinema","tell him if she can wait","meet him outside the cinema at 7 o'clock"],"a":1,"tg":["inference","reported-speech"],"e":"\"Text him to let him know\" = bekleyip bekleyemeyeceğini ona haber vermesi isteniyor."}]},{"k":"R2","nm":"Okuma 2 · Eşleştirme","ti":"Reading Part 2","ins":"Aşağıdaki beş kişi bir yaz kursu arıyor. Her kişi için en uygun kursu (A–H) seç.","ty":"match","pl":"Metinler (A–H)","opts":[["A","Weekend Kitchen","Two-day course on Saturday and Sunday. Learn to bake bread and cakes. Everything you make goes home with you in a box."],["B","Sea Kayaking","A five-day course for teenagers who have never kayaked. All equipment is provided. Daily trips along the coast."],["C","Spanish Conversation","Evening classes, Monday to Thursday, maximum six students per group. Speaking only – no grammar homework!"],["D","City Walks","Discover the old town with an expert guide. Each day we visit a castle, a museum or an old market."],["E","Photo Club","For people who already know their camera well. Weekly competitions and discussions about famous photographers."],["F","Camera Basics","Learn every button on your camera in four lessons. The best photos from the group will appear in the library exhibition."],["G","Spanish Grammar","Morning classes for groups of up to twenty. Lots of written exercises to prepare for exams."],["H","Climbing Day","A one-day climbing taster for adults with some experience. Lunch included."]],"q":[{"q":"Maria: Maria wants to improve her cooking. She works during the week, so she can only go at weekends. She would like to take home what she makes.","a":"A","tg":["detail"],"e":"Hafta sonu, yaptığını eve götürme: A."},{"q":"Daniel: Daniel is 16 and loves being outdoors. He wants to try a sport he has never done before, and he would prefer a course that lasts several days.","a":"B","tg":["detail"],"e":"Daha önce yapmadığı bir spor, birkaç gün, gençler: B (H tek gün ve deneyimli yetişkinler için)."},{"q":"Sophie: Sophie is a beginner photographer. She wants to learn to use her new camera and she hopes to have her photos shown somewhere at the end.","a":"F","tg":["detail"],"e":"Yeni başlayan, fotoğraflar sergilenecek: F (E kamerasını iyi bilenler için)."},{"q":"Ahmed: Ahmed wants to practise speaking another language before a trip to Spain. He prefers small groups and evening classes.","a":"C","tg":["detail"],"e":"Konuşma, küçük grup, akşam: C (G sabah ve kalabalık)."},{"q":"Chen: Chen is interested in history. He would like a course with visits to real places rather than just lessons in a classroom.","a":"D","tg":["detail"],"e":"Gerçek yerlere ziyaret: D."}]},{"k":"R3","nm":"Okuma 3 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading Part 3","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","ty":"mc","title":"My first job","p":["When I was seventeen, I got a summer job at a small hotel by the sea. I had never worked before, and to be honest, I was more interested in the beach than in the money. My parents thought it would be good for me to learn what work was really like.","On my first day, the manager, Mrs Grant, gave me a uniform that was far too big and asked me to clean twenty rooms before lunch. I thought it was impossible. By twelve o'clock I had finished only eight, and I was sure she would be angry. Instead, she sat down with me and showed me a better way to organise my work. \"Everyone is slow at the beginning,\" she said.","After a few weeks, I was much faster, and I started to enjoy talking to the guests. Some of them came back every year and told me stories about the hotel in the past. I also made friends with the other young workers, and we often went swimming together after work.","Looking back, I realise that job taught me more than any school lesson. I learned to be patient, to ask for help when I needed it, and to be proud of doing something well. I still use those lessons today in my job as a teacher."],"q":[{"q":"Why did the writer take the job?","o":["He needed money for university.","His parents encouraged him to work.","He wanted to live near the beach.","A friend already worked at the hotel."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"My parents thought it would be good for me\" – ailesi teşvik etti."},{"q":"How did the writer feel at twelve o'clock on his first day?","o":["pleased that he had finished so many rooms","angry with the manager","worried about the manager's reaction","bored with the work"],"a":2,"tg":["inference","past-perfect"],"e":"\"I was sure she would be angry\" – yöneticinin tepkisinden endişe ediyordu."},{"q":"What did Mrs Grant do?","o":["She gave him an easier job.","She explained how to work more efficiently.","She told him to finish the rooms after lunch.","She gave him a smaller uniform."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"showed me a better way to organise my work\" = daha verimli çalışmayı gösterdi."},{"q":"What did the writer enjoy most after a few weeks?","o":["swimming alone","the guests' stories","cleaning the rooms quickly","working extra hours"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Misafirlerle konuşmayı ve onların otelin geçmişiyle ilgili hikâyelerini sevdi."},{"q":"What is the writer's main point in the text?","o":["Hotel work is too difficult for young people.","Teachers should have experience of working in other jobs first.","His first job taught him important lessons for life.","Managers should be kind to new workers."],"a":2,"tg":["gist"],"e":"Son paragraf: o iş ona okul derslerinden daha çok şey öğretti."}]},{"k":"R4","nm":"Okuma 4 · Cümle yerleştirme","ti":"Reading Part 4","ins":"Metinden beş cümle çıkarıldı. Her boşluk için uygun cümleyi (A–H) seç. Üç cümle fazladır.","ty":"gap","title":"The town that gave up cars","p":"Ten years ago, the small town of Lindhaven decided to close its centre to cars. Many shop owners were against the idea. (1) ______ They were worried that customers would stop coming.\nIn the first few months, things were difficult. Some shops did lose business. (2) ______ People began to walk and cycle into town, and they stayed longer because the streets were quieter and cleaner.\nThe town also built a large car park just outside the centre. (3) ______ The bus is free, so people do not mind leaving their cars there.\nToday, Lindhaven has more shops than before. (4) ______ Visitors from other countries come to see how the town changed.\nNot everyone is happy, of course. Older people sometimes find it hard to carry heavy shopping a long way. (5) ______ It seems that most problems can be solved if people are willing to talk.","opts":[["A","But after a year, the situation started to change."],["B","From there, a small bus takes visitors to the main square every ten minutes."],["C","They believed that people would not want to walk."],["D","The town plans to open the centre to cars again next year."],["E","To help them, the town now offers a free delivery service."],["F","Many of them are cafés with tables outside in the street."],["G","The bus stopped running because nobody used it."],["H","Cars are much cheaper to use in the countryside."]],"a":["C","A","B","F","E"],"tg":[["coherence"],["coherence"],["reference"],["coherence"],["reference"]],"e":["\"They were worried...\" öncesinde dükkân sahiplerinin düşüncesi gelmeli: C.","\"did lose business\" sonrası \"But after a year\" ile değişim başlıyor: A.","\"From there\" = otoparktan; sonraki cümlede \"The bus is free\": B.","Daha çok dükkân → \"Many of them are cafés\": F.","Yaşlıların zorluğuna çözüm: \"To help them\": E."]},{"k":"R5","nm":"Okuma 5 · Çoktan seçmeli boşluk","ti":"Reading Part 5","ins":"Metni oku ve her boşluk için doğru kelimeyi seç.","ty":"cloze","title":"A weekend in the mountains","p":"Last weekend my family and I (1) ______ on a trip to the mountains. We had booked a small cabin (2) ______ was next to a lake. On Saturday we got up early and (3) ______ a long walk through the forest. It was quite (4) ______, but the views from the top were amazing. In the evening it started to rain, so we stayed inside and played cards. We enjoyed it so much (5) ______ we have already decided to go back next year. I would (6) ______ it to anyone who loves nature.","o":[[["went","made","did","took"],0,["vocab"]],[["who","which","where","what"],1,["relative-clauses"]],[["went","made","took","did"],2,["vocab"]],[["tiring","tired","tire","tiredness"],0,["vocab"]],[["because","that","so","than"],1,["linkers-contrast-purpose"]],[["advise","recommend","tell","say"],1,["reporting-verbs"]]],"e":["go on a trip: geziye çıkmak.","Şey (cabin) için which; arkasından fiil geliyor.","take a walk: yürüyüşe çıkmak.","Yürüyüş yorucuydu: tiring (yorucu). tired = yorgun (kişi).","so ... that: o kadar ... ki.","recommend something to someone: birine bir şeyi tavsiye etmek. advise ve tell bu kalıpla kullanılmaz."]},{"k":"R6","nm":"Okuma 6 · Açık boşluk","ti":"Reading Part 6","ins":"Metni oku ve her boşluğa TEK kelime yaz.","ty":"open","title":"Hi Jess!","p":"Hi Jess!\nI'm writing (1) ______ tell you about my new school. I started here two weeks ago and I really like it. The teachers are very friendly and there are lots of clubs. I've already joined the drama club, (2) ______ meets every Wednesday. My best friend here is called Mia. She lives (3) ______ the same street as me, so we walk to school together. The only problem is that the school is (4) ______ bigger than my old one, and I sometimes get lost! Have you ever (5) ______ to a new school? Write back soon and tell me (6) ______ you are doing.\nLove, Ella","a":[["to"],["which"],["on","in"],["much","far","a lot"],["been","gone","moved","changed"],["how","what"]],"tg":[["gerund-infinitive"],["relative-clauses"],["prepositions-in-on-at"],["comparatives-superlatives"],["present-perfect"],["reported-speech"]],"e":["Amaç: to + fiil (I'm writing to tell you).","Virgüllü ek bilgi, şey (club): which.","Sokak adıyla on (İngiltere'de in de kullanılır).","Karşılaştırmayı güçlendirme: much / far / a lot bigger.","Have you ever been to ...? deneyim sorusu (moved / changed da anlamca kabul edilir).","Dolaylı soru: tell me how you are doing (what you are doing da olur)."]}]},"L":{"min":30,"parts":[{"k":"L1","nm":"Dinleme 1 · Kısa kayıtlar","ti":"Listening Part 1","ins":"Yedi kısa kayıt dinleyeceksin. Her soru için doğru cevabı seç.","ty":"mc","q":[{"id":"l1a","sc":[["M","Excuse me, how much is a return ticket to Oxford?"],["F","It's twelve pounds fifty. But if you travel after half past nine, it's only nine pounds twenty."],["M","Oh, I'm leaving at ten, so that's fine."]],"q":"How much will the man pay?","o":["£12.50","£9.20","£9.50"],"a":1,"tg":["detail","spelling"],"e":"Saat 10'da gidiyor; 9.30'dan sonra bilet 9,20 sterlin."},{"id":"l1b","sc":[["F","Did you buy the present for Grandma?"],["M","Yes. I was going to get her a scarf, but she has so many. So I got her a book about gardens instead."],["F","Good idea. She loves her garden."]],"q":"What did the boy buy for his grandmother?","o":["a scarf","a book","some flowers"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"I was going to get her a scarf ... I got her a book\" – sonunda kitap aldı."},{"id":"l1c","sc":[["M","Hi, it's Sam. Sorry, I'll be late for the meeting. My train has been delayed. I think I'll be there by quarter to eleven, not half past ten as we planned."]],"q":"What time will Sam arrive?","o":["10.15","10.30","10.45"],"a":2,"tg":["detail","passive-advanced"],"e":"quarter to eleven = 10.45. 10.30 planlanan saatti."},{"id":"l1d","sc":[["F","What was the weather like on your holiday?"],["M","Well, the first two days were sunny, but then it rained for the rest of the week. We spent most of the time in museums."]],"q":"What was the weather like for most of the holiday?","o":["sunny","rainy","windy"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"İlk iki gün güneşli, haftanın geri kalanı yağmurlu."},{"id":"l1e","sc":[["M","Where shall we meet tomorrow? At the café?"],["F","It's closed on Sundays. Let's meet outside the station, and we can walk to the park from there."],["M","OK, see you there."]],"q":"Where will they meet?","o":["outside the café","at the station","in the park"],"a":1,"tg":["detail","will-going-to"],"e":"Kafe pazar kapalı; istasyonun önünde buluşacaklar."},{"id":"l1f","sc":[["F","Have you finished your project yet?"],["M","Almost. I've written the text and I've found the photos. I just need to print it. The printer at home isn't working, so I'll do it at the library tomorrow."]],"q":"What does the boy still need to do?","o":["finish writing the text","find some photos","print the project"],"a":2,"tg":["detail","present-perfect"],"e":"Metni yazmış, fotoğrafları bulmuş; yalnızca yazdırması kaldı."},{"id":"l1g","sc":[["M","I really want to learn a musical instrument. I tried the guitar last year, but it hurt my fingers. My sister plays the piano, but we haven't got space for one. I think I'll try the violin."]],"q":"Which instrument is the man going to learn?","o":["the guitar","the piano","the violin"],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Gitarı denedi, piyanoya yer yok; kemanı deneyecek."}]},{"k":"L2","nm":"Dinleme 2 · Kısa konuşmalar","ti":"Listening Part 2","ins":"Altı kısa konuşma dinleyeceksin. Her soru için doğru cevabı seç.","ty":"mc","q":[{"id":"l2a","sc":[["F","How was the new restaurant?"],["M","The food was lovely, but we waited almost an hour for it. And it was so noisy we could hardly talk."],["F","So you won't go again?"],["M","Probably not."]],"q":"What did the man think of the restaurant?","o":["The food was disappointing.","The service was too slow.","It was too expensive."],"a":1,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Yemek güzeldi ama neredeyse bir saat beklediler: servis yavaştı."},{"id":"l2b","sc":[["M","Are you enjoying your new job?"],["F","The work is interesting, and my colleagues are nice. It's just that it takes me an hour and a half to get there every morning."],["M","That's a long time."]],"q":"What is the woman's problem with her job?","o":["the journey to work","the people she works with","the type of work"],"a":0,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"İş ilginç, arkadaşlar iyi; sorun her sabah bir buçuk saatlik yol."},{"id":"l2c","sc":[["F","Did you see the film last night?"],["M","Yes. Everyone said it was brilliant, but I thought the story was hard to follow."],["F","Really? I loved it."]],"q":"How did the man feel about the film?","o":["He found it confusing.","He thought it was too long.","He agreed it was brilliant."],"a":0,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"\"the story was hard to follow\" = kafa karıştırıcı buldu."},{"id":"l2d","sc":[["M","I'm thinking of selling my bike."],["F","Why? You use it every day."],["M","I know, but I'm moving to the city centre next month, and I'll be able to walk everywhere."]],"q":"Why does the man want to sell his bike?","o":["It is too old and often breaks down.","He won't need it any more.","He wants a faster one."],"a":1,"tg":["inference","will-going-to"],"e":"Şehir merkezine taşınıp her yere yürüyebilecek: bisiklete ihtiyacı kalmayacak."},{"id":"l2e","sc":[["F","You look tired."],["M","I am. My neighbours had a party last night, and it didn't finish until three. If I had known, I would have stayed at my brother's."]],"q":"Why is the man tired?","o":["He worked late.","He stayed at his brother's.","His neighbours were noisy."],"a":2,"tg":["detail","third-conditional"],"e":"Komşuların partisi sabah üçe kadar sürdü. Kardeşinde kalmadı; \"kalırdım\" diyor (third conditional)."},{"id":"l2f","sc":[["M","Shall we go to the beach on Saturday?"],["F","I'd love to, but I promised to help my mum paint the kitchen. How about Sunday?"],["M","Sunday's fine."]],"q":"What will the woman do on Saturday?","o":["go to the beach","help her mother","paint her own room"],"a":1,"tg":["detail","reporting-verbs"],"e":"Annesine mutfağı boyamada yardım edeceğine söz verdi."}]},{"k":"L3","nm":"Dinleme 3 · Not tamamlama","ti":"Listening Part 3","ins":"Bir müze rehberini dinle. Her boşluğa bir kelime, sayı ya da tarih yaz.","ty":"open","id":"l3","sc":[["F","Good morning, everyone, and welcome to the City Museum. Before you start your visit, here is some useful information. The museum opens at nine thirty every day and closes at five, except on Thursdays, when we stay open until eight in the evening. On the ground floor you will find our main exhibition, which is about the history of the river. On the first floor there is a special exhibition about toys from the last hundred years. Children love it, and so do many adults! Our café is on the top floor. It has great views of the city, and today the special is vegetable soup. Please note that you can take photos everywhere except in the toy exhibition. If you lose anything, go to the information desk next to the main entrance. Finally, don't forget that our free guided tour starts at eleven o'clock from the shop. Enjoy your visit!"]],"title":"Notlar","p":"City Museum\nOpening time: (1) ______\nOn Thursdays open until: (2) ______ p.m.\nGround floor exhibition: the history of the (3) ______\nFirst floor exhibition: (4) ______ from the last 100 years\nToday's special in the café: vegetable (5) ______\nFree guided tour starts from the (6) ______","a":[["9.30","9:30","half past nine","nine thirty"],["8","eight","8.00"],["river"],["toys","toy"],["soup"],["shop"]],"tg":[["spelling"],["spelling"],["detail"],["detail"],["detail"],["detail"]],"e":["\"opens at nine thirty\"","\"until eight in the evening\"","\"the history of the river\"","\"toys from the last hundred years\"","\"today the special is vegetable soup\"","\"starts at eleven o'clock from the shop\""]},{"k":"L4","nm":"Dinleme 4 · Röportaj","ti":"Listening Part 4","ins":"Genç bir dağcıyla yapılan röportajı dinle ve soruları cevapla.","ty":"mc","id":"l4","sc":[["M","Today I'm talking to Emma Price, who, at just nineteen, has climbed some of the highest mountains in Europe. Emma, how did you start?"],["F","Well, my parents loved walking, so we spent every holiday in the hills. When I was twelve, my uncle took me to a climbing wall, and I was hooked. I didn't try a real mountain until I was fifteen."],["M","What's the hardest thing about climbing?"],["F","People think it's being afraid of heights, but actually I've never had that problem. For me, the hardest part is the cold. You have to keep going even when you can't feel your hands."],["M","Do you climb alone?"],["F","Never. It's much safer to climb with a team, and anyway, sharing the experience is half the fun. My climbing partner, Lisa, has been my best friend since school."],["M","How do you prepare for a big climb?"],["F","I go running every morning and I go to the gym three times a week. But I think planning is even more important than fitness. We study the weather and the route for weeks before we go."],["M","And what's next?"],["F","Next summer we're going to try a mountain in South America. I'm excited, but I'm also a bit nervous because it's much higher than anything we've done before."]],"q":[{"q":"When did Emma first climb a real mountain?","o":["when she was twelve","when she was fifteen","when she was nineteen"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"12 yaşında tırmanma duvarı; gerçek dağ 15 yaşında."},{"q":"What does Emma find most difficult about climbing?","o":["being high up","the low temperatures","carrying heavy equipment"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Yükseklik korkusu yok; en zor kısım soğuk."},{"q":"Why does Emma prefer to climb with other people?","o":["It is more enjoyable and safer.","Her parents won't let her climb alone.","She is not experienced enough."],"a":0,"tg":["inference"],"e":"\"much safer ... sharing the experience is half the fun\""},{"q":"Who is Lisa?","o":["Emma's sister","Emma's teacher","Emma's friend"],"a":2,"tg":["detail","present-perfect"],"e":"\"has been my best friend since school\""},{"q":"According to Emma, what is the most important part of preparation?","o":["running","going to the gym three times a week","planning the trip"],"a":2,"tg":["detail","comparatives-superlatives"],"e":"\"planning is even more important than fitness\""},{"q":"How does Emma feel about her next climb?","o":["only excited","both excited and worried","not sure if she will be able to go"],"a":1,"tg":["attitude","will-going-to"],"e":"\"I'm excited, but I'm also a bit nervous\""}]}]},"W":{"min":45,"tasks":[{"id":"b1x-w1","part":1,"t":"Part 1 · E-posta: İngilizce kulübü","in":"Read this email from your English teacher, Mr Clark, and the notes you have made. Write your email to Mr Clark using all the notes. Write about 100 words.","mail":"From: Mr Clark\nSubject: English club\n\nDear students,\nWe are starting an English club after school this term. [Great idea!]\nWe can meet on Tuesdays or Fridays. Which day is better for you? [Say which and why]\nWhat activities would you like to do in the club? [Suggest ...]\nCould you also help us to tell other students about the club? [Yes, I can ...]\nBest wishes,\nMr Clark","pts":[["Fikri beğendiğini söyle",["great","good idea","love","excited","fantastic","brilliant","sounds"]],["Gün seç ve nedenini söyle",["tuesday","friday"]],["Etkinlik öner",["film","game","play","song","quiz","drama","speak","talk","music","book","debate"]],["Diğer öğrencilere nasıl haber vereceğini söyle",["poster","tell","social media","message","website","class","friends","announce"]]],"min":80,"max":130,"model":"Dear Mr Clark,\nThank you for your email. I think an English club is a great idea, and I would love to join it.\nFriday would be better for me, because I have basketball training on Tuesdays after school. Also, on Fridays we don't have much homework, so everyone will be more relaxed.\nIn the club, we could watch short English films and talk about them. It would also be fun to play quiz games and sing songs together.\nOf course I can help. I could make some posters for the school corridors and send a message to my classmates.\nBest wishes,\nDeniz","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı fikirlerle","Dear Mr Clark,\nThanks for letting us know about the new English club – I think it's a fantastic idea and I'd definitely like to be part of it.\nTuesday would suit me better. On Fridays I usually visit my grandparents straight after school, so I wouldn't be able to come.\nAs for activities, I'd love to do some drama, for example acting out short scenes in English. We could also have a book club where we read easy novels and talk about them.\nI'd be happy to help. I could write a short article for the school website and talk to the students in my class about it.\nKind regards,\nEfe"],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (B2'ye yakın)","Dear Mr Clark,\nThank you for your email. I think an English club is a brilliant idea, and I'm sure lots of students will be interested.\nPersonally, Friday would work best for me. Not only do I have football practice on Tuesdays, but by Friday everyone is more relaxed and in the mood for something fun.\nIn terms of activities, it would be great to organise debates on topics that matter to teenagers, as they would help us practise speaking. Watching music videos and discussing the lyrics might also be popular.\nOf course I'll help spread the word. I could design a poster for the noticeboards and share it on our class group chat, which almost everyone reads.\nBest wishes,\nAslı"]]},{"choice":[{"id":"b1x-w2","part":2,"kind":"article","t":"Part 2 · Makale: Unutulmaz bir gün","in":"You see this announcement in an English-language magazine. Write your article in about 100 words.","mail":"ARTICLES WANTED!\nA day I will never forget\nTell us about a special day in your life. What happened? Who were you with? Why will you always remember it?\nThe best articles will be published next month.","pts":[["Ne olduğunu anlat",["was","were","went","had","happened","saw"]],["Kiminle olduğunu söyle",["with","friend","family","mother","father","sister","brother","cousin","grandmother","grandfather"]],["Neden unutamayacağını açıkla",["because","never forget","remember","special","reason","so"]],["Bir başlık kullan",["@title"]]],"min":80,"max":140,"model":"My first concert\nThe day I will never forget is the day I saw my favourite band live. It was my fifteenth birthday, and my older sister had bought tickets as a surprise.\nWe travelled to Istanbul by train and arrived at the stadium two hours early. When the band finally came on stage, everyone started singing and dancing. I knew every word of every song!\nI will always remember that day because it was the first time I had been to a concert, and because my sister had planned everything for me. It made me feel really special.","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı fikirlerle","The day I met my baby sister\nI will never forget the fourteenth of March, three years ago. That morning, my dad woke me up early and told me that my mum was in hospital. I was so excited that I couldn't eat my breakfast!\nMy dad and I drove to the hospital together, and when we arrived, my mum was holding a tiny baby wrapped in a pink blanket. It was my sister, Ela.\nI'll always remember that day because my life changed completely. I wasn't an only child any more, and now Ela is my best friend – even when she takes my things without asking!"],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (B2'ye yakın)","Lost in the mountains – and found\nThe day I will never forget began like any other school trip. Our class had gone hiking in the mountains, and my best friend and I stopped to take photos of a waterfall. By the time we looked up, the rest of the group had disappeared.\nAt first we laughed, but as the sky grew darker and our phones had no signal, we started to panic. Eventually, we decided to follow the river downhill, and after two long hours we finally saw the lights of a village.\nI will always remember that day, not because it was frightening, but because I discovered I could stay calm in a difficult situation – and because I have never been so happy to see my teacher!"]]},{"id":"b1x-w3","part":2,"kind":"story","t":"Part 2 · Hikâye: Yanlış otobüs","in":"Your English teacher has asked you to write a story. Your story must begin with this sentence. Write your story in about 100 words.","mail":"Your story must begin with this sentence:\nAfter ten minutes on the bus, Maya realised she was going the wrong way.","pts":[["Verilen cümleyle başla",["after ten minutes on the bus"]],["Geçmiş zamanla olayları anlat",["was","were","got","looked","asked","decided","saw"]],["Bir sorun ve çözüm olsun",["but","so","then","luckily","suddenly"]],["Hikâyeyi bitir (sonuç)",["in the end","finally","at last","happy","relieved","laughed","never"]]],"min":80,"max":140,"model":"After ten minutes on the bus, Maya realised she was going the wrong way. She was supposed to be at her friend's birthday party at six, and it was already half past five.\nShe got off at the next stop and looked around. She didn't know the area at all, and her phone battery was almost empty. Luckily, she saw an old man waiting at the bus stop and asked him for help.\n\"Take the number 12,\" he said. \"It goes straight to the city centre.\"\nIn the end, Maya arrived at the party only fifteen minutes late, and she had a funny story to tell everyone.","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı fikirlerle","After ten minutes on the bus, Maya realised she was going the wrong way. Instead of the city centre, the bus was heading out of town towards the airport.\nShe was on her way to a job interview at ten o'clock, and it was already twenty past nine. She felt her heart beating faster. Then she noticed that the woman sitting next to her was wearing a uniform from the same company.\n\"Excuse me,\" said Maya, \"are you going to Green Office too?\"\nThe woman smiled. \"Yes, our new office is near the airport now.\" It turned out that Maya had been looking at the old address. She arrived early and got the job."],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (B2'ye yakın)","After ten minutes on the bus, Maya realised she was going the wrong way. The view outside the window, which should have been full of shops and offices, had turned into fields and farms.\nShe was supposed to be meeting her cousin, who was arriving from Germany, at the train station in half an hour. Panicking, she pressed the bell and jumped off at the next stop, only to find that the next bus back would not come for forty minutes.\nJust as she was about to give up, a farmer driving a small truck stopped and offered her a lift. Although she was nervous at first, he turned out to be very friendly and even told her funny stories about his cows.\nIn the end, Maya arrived at the station just as her cousin was walking out, and the two of them laughed about her adventure all the way home."]]}]}]},"S":{"parts":[{"id":"b1x-s1","p":1,"t":"Part 1 · Tanışma","ins":"Sınav görevlisi seni tanımak için sorular soruyor. Her soruya 2–3 cümleyle cevap ver.","q":[["What's your name? Where do you live?",20,["my name is","I live in","I come from"],["My name is Deniz Yılmaz, and I live in Izmir, on the west coast of Turkey. I live with my parents in a flat near the sea. I've lived there all my life.","I'm Kaan. I come from Ankara, the capital, but last year my family moved to Antalya because of my father's job. I really like it here because of the weather."]],["Do you work or are you a student?",20,["I'm a student","I study","I work as"],["I'm a student. I'm in my last year at high school, and I'm preparing for the university exam. My favourite subject is biology, because I want to be a doctor.","I work in a bookshop in the city centre. I've been there for about a year. It isn't very well paid, but I love books, and I enjoy talking to the customers."]],["What do you usually do at the weekend?",30,["usually","often","at the weekend","I like"],["I usually meet my friends on Saturday, and we go to a café or play football in the park. On Sundays I often stay at home and relax with my family. Sometimes we have a big breakfast together, which can take two hours!","At the weekend, I like doing something active. In summer I go swimming or cycling along the coast, and in winter I go to the gym. On Saturday evenings I usually watch a film with my brother."]],["Tell us about something you did last week.",30,["last week","I went","it was"],["Last week I went to the cinema with my cousin. We watched a science fiction film about a group of astronauts. It was a bit long, but the special effects were amazing, and afterwards we had dinner at a new burger restaurant.","Last week was my best friend's birthday, so we organised a surprise party for her. We decorated her living room with balloons while she was at work. When she came home, she was so surprised that she started crying!"]],["What would you like to do in the future?",30,["I'd like to","I hope","I want to","because"],["I'd like to study medicine at university, because I want to help people. After that, I hope to work in a big hospital. I'd also love to travel to Canada one day, because my uncle lives there.","In the future, I'd like to open my own café. I love cooking and baking, and I think it would be great to have a place where people can relax. First, though, I need to save some money and learn about business."]]]},{"id":"b1x-s2","p":2,"t":"Part 2 · Fotoğrafı anlat","ins":"Bu bölümde bir fotoğrafı yaklaşık 1 dakika anlatırsın: kimler var, ne yapıyorlar, nerede, neler görüyorsun, nasıl hissediyor olabilirler.","img":[["👨‍🍳👩‍🍳🧑‍🍳🍕🥗🔥","Bir mutfakta üç genç birlikte yemek yapıyor: biri pizza hamuru açıyor, biri salata hazırlıyor, arkada fırın yanıyor. Herkes gülümsüyor.","b1-cooking"]],"q":[["Now, here is your photograph. It shows people cooking together. Please tell us what you can see in the photograph.",60,["in this photo I can see","on the left","on the right","in the background","they are","it looks like","they seem"],["In this photo I can see three young people who are cooking together in a kitchen. On the left, a boy is making a pizza – he's putting tomato sauce on the dough, and he's wearing a white apron. In the middle, a girl is preparing a salad. She's cutting some vegetables, like tomatoes and cucumbers. On the right, another girl is looking at the oven, which is in the background. It looks very hot. There are lots of vegetables and bowls on the table. They all seem very happy – they're smiling and laughing. I think they're friends, and maybe they're preparing dinner for a party or a special occasion. It looks like a modern kitchen, and it's quite bright, so it's probably daytime.","This photo shows a kitchen with three young people in it. They're making food together, and it looks like they're having a great time. The boy on the left is working with some pizza dough. He's got flour on his hands and on his face, which is quite funny! The girl next to him is making a salad in a big bowl. The third person is checking the oven at the back of the kitchen. Maybe they're students and they're learning to cook in a cooking class, because they're all wearing aprons. Or perhaps they're flatmates making lunch. I think they feel relaxed and happy, because cooking with friends is fun."]]]},{"id":"b1x-s3","p":3,"t":"Part 3 · Birlikte karar verme","ins":"Gerçek sınavda bu bölüm bir partnerle yapılır. Burada iki tarafı da sen düşün: önce seçenekleri tek tek değerlendir, sonra en iyisine karar ver.","opts":["🎨 sanat atölyesi","⚽ spor turnuvası","🎬 film gecesi","🌳 park temizliği","🎤 müzik yarışması"],"q":[["A school wants to organise an activity to help students make new friends. Here are some ideas they're thinking about. Talk about the different activities and say how they would help students make friends.",90,["what about","I think","because","it would be","on the other hand","that's a good idea","I'm not sure"],["Let's start with the art workshop. I think it would be good for quiet students, because they can be creative without speaking too much. But people mostly work alone, so they might not talk very much. A sports tournament is fun, and students have to work in teams, so they would get to know each other quickly. However, some people don't like sport, and they wouldn't take part. A film night is relaxing and cheap, but you can't talk during the film, so it's not great for making friends. Cleaning the park is a good idea because students work together and do something useful at the same time. They'd have lots of time to chat. A music competition could be exciting, but some students might feel nervous on stage, and only a few people would actually perform.","I'll start with the music competition. It would be exciting and lots of students would come to watch, but the people performing might not have time to meet others. The film night is a nice idea, especially if there's popcorn and time to talk afterwards. The art workshop could work well if students make something together, like a big painting for the school. The sports tournament is probably the most popular idea, because many teenagers love football or basketball, and being in a team is a great way to make friends. Finally, cleaning the park is useful for the environment, but I'm not sure students would want to spend their free time picking up rubbish."]],["Now decide which activity would be best for making new friends.",30,["I think the best","because","I'd choose","in my opinion"],["In my opinion, the best activity would be the park clean-up, because everyone can join, students work together in small groups, and they have plenty of time to talk to each other. They also feel proud because they're doing something good for their town.","I think the sports tournament would be best. Teams have to communicate and help each other, and when you win or lose together, you feel closer to your teammates. Students who don't play can still come and support their friends."]]]},{"id":"b1x-s4","p":4,"t":"Part 4 · Tartışma","ins":"Sınav görevlisi 3. bölümün konusuyla ilgili görüşünü soruyor. Her soruya fikrini ve nedenini söyle.","q":[["Is it easy or difficult to make new friends at a new school? Why?",40,["I think","because","for example","it depends"],["I think it can be quite difficult at first, because everyone already has their own group of friends, and new students often feel shy. But if you join a club or a sports team, it becomes much easier, because you meet people who like the same things as you. Teachers can also help by asking students to work in pairs or groups.","It depends on the person. If you're confident and friendly, it's quite easy, because people are usually curious about new students. But shy people may find it hard to start conversations. When I changed schools, a girl in my class invited me to sit with her at lunch, and that helped me a lot."]],["Do you prefer doing activities in a big group or in a small group?",40,["I prefer","because","on the other hand","in a small group"],["I prefer small groups, because everyone can speak and you get to know people better. In a big group it's more exciting, but quiet people often don't say anything, and it can be difficult to make decisions. For example, when we plan a trip with ten people, it takes hours to agree on anything!","I prefer big groups, because there's more energy and you meet more people. For activities like football or parties, you need lots of people. In a small group, if someone is in a bad mood, it affects everyone. In a big group, there's always someone you can talk to, and you often meet friends of friends."]],["Do you think young people spend too much time online instead of meeting friends?",40,["I agree","I don't think","however","for example","these days"],["I partly agree. These days many young people chat online for hours, and that isn't the same as meeting face to face. You can't really see how someone feels in a message. However, online chats can also help you stay in touch with friends who live far away, so I don't think the internet is always bad.","Yes, I think so. Many teenagers sit in the same room and look at their phones instead of talking to each other. I think they should spend more time outside, doing sport or meeting friends in the park. Parents should also set a good example, because they use their phones a lot too."]]]}]}},"lv":"B1","name":"B1 Preliminary"},{"id":"b2-1","t":"Sınav 1","sec":{"R":{"min":75,"parts":[{"k":"R1","nm":"Okuma 1 · Çoktan seçmeli boşluk","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 1","ins":"Metni oku ve her boşluk için en uygun kelimeyi (A, B, C ya da D) seç.","ty":"cloze","title":"The rise of urban gardening","p":"In many cities around the world, people are (1) ______ empty roofs and car parks into vegetable gardens. Urban gardening has become so popular that some cities now (2) ______ residents with free seeds and tools. Supporters argue that growing your own food not only saves money but also (3) ______ people closer to nature. It can also (4) ______ a real difference to local communities, as neighbours who had never spoken before often end up working side by side. However, city gardeners have to (5) ______ with problems that farmers in the countryside rarely face, such as polluted soil and a (6) ______ of space. Some projects have found creative solutions, growing plants in old boxes or even on the walls of buildings. Whether urban gardens can (7) ______ produce enough food to feed a city is still open to (8) ______, but few would deny that they make cities greener.","o":[[["putting","turning","becoming","getting"],1,["collocation"]],[["give","offer","provide","hand"],2,["collocation"]],[["brings","takes","leads","carries"],0,["collocation"]],[["do","have","take","make"],3,["collocation"]],[["cope","handle","manage","face"],0,["vocab"]],[["need","lack","loss","want"],1,["vocab"]],[["ever","once","yet","already"],0,["vocab"]],[["question","discussion","debate","argument"],2,["collocation"]]],"e":["turn something into something: bir şeyi başka bir şeye dönüştürmek. put, become ve get bu kalıba uymaz.","provide someone with something: birine bir şey sağlamak. give/offer/hand \"with\" almaz.","bring someone closer to: birini bir şeye yaklaştırmak.","make a difference: fark yaratmak.","cope with: başa çıkmak. handle/manage \"with\" almaz; face doğrudan nesne alır.","a lack of: eksikliği.","ever: hiç, bir gün (olasılığı vurgular).","open to debate: tartışmaya açık."]},{"k":"R2","nm":"Okuma 2 · Açık boşluk","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 2","ins":"Metni oku ve her boşluğa TEK kelime yaz.","ty":"open","one":true,"title":"The man who walked around the world","p":"In 1998, Karl Bushby set (1) ______ from the southern tip of South America with an ambitious plan: to walk all the way back to his home in England. Since (2) ______, he has crossed deserts, jungles and even a frozen sea. Unlike most travellers, Bushby made a rule that he would never use any form of transport (3) ______ than his own feet. This meant that when he reached the Bering Strait, he had (4) ______ walk across the ice to Russia. The journey has taken much longer (5) ______ he expected. At one point he was stuck for years, unable to get the visas he needed. Yet (6) ______ the many difficulties, he has refused to give up. When asked why, he simply says that he has come too far to stop now. Few people would have the patience to do (7) ______ he has done, and his story has inspired thousands (8) ______ people.","a":[["off","out"],["then"],["other"],["to"],["than"],["despite"],["what"],["of"]],"tg":[["vocab"],["present-perfect"],["comparatives-superlatives"],["must-have-to-should"],["comparatives-superlatives"],["linkers-contrast-purpose"],["relative-clauses"],["vocab"]],"e":["set off / set out: yola çıkmak.","Since then: o zamandan beri.","other than: -dan başka.","had to: zorunda kaldı.","longer than: -den daha uzun.","despite + isim: -e rağmen (although değil, arkasında cümle yok).","what = the thing(s) that.","thousands of people."]},{"k":"R3","nm":"Okuma 3 · Kelime türetme","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 3","ins":"Metni oku. Her boşluk için verilen kök kelimeyi uygun biçime getir (isim, sıfat, zarf, olumsuz vb.).","ty":"open","title":"Why we should all take a nap","st":["SCIENCE","EFFECT","SLEEP","IMPROVE","ADVISE","PRODUCE","LIKELY","RESPONSE"],"p":"For many years, sleeping during the day was seen as a sign of laziness. Now, however, (1) ______ research suggests that a short afternoon nap can have a surprisingly positive (2) ______ on our health. People who feel (3) ______ after lunch are not necessarily tired – it is simply the way our bodies work. Studies show that a twenty-minute nap can lead to a significant (4) ______ in memory and concentration. Experts' (5) ______ is to keep naps short, as longer ones can leave you feeling worse. Some companies have even introduced nap rooms, believing that rested employees are more (6) ______. Those who nap regularly are also less (7) ______ to make mistakes. Perhaps it is time we all took more (8) ______ for our own rest.","a":[["scientific"],["effect"],["sleepy"],["improvement"],["advice"],["productive"],["likely"],["responsibility"]],"tg":[["word-formation"],["word-formation"],["word-formation"],["word-formation"],["word-formation"],["word-formation"],["word-formation"],["word-formation"]],"e":["İsimden önce sıfat: scientific research.","have an effect on: üzerinde etkisi olmak (isim).","feel + sıfat: sleepy (uykulu).","a significant + isim: improvement (gelişme).","Experts' + isim: advice (tavsiye; sayılamaz).","more + sıfat: productive (verimli).","be likely to: -mesi muhtemel olmak. \"less likely\" anlamı zaten olumsuzlaştırıyor; unlikely olmaz.","take responsibility for: sorumluluk almak."]},{"k":"R4","nm":"Okuma 4 · Cümle dönüştürme","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 4","ins":"İkinci cümleyi, birinciyle aynı anlamı verecek şekilde tamamla. Verilen kelimeyi değiştirmeden kullan; 2–5 kelime yaz.","ty":"kwt","q":[{"s1":"\"I'm sorry I broke your phone,\" Tom said to his sister.","key":"APOLOGISED","s2":"Tom ______ his sister's phone.","a":["apologised for breaking","apologised for having broken"],"tg":["reporting-verbs"],"e":"apologise for + -ing."},{"s1":"It isn't necessary for you to bring any food.","key":"HAVE","s2":"You ______ any food.","a":["do not have to bring","don't have to bring"],"tg":["must-have-to-should"],"e":"don't have to: gerek yok."},{"s1":"I'd prefer not to go out tonight.","key":"RATHER","s2":"I ______ out tonight.","a":["would rather not go","'d rather not go"],"tg":["vocab"],"e":"would rather + yalın fiil; olumsuz: would rather not go."},{"s1":"They will announce the results tomorrow.","key":"BE","s2":"The results ______ tomorrow.","a":["will be announced"],"tg":["passive-voice"],"e":"Gelecek zaman edilgen: will be + V3."},{"s1":"I didn't study enough, so I failed the exam.","key":"HAD","s2":"If I ______ more, I wouldn't have failed the exam.","a":["had studied"],"tg":["third-conditional"],"e":"Third conditional: If + had + V3."},{"s1":"It's possible that Sarah forgot about the meeting.","key":"MIGHT","s2":"Sarah ______ about the meeting.","a":["might have forgotten"],"tg":["modals-deduction"],"e":"Geçmişe dair olasılık: might have + V3."}]},{"k":"R5","nm":"Okuma 5 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 5","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","ty":"mc","title":"Learning to fail","p":["When I was seventeen, I was convinced that I would become a professional tennis player. I trained every morning before school and every evening after it, and my coach told anyone who would listen that I had a real future in the sport. So when I lost in the first round of the national junior championships, I was devastated. I remember sitting in the changing room, unable to move, feeling that years of work had been wasted in a single afternoon.","My father, who had never been particularly interested in sport, found me there. I expected him to tell me that I would do better next time, which is what everyone else had said. Instead, he asked me what I had learned. I was annoyed at the question – it seemed to me that the only lesson was that I wasn't good enough. But he waited, and eventually I admitted that I had been so nervous that I had stopped playing my natural game.","That conversation changed the way I approached not just tennis, but everything. I started to see each defeat as information rather than a judgement. I kept a notebook in which I wrote down, after every match, one thing that had gone wrong and one thing I could do differently. Some of the entries look rather foolish to me now, but the habit of looking honestly at my mistakes has stayed with me.","I never did become a professional. A shoulder injury at nineteen put an end to that dream, and for a while I found it hard to imagine a life without competition. Today I work as a coach myself, and I often tell my students about that afternoon in the changing room. Most of them, I suspect, think I am simply trying to make them feel better about losing. A few, though, start keeping notebooks of their own."],"q":[{"q":"How did the writer feel after losing in the championships?","o":["angry with his coach","determined to train harder","shocked and hopeless","relieved that it was over"],"a":2,"tg":["inference"],"e":"\"devastated ... unable to move ... years of work had been wasted\": yıkılmış ve umutsuz."},{"q":"What surprised the writer about his father's reaction?","o":["He seemed more upset about the result than the writer was.","He did not try to comfort him in the usual way.","He criticised the way the writer had played.","He had watched the whole match."],"a":1,"tg":["inference"],"e":"Herkes \"bir dahakine daha iyi olur\" derken babası ne öğrendiğini sordu."},{"q":"Why was the writer annoyed by his father's question?","o":["He thought the answer was obvious.","He did not want to talk about tennis.","He felt his father did not understand the sport.","He wanted to be left alone."],"a":0,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"\"the only lesson was that I wasn't good enough\": cevabın belli olduğunu düşündü."},{"q":"What does the writer say about his notebook?","o":["He still writes in it today.","His coach asked him to keep it.","Some of what he wrote now seems silly.","It helped him win the next championship."],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"Some of the entries look rather foolish to me now\"."},{"q":"Why did the writer stop playing tennis seriously?","o":["He lost interest in competition.","He was injured.","He decided to become a coach.","His father disapproved."],"a":1,"tg":["detail","past-simple"],"e":"19 yaşında omuz sakatlığı."},{"q":"What does the writer suggest about his students in the last paragraph?","o":["Most of them are better players than he was.","They all find his story helpful.","Only some of them take his advice seriously.","They are afraid of losing."],"a":2,"tg":["inference"],"e":"Çoğu onu sadece teselli ediyor sanıyor; birkaçı defter tutmaya başlıyor."}]},{"k":"R6","nm":"Okuma 6 · Cümle yerleştirme","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 6","ins":"Metinden altı cümle çıkarıldı. Her boşluk için uygun cümleyi (A–G) seç. Bir cümle fazladır.","ty":"gap","title":"The secret life of trees","p":"For a long time, scientists believed that trees in a forest competed with each other for light, water and space. Recent research, however, paints a very different picture. (1) ______\nThe key to this network lies underground. Tree roots are connected by fungi which spread through the soil in thin threads. (2) ______ In return, the fungi receive sugars that the trees produce through photosynthesis.\nThis exchange is not just between a tree and its fungi. (3) ______ Researchers have found that older trees, sometimes called \"mother trees\", send food to young trees growing in their shade.\nEven more remarkably, trees appear to warn each other of danger. (4) ______ Nearby trees that receive these signals then start producing chemicals that make their leaves taste unpleasant to insects.\nThese discoveries have important consequences for the way we manage forests. (5) ______ Removing the largest trees, as many logging companies do, may damage the whole network.\nNot all scientists agree on how these findings should be interpreted. (6) ______ Nevertheless, the idea that a forest is a community rather than a collection of individuals is beginning to change the way we think about nature.","opts":[["A","When a tree is attacked by insects, it can send chemical signals through the fungal network."],["B","It suggests that trees actually cooperate, sharing resources through a hidden network."],["C","These threads carry water and nutrients from the soil to the trees."],["D","Some argue that words like \"communication\" make trees sound more human than they are."],["E","Through the same network, trees can pass nutrients to one another."],["F","If trees depend on each other, cutting down the oldest ones could harm the rest of the forest."],["G","Most forests in Europe were planted by humans in the last two hundred years."]],"a":["B","C","E","A","F","D"],"tg":[["coherence"],["reference"],["coherence"],["reference"],["coherence"],["coherence"]],"e":["\"very different picture\" → işbirliği fikri; sonraki paragraf \"this network\" diyor: B.","\"thin threads\" → \"These threads carry...\". \"In return\" ile mantar karşılık alıyor: C.","\"not just between a tree and its fungi\" → ağaçlar birbirine de besin veriyor: E.","\"warn each other of danger\" → \"these signals\": A.","\"consequences for the way we manage forests\" → en yaşlı ağaçları kesmek: F.","\"Not all scientists agree\" → bazıları \"iletişim\" kelimesine itiraz ediyor: D. G konuyla ilgisiz."]},{"k":"R7","nm":"Okuma 7 · Çoklu eşleştirme","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 7","ins":"Dört metni (A–D) oku. Her soru için doğru metni seç. Metinler birden fazla kez seçilebilir.","ty":"match","title":"Four people talk about learning a musical instrument as an adult","opts":[["A","Helen, 45 – cello","I had wanted to play the cello since I was a child, but my parents couldn't afford lessons. When my own children left home, I finally decided it was my turn. The first year was frustrating: my fingers simply wouldn't do what I wanted, and I sounded terrible. What kept me going was my teacher, who never once made me feel stupid. Now I play in a small amateur orchestra, and the feeling of being part of something bigger is wonderful. I only wish I hadn't waited so long."],["B","Raj, 38 – piano","I'm an engineer, and I approached the piano like a technical problem. I bought an app, set myself targets and practised for exactly thirty minutes every day. It worked surprisingly well at first. But after about six months I hit a wall – I could play the notes, but it didn't sound like music. Taking lessons with a real teacher made me realise that I had been so focused on accuracy that I had forgotten to listen. I'm slower now, but I enjoy it far more."],["C","Maria, 52 – guitar","My son plays the guitar, and I started partly so that we would have something to share. It's been a lovely way to spend time together, although he's a rather impatient teacher! I've never been very disciplined about practising, and I don't mind admitting that I'm not particularly good. But I don't play to impress anyone. It helps me relax after work, and that's enough for me."],["D","Tom, 61 – saxophone","After I retired, I needed something to fill my days, and a friend suggested joining a jazz class. I was nervous about playing in front of others – I'd never done anything like that. In fact, the group turned out to be the best thing about it. We're all beginners, so nobody judges anyone. Learning has been harder than I expected, especially reading music, but I've met people I would never otherwise have known."]],"q":[{"q":"Who mentions that their teacher's attitude helped them continue?","a":"A","tg":["detail"],"e":"Helen: öğretmeni onu hiç aptal hissettirmedi."},{"q":"Who says that they do not practise regularly?","a":"C","tg":["detail"],"e":"Maria: \"never been very disciplined about practising\"."},{"q":"Who found that their progress suddenly stopped?","a":"B","tg":["inference"],"e":"Raj: \"after about six months I hit a wall\"."},{"q":"Who regrets not starting earlier?","a":"A","tg":["wish-if-only"],"e":"Helen: \"I only wish I hadn't waited so long\"."},{"q":"Who was worried about performing with other people?","a":"D","tg":["detail"],"e":"Tom: başkalarının önünde çalmaktan çekiniyordu."},{"q":"Who began learning to share an interest with a family member?","a":"C","tg":["purpose"],"e":"Maria: oğluyla ortak bir şeyleri olsun diye."},{"q":"Who used technology to plan their learning?","a":"B","tg":["detail"],"e":"Raj bir uygulama aldı, hedefler koydu."},{"q":"Who says that making new friends has been the main benefit?","a":"D","tg":["attitude"],"e":"Tom: grup en iyi şeyi oldu; başka türlü tanımayacağı insanlarla tanıştı."},{"q":"Who admits that they are not very skilled?","a":"C","tg":["attitude"],"e":"Maria: \"I'm not particularly good\"."},{"q":"Who realised that they had been concentrating on the wrong thing?","a":"B","tg":["inference"],"e":"Raj: doğruluğa o kadar odaklanmış ki dinlemeyi unutmuş."}]}]},"L":{"min":40,"parts":[{"k":"L1","nm":"Dinleme 1 · Kısa kayıtlar","ti":"Listening Part 1","ins":"Sekiz farklı durumda kısa kayıtlar dinleyeceksin. Her soru için en uygun cevabı seç.","ty":"mc","q":[{"id":"l1a","sc":[["F","I've been to that café three times now and I still can't decide. The coffee's fine, nothing special. The cakes are lovely, though a bit pricey. What really gets me is that you can never find a seat – it's always packed with students working on laptops for hours."]],"q":"You hear a woman talking about a café. What is her main complaint?","o":["The coffee is poor.","It is too expensive.","It is too crowded."],"a":2,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"\"What really gets me ...\" asıl şikâyeti gösterir: hiç yer bulunamıyor."},{"id":"l1b","sc":[["M","So how was the interview?"],["F","Well, they asked all the questions I'd prepared for, which was a relief. But then they asked me to give a presentation I didn't know about. I think I managed, but I could have done it much better with some warning."]],"q":"You hear a woman talking about a job interview. How does she feel about it?","o":["confident that the interview went better than expected","disappointed with part of her performance","annoyed by the questions she was asked"],"a":1,"tg":["attitude","modals-deduction"],"e":"\"I could have done it much better\" – sunum kısmından memnun değil."},{"id":"l1c","sc":[["M","The documentary was supposed to be about climate change, but honestly, half of it was the presenter talking about his own travels. There were some amazing shots of the Arctic, I'll give it that. But I learned almost nothing new."]],"q":"You hear a man talking about a documentary. What is his opinion?","o":["It was too focused on the presenter.","The photography was less impressive than expected.","It was too long."],"a":0,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Yarısı sunucunun kendi gezileriydi; görüntüleri beğendi."},{"id":"l1d","sc":[["F","Hi Josh, it's Emma. About Saturday – I know we said we'd meet at the station, but my brother can give us a lift, so we can pick you up from your place at ten instead. Let me know if that works."]],"q":"You hear a woman leaving a message. Why is she calling?","o":["to cancel their plans for Saturday","to change an arrangement","to ask for a lift"],"a":1,"tg":["purpose"],"e":"İstasyon yerine evden alacaklar: plan değişikliği."},{"id":"l1e","sc":[["M","People think being a mountain guide is all about climbing. Actually, most of my job is checking weather reports and making decisions about whether it's safe to go. The hardest part is telling a group who have paid a lot of money that we're turning back."]],"q":"You hear a mountain guide talking about his job. What does he find most difficult?","o":["understanding weather reports","disappointing his clients","climbing in bad conditions"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"The hardest part is telling a group ... we're turning back\"."},{"id":"l1f","sc":[["F","Did you finish that novel?"],["M","I did, eventually. The first half really dragged, to be honest. I nearly gave up. But the ending was so clever that I immediately wanted to read it again."],["F","That's quite a recommendation."]],"q":"You hear two friends talking about a novel. What does the man say about it?","o":["It started slowly but was worth finishing.","It was much better than the film version of it.","The ending was disappointing."],"a":0,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"İlk yarı sıkıcıydı ama sonu çok zekiceydi."},{"id":"l1g","sc":[["M","Right, everyone, before we start. The lab on the second floor is being repaired, so today's experiment will be in room fourteen. Please leave your bags outside and don't touch any equipment until I say so."]],"q":"You hear a teacher talking to students. What is he doing?","o":["explaining a change of room","describing an experiment","apologising for being late"],"a":0,"tg":["gist"],"e":"Laboratuvar tamirde; deney 14 numaralı odada."},{"id":"l1h","sc":[["F","When I started the business, I thought the difficult part would be finding customers. In fact, they came quite quickly. What I hadn't expected was how hard it would be to find reliable staff. I must have interviewed fifty people before I found the right team."]],"q":"You hear a woman talking about starting a business. What surprised her?","o":["how quickly she managed to find customers","the difficulty of hiring good employees","the amount of money she needed"],"a":1,"tg":["detail","past-perfect"],"e":"\"What I hadn't expected was how hard it would be to find reliable staff\"."}]},{"k":"L2","nm":"Dinleme 2 · Cümle tamamlama","ti":"Listening Part 2","ins":"Bir konuşma dinleyeceksin. Cümleleri bir kelime ya da kısa bir ifadeyle tamamla.","ty":"open","id":"l2","title":"Cümleler","sc":[["F","Hi everyone. I'm Lena, and I spent last summer working as a volunteer at a sea turtle project in Costa Rica. I want to tell you a bit about it in case any of you are thinking of doing something similar. I'd always loved animals, but what made me apply was a documentary I watched about turtles losing their nesting beaches. The project is on a remote beach, and the nearest town is two hours away by boat, so you really are cut off. We lived in simple wooden huts, and there was electricity for only three hours each evening. Our main job was patrolling the beach at night, looking for female turtles coming ashore to lay their eggs. We had to move the eggs to a protected area called a hatchery, because otherwise they'd be taken by people or eaten by dogs. The patrols started at nine p.m. and could last until four in the morning, which was exhausting. People often ask what the most difficult thing was. Surprisingly, it wasn't the long nights – it was the insects! I'd recommend bringing lots of strong repellent. The best moment for me was watching about a hundred baby turtles reach the sea for the first time. If you're interested, the project accepts volunteers for a minimum of four weeks, and you don't need any experience, just a lot of patience."]],"p":"Lena decided to volunteer after seeing a (1) ______ about turtles.\nThe nearest town can only be reached by (2) ______.\nThe volunteers had electricity for (3) ______ hours a day.\nAt night, volunteers looked for turtles on the (4) ______.\nThe eggs were moved to a place called the (5) ______.\nWithout protection, the eggs might be eaten by (6) ______.\nNight patrols sometimes finished at (7) ______ a.m.\nLena found the (8) ______ the hardest thing to deal with.\nHer favourite moment was seeing young turtles reach the (9) ______.\nVolunteers must stay for at least (10) ______ weeks.","a":[["documentary"],["boat"],["3","three"],["beach"],["hatchery"],["dogs"],["4","four","4.00"],["insects"],["sea"],["4","four"]],"tg":[["detail"],["detail"],["spelling"],["detail"],["spelling"],["detail"],["spelling"],["detail"],["detail"],["spelling"]],"e":["\"a documentary I watched about turtles\".","\"two hours away by boat\".","\"electricity for only three hours each evening\".","\"patrolling the beach at night\".","\"a protected area called a hatchery\".","\"taken by people or eaten by dogs\" – boşluk \"eaten by\"dan sonra: dogs.","\"could last until four in the morning\".","\"it wasn't the long nights – it was the insects\".","\"reach the sea for the first time\".","\"a minimum of four weeks\"."]},{"k":"L3","nm":"Dinleme 3 · Çoklu eşleştirme","ti":"Listening Part 3","ins":"Beş kişinin konuşmasını dinleyeceksin. Her konuşmacı için doğru seçeneği (A–H) seç. Üç seçenek fazladır.","ty":"match","id":"l3","sc":[["M","Speaker one. I'd always assumed I'd go to university straight after school, like my friends. But I wasn't sure what to study, so I took a year off and worked in a hotel. It was the best decision I ever made. Dealing with difficult guests taught me more about people than any course could have."],["F","#1.2"],["F","Speaker two. For me, the turning point was a teacher who believed in me. I'd always been told I was bad at maths, and I'd started to believe it. She showed me that I'd simply been taught in the wrong way. I'm now in my second year of an engineering degree, which my younger self would never have believed."],["M","#1.2"],["M2","Speaker three. I moved abroad when I was twenty, without speaking a word of the language. The first few months were really lonely. But being forced to manage on my own made me far more confident. These days I'm not afraid of anything new, whether it's a job or a hobby."],["F","#1.2"],["F2","Speaker four. I failed my driving test four times. Each time I was more nervous than the last. Eventually my instructor told me to stop thinking about the test and just enjoy driving. It sounds silly, but it worked. And I learned that worrying about failure can actually make you fail."],["M","#1.2"],["M","Speaker five. When my grandfather got ill, I spent a lot of time looking after him. We talked for hours, and I realised how little I'd known about his life. It made me want to record older people's stories, and that's what I do now – I make podcasts about people's memories."]],"opts":[["A","It made me less frightened of new experiences."],["B","It showed me that someone else's opinion can change my view of myself."],["C","It gave me an idea for my career."],["D","It taught me to deal with people."],["E","It made me realise I should have studied harder."],["F","It helped me become closer to my friends."],["G","It showed me that anxiety can make things worse."],["H","It made me decide to move abroad."]],"q":[{"q":"Speaker 1","a":"D","tg":["gist"],"e":"Otelde zor misafirlerle uğraşmak ona insanları öğretti."},{"q":"Speaker 2","a":"B","tg":["gist"],"e":"Ona inanan bir öğretmen kendine bakışını değiştirdi."},{"q":"Speaker 3","a":"A","tg":["gist"],"e":"\"I'm not afraid of anything new\"."},{"q":"Speaker 4","a":"G","tg":["gist"],"e":"\"worrying about failure can actually make you fail\"."},{"q":"Speaker 5","a":"C","tg":["gist"],"e":"Büyükbabasıyla konuşmaları onu anı podcast'leri yapmaya yöneltti."}]},{"k":"L4","nm":"Dinleme 4 · Röportaj","ti":"Listening Part 4","ins":"Bir röportaj dinleyeceksin. Her soru için en uygun cevabı seç.","ty":"mc","id":"l4","sc":[["M","My guest today is Sophie Adams, who designs video games. Sophie, how did you get into the industry?"],["F","Almost by accident, really. I studied art at university and I wanted to be an illustrator. But a friend was working on a small game and needed someone to draw the characters. I said yes mainly because I needed the money, and I just fell in love with it."],["M","Many people imagine game designers spend all day playing games. Is that true?"],["F","I wish! Actually, I spend most of my time in meetings, discussing ideas with programmers and writers. The creative part, sketching characters and worlds, is maybe twenty percent of my week. It's the part I love, but games are made by large teams, so communication is everything."],["M","What makes a game successful, in your view?"],["F","People often say graphics, but I disagree. Some of the most popular games have very simple graphics. What matters is whether players care about what happens. If they feel something for the characters, they'll keep playing."],["M","You've spoken publicly about the long working hours in the industry."],["F","Yes. Before a game is released, teams often work late every night for months. Some people see that as part of the job, but I think it's a sign of poor planning. On my current project, we've set clear deadlines from the start, and so far it's working."],["M","What advice would you give to young people who want to do your job?"],["F","Make things. Don't wait for a degree or permission. There are free tools online, and even a very simple game you've finished will teach you more than a perfect one you never complete. And play games critically – ask yourself why something works or doesn't."],["M","And what's next for you?"],["F","I'd love to make a game for younger children that helps them learn to read. It's a completely different challenge, and that's what excites me."]],"q":[{"q":"Why did Sophie first work on a game?","o":["She had always wanted to design games.","She needed to earn some money.","Her university course required it."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"I said yes mainly because I needed the money\"."},{"q":"What does Sophie say about her working week?","o":["She spends most of it sketching characters and worlds.","She would much prefer to spend less time in meetings.","Working with other people takes up most of her time."],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Zamanının çoğu toplantıda; yaratıcı kısım %20."},{"q":"According to Sophie, what makes a game successful?","o":["impressive graphics and realistic worlds","an emotional connection with characters","a simple story that is easy to follow"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"If they feel something for the characters, they'll keep playing\"."},{"q":"What is Sophie's view of long working hours?","o":["They are unavoidable in the industry.","They are the result of bad organisation.","They help teams to finish games more quickly."],"a":1,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"\"I think it's a sign of poor planning\"."},{"q":"What does Sophie advise young people to do?","o":["get a degree in game design","finish small projects of their own","wait until they have the right equipment"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Basit ama bitmiş bir oyun, hiç bitmeyen mükemmel bir oyundan çok şey öğretir."},{"q":"Why does Sophie want to make a game for children?","o":["She wants to try something new and difficult.","Her own children have found learning to read hard.","Games for children sell better."],"a":0,"tg":["inference"],"e":"\"a completely different challenge, and that's what excites me\"."},{"q":"How does Sophie feel about her career?","o":["She regrets not becoming an illustrator.","She is enthusiastic about it.","She finds it less creative than she hoped."],"a":1,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"\"I just fell in love with it\" ve yeni projeden heyecanla bahsediyor."}]}]},"W":{"min":80,"tasks":[{"id":"b2x-w1","part":1,"kind":"essay","t":"Part 1 · Deneme: Okul üniforması","in":"In your English class you have been talking about school rules. Now your English teacher has asked you to write an essay. Write your essay using all the notes and giving reasons for your point of view. Write 140–190 words.","mail":"Should all students wear a school uniform?\n\nNotes – write about:\n1. cost for families\n2. students' freedom to express themselves\n3. ............ (your own idea)","pts":[["Aileler için maliyetten bahset",["cost","expensive","money","cheap","afford","price"]],["Kendini ifade özgürlüğünden bahset",["express","freedom","individual","personality","identity","style"]],["Kendi fikrini ekle",["also","another","in addition","moreover","furthermore","what is more"]],["Bir sonuç / görüş belirt",["in conclusion","to sum up","overall","in my opinion","i believe"]]],"min":140,"max":190,"model":"School uniforms are common in many countries, but there is still a lively debate about whether they are a good idea.\n\nOne argument in favour of uniforms is cost. Families do not have to buy a wide range of fashionable clothes for school, and children are less likely to feel pressure to wear expensive brands. On the other hand, uniforms themselves can be surprisingly expensive, especially if they can only be bought from one shop.\n\nCritics also argue that uniforms limit students' freedom to express their personality. Teenagers are developing their identity, and clothes are an important part of this. However, students can still show their individuality through their hairstyles, bags and, above all, their ideas.\n\nIn addition, uniforms may help create a sense of belonging. When everyone looks the same, it is harder to judge others by their appearance, which can reduce bullying.\n\nIn conclusion, although uniforms are not perfect, I believe their advantages outweigh their disadvantages, provided that schools keep them simple and affordable.","more":[["Örnek 2 · karşı görüşle","The question of whether students should wear uniforms is one that divides parents, teachers and students alike. In my opinion, schools should allow students to choose their own clothes.\n\nSupporters of uniforms often argue that they save families money. However, this is not always true. Uniforms frequently have to be bought from specific shops at high prices, and students still need ordinary clothes for evenings and weekends, so families end up paying twice.\n\nMore importantly, uniforms prevent young people from expressing their personality at an age when they are trying to discover who they are. Choosing what to wear helps teenagers develop confidence and make their own decisions.\n\nIt is also worth considering comfort. Many uniforms are made from cheap materials that are too hot in summer and too cold in winter, which can make it harder for students to concentrate.\n\nIn conclusion, although uniforms may appear to create equality, I believe their disadvantages are greater. A simple dress code that bans inappropriate clothing would be a fairer and more practical solution."],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (C1'e yakın)","School uniforms are a familiar sight in many countries, yet whether they actually benefit students remains a matter of considerable debate.\n\nFrom a financial point of view, the picture is mixed. Admittedly, uniforms remove the pressure to keep up with expensive fashion trends, which can be a significant burden for less well-off families. On the other hand, compulsory items sold by a single supplier can be surprisingly costly.\n\nThe argument that uniforms restrict self-expression is perhaps overstated. Students have countless other ways of showing their individuality, from their hairstyles and accessories to the music they listen to and the opinions they hold. Arguably, removing clothing as a source of competition allows them to focus on what they say rather than what they wear.\n\nA further point in favour of uniforms is safety. On school trips, for instance, teachers can identify their students instantly in a crowd.\n\nWeighing up these arguments, I am in favour of school uniforms, provided they are affordable and comfortable. Schools should consult students and parents when designing them, so that the uniform becomes a source of pride rather than resentment."]]},{"choice":[{"id":"b2x-w2","part":2,"kind":"article","t":"Part 2 · Makale: Hayatımı değiştiren bir uygulama","in":"You see this announcement in an English-language magazine. Write your article in 140–190 words.","mail":"ARTICLES WANTED\nThe app that changed my life\nIs there an app that has made a real difference to your daily life? What does it do, and why is it so important to you? Would you recommend it to others?\nWe will publish the best articles next month.","pts":[["Uygulamanın ne yaptığını anlat",["app","helps","allows","lets","use"]],["Neden önemli olduğunu açıkla",["because","since","important","difference","changed"]],["Tavsiye edip etmediğini söyle",["recommend","would suggest","should try","advise"]],["Okuyucuya hitap eden bir başlık",["@title"]]],"min":140,"max":190,"model":"The little green owl that taught me Spanish\n\nTwo years ago, I could barely say \"hello\" in Spanish. Today I can chat with my Argentinian neighbours for hours, and I owe it all to a language app with a cheerful green owl.\n\nThe app gives you short lessons that take just five minutes. You match words, listen to sentences and repeat them out loud. It sounds simple, but the secret is that it reminds you every day. If you forget, the owl sends you a slightly sad message – and believe me, you will not want to disappoint it!\n\nWhat really changed my life, though, was not just learning a language. The app taught me that small daily habits can lead to big results. I now use the same approach for exercise and reading.\n\nWould I recommend it? Absolutely, but with one warning. An app alone will not make you fluent. You also need to speak to real people, make mistakes and laugh about them. Think of the app as a friendly start, not the whole journey.","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı bir uygulama","How a map app made me braver\n\nI have always had a terrible sense of direction. For years, I was too nervous to explore new places on my own in case I got lost. Then a map app changed everything.\n\nThe app shows you exactly where you are, gives you directions step by step and even tells you which bus to take and when it will arrive. It also suggests interesting places nearby, such as cafés, museums and parks, which I would never have discovered otherwise.\n\nThanks to it, I have become much more independent. Last summer I travelled alone to a city I had never visited, and I found my way around without any problems. My parents were amazed!\n\nWould I recommend it? Definitely, especially to anyone who feels anxious about travelling. However, I would also advise people to look up from the screen sometimes. Getting a little lost can be part of the adventure, and some of my best memories come from the moments when I ignored the app completely."],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (C1'e yakın)","The app that gave me back my evenings\n\nI used to be the kind of person who promised to start exercising \"next Monday\" – every Monday. That all changed when a friend persuaded me to download a simple habit-tracking app.\n\nAt first glance, it does very little. You choose the habits you want to build, such as drinking more water or going for a run, and tick them off each day. What makes it surprisingly powerful is the chain it creates: after a week of ticks, you become strangely reluctant to break it.\n\nWithin two months, I was running three times a week and reading before bed instead of scrolling through social media. The app did not make me more disciplined overnight, but it made my progress visible, and that turned out to be enormously motivating.\n\nI would wholeheartedly recommend it to anyone who struggles to stick to good intentions. One word of warning, though: choose one or two habits to start with. Try to change everything at once, and you may well end up changing nothing at all."]]},{"id":"b2x-w3","part":2,"kind":"review","t":"Part 2 · İnceleme: Bir restoran ya da kafe","in":"You see this announcement on an English-language website. Write your review in 140–190 words.","mail":"REVIEWS WANTED\nA place to eat\nHave you been to a restaurant or café recently that you think other people should know about? Write a review describing the place, the food and the service, and say whether you would recommend it.\nThe best reviews will be posted on our website.","pts":[["Yeri tanıt",["located","situated","café","restaurant","atmosphere","decor"]],["Yemekten bahset",["food","dish","menu","delicious","tasty","ordered"]],["Servisten bahset",["service","staff","waiter","waitress","friendly","slow"]],["Tavsiye et ya da etme",["recommend","worth","would suggest","avoid"]]],"min":140,"max":190,"model":"Hidden gem: Café Lavanta\n\nTucked away in a narrow street behind the main square, Café Lavanta is easy to miss – but it is definitely worth looking for.\n\nThe café is small, with only eight tables, and the walls are covered with paintings by local artists. Soft jazz plays in the background, which makes it a perfect place to relax or read a book on a rainy afternoon.\n\nThe menu is short but creative. I ordered the lentil soup, which was rich and full of flavour, followed by a lemon cake that was light and not too sweet. Prices are reasonable, especially considering the quality of the ingredients, most of which come from nearby farms.\n\nThe only disappointment was the service. Although the staff were friendly, there were only two of them, and we waited almost twenty minutes for our drinks.\n\nOverall, I would highly recommend Café Lavanta to anyone looking for a quiet place with excellent home-made food. Just make sure you are not in a hurry!","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı bir yer","Burger Box: big flavours, small space\n\nIf you are looking for a quick, tasty meal in the city centre, Burger Box on Station Road is well worth a visit.\n\nThe restaurant is tiny, with just a few high tables and a long counter facing the open kitchen, so you can watch the chefs at work. The atmosphere is lively and the music is loud, which makes it popular with students but perhaps less suitable for a quiet conversation.\n\nThe menu focuses on burgers, and they are excellent. I tried the classic cheeseburger, which was juicy and served in a soft, freshly baked bun. The sweet potato fries were crispy and full of flavour. There are also two vegetarian options, which my friend said were just as good.\n\nThe service was fast and friendly, and our food arrived in less than ten minutes, even though the place was full.\n\nMy only complaint is the price of drinks, which seemed rather high compared with the food. Overall, though, I would definitely recommend Burger Box for a casual lunch with friends."],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (C1'e yakın)","A taste of the sea: Balıkçı Ahmet\n\nPerched at the end of a small fishing harbour, Balıkçı Ahmet looks, at first glance, like little more than a wooden hut. Do not be fooled. This modest family-run restaurant serves some of the freshest seafood I have ever eaten.\n\nThere is no printed menu. Instead, the owner, Ahmet, brings a tray of that morning's catch to your table and recommends how each fish should be cooked. We chose grilled sea bass, which arrived simply seasoned with lemon and olive oil, alongside a selection of home-made starters, including a remarkable smoked aubergine salad.\n\nThe service is warm rather than polished. Ahmet's teenage son, who was clearly still learning, forgot our drinks twice, but his enthusiasm more than made up for it. Prices are reasonable, though not cheap, which is hardly surprising given the quality of the ingredients.\n\nIf I had one reservation, it would be the lack of indoor seating, which makes the restaurant far less appealing on cold or windy evenings.\n\nNevertheless, for anyone who appreciates simple food cooked with care, Balıkçı Ahmet is an absolute must. Book ahead in summer, and ask for a table by the water."]]}]}]},"S":{"parts":[{"id":"b2x-s1","p":1,"t":"Part 1 · Tanışma","ins":"Sınav görevlisi seninle ilgili sorular soruyor. Her soruya birkaç cümleyle, nedenler ve örnekler vererek cevap ver.","q":[["Where are you from, and what do you like about living there?",30,["I'm from","what I like","the best thing","because"],["I'm from Izmir, on the Aegean coast. What I like most is that you're never far from the sea – even in the city centre you can walk along the seafront in the evening. People are also generally quite relaxed and friendly, and there's a really good mix of old neighbourhoods and modern areas.","I'm originally from Konya, in central Anatolia, although I've been living in Istanbul for a few years now. What I miss about Konya is how calm it is, and the food, of course. Istanbul is exciting, but it's exhausting – the traffic can be a nightmare."]],["What do you enjoy doing in your free time?",30,["I enjoy","I'm really into","whenever I can"],["I'm really into photography at the moment. Whenever I have a free afternoon, I take my camera and wander around the old parts of the city looking for interesting faces and buildings. I've also started editing my photos, which takes almost as long as taking them!","I spend a lot of my free time playing volleyball. I'm in a local team and we train twice a week, with matches most weekends. Apart from that, I enjoy cooking for friends – I'm trying to learn dishes from different countries."]],["Do you think you will use English in your future job? Why?",30,["I'm likely to","I'll probably","I hope","because"],["I'll probably use it a lot, because I'm hoping to work in tourism, and most of our visitors communicate in English even if it isn't their first language. Even in jobs that don't seem international, English is useful for reading research or using software, so I think it's essential.","Definitely. I want to study engineering, and most of the best articles and online courses are in English. I'd also like to work for an international company one day, perhaps abroad, so being able to communicate confidently would give me a real advantage."]],["Tell us about a memorable celebration you have been to.",40,["I remember","it was","what made it","I'll never forget"],["I'll never forget my cousin's wedding last year. It was held outdoors in an olive grove, and what made it special was that all the food was cooked by the family, not a catering company. There must have been three hundred guests. We danced until three in the morning, and my grandparents, who are in their eighties, danced longer than anyone!","A memorable celebration for me was my graduation from secondary school. After the ceremony, our whole class went to a restaurant by the sea with our teachers. Some of the teachers gave funny speeches about each student, and it was the first time I'd seen them so relaxed. It was emotional too, because we knew we'd all be going in different directions."]]]},{"id":"b2x-s2","p":2,"t":"Part 2 · İki fotoğrafı karşılaştır","ins":"İki fotoğrafı yaklaşık 1 dakika karşılaştır ve sorulan soruyu cevapla. Fotoğrafları tek tek anlatma; benzerlik ve farkları söyle, tahmin yürüt.","img":[["👩‍💻🏢☕","Fotoğraf 1: Kalabalık bir ofiste, bilgisayar başında çalışan genç bir kadın.","b2-office"],["👨‍🌾🌾🚜","Fotoğraf 2: Tarlada, traktörün yanında çalışan bir çiftçi.","b2-farm"]],"q":[["Your photographs show people working in different places. Compare the photographs, and say what might be enjoyable about working in each place.",60,["both photos show","whereas","while","on the other hand","might","I imagine","it looks as if"],["Both photos show people at work, but in very different environments. In the first one, a young woman is working at a computer in what looks like a busy open-plan office, whereas the second shows a farmer standing next to a tractor in a field. I imagine the office worker might enjoy being surrounded by colleagues – she can chat during breaks and probably works as part of a team. Her job is probably quite varied, and she's protected from the weather. On the other hand, the farmer is working outdoors, which must be wonderful on a sunny day. He's probably his own boss, so he can decide how to organise his day, and there's something satisfying about producing food. However, his work is likely to be physically much harder, and he depends on the weather.","These two photographs show contrasting workplaces. The woman in the first one seems to be working in a modern office, surrounded by other people and screens, while the man in the second one is out in the countryside on a farm. What might be enjoyable about the office is the social side – having colleagues to share ideas with and perhaps opportunities for promotion. It also looks comfortable, with a coffee machine nearby. For the farmer, the enjoyable part is probably the freedom and fresh air. He doesn't have to sit at a desk all day, and he can see the direct results of his hard work when he harvests his crops. I suppose it's also peaceful compared with a noisy office."]],["Which of these places would you prefer to work in? Why?",30,["I'd prefer","I'd rather","because"],["Personally, I'd rather work in the office, because I enjoy working with other people, and I'm not sure I'd cope with getting up at five every morning like a farmer. I also like having clear working hours, so I can plan my free time.","I'd choose the farm, to be honest. I can't imagine sitting in front of a screen for eight hours a day. Working outdoors with my hands would make me feel healthier and more connected to nature, even if it's harder physically."]]]},{"id":"b2x-s3","p":3,"t":"Part 3 · Birlikte karar verme","ins":"Gerçek sınavda bu bölüm bir partnerle yapılır. Burada önce seçenekleri tek tek tartış (2 dakika), sonra bir karar ver (1 dakika).","opts":["🌳 more parks","🚲 bike lanes","🎭 a cultural centre","🏊 a sports centre","🚌 cheaper public transport"],"q":[["Imagine that a town wants to improve life for young people. Here are some ideas they are considering. Talk about how these ideas might improve life for young people in the town.",120,["let's start with","what do you think about","that's a good point","I'm not so sure","on the other hand","it would encourage"],["Let's start with more parks. They would give young people somewhere free to meet, which is important because cafés and cinemas are quite expensive. They're also good for mental health. Bike lanes are a good idea too, since they'd make cycling safer and encourage people to be more active, and teenagers could get around without depending on their parents. A cultural centre could offer concerts, theatre and workshops, which might help young people discover new talents, although it would only be useful if the activities were cheap or free. A sports centre is obviously great for health, but many towns already have one, so it might not make much difference. Finally, cheaper public transport would make a huge difference, because it would allow young people to get to school, work and all these other places more easily. In a way, it would make all the other ideas more useful. So for me, the best approach would be to combine a couple of these ideas – for example, cheaper transport together with a cultural centre – because young people have different interests and needs. A sports-loving teenager and an artistic one would want very different things, and the town should try to offer something for both.","I'll begin with the cultural centre, because I think it's the most interesting option. It could host music lessons, art exhibitions and film clubs, and it would give young people a place to express themselves. On the other hand, a sports centre would probably attract more people, since sport is popular with most teenagers. Bike lanes are practical but perhaps less exciting – they'd improve safety, though, which is important. More parks would make the town greener and give young people a place to hang out, especially in summer. As for cheaper public transport, I think it's essential in towns where young people live far from the centre. Without it, they can't really take advantage of any of these facilities. I also think it's worth considering cost. A cultural centre or a sports centre would be very expensive to build and run, whereas bike lanes and parks are relatively cheap and last for years. So the town would need to think carefully about what gives the best value for money, and ideally it should ask young people what they actually want before deciding anything."]],["Now you have about a minute to decide which idea would be the most useful for young people.",60,["I think we should choose","the most useful","because","even though","it would benefit"],["If I had to choose one, I'd go for cheaper public transport. Even though it's less exciting than a cultural centre, it would benefit almost every young person in the town, whether they're students or have part-time jobs. It would also make all the other facilities easier to reach, and it's good for the environment, since fewer people would need to be driven around by their parents. It's also the option that helps young people from poorer families the most, which I think matters.","I think the cultural centre would be the most valuable. Many towns already have parks and sports facilities, but there's often nowhere for young people interested in art, music or drama to meet. It would offer something genuinely new and could become a real centre of community life. It might even encourage young people who usually stay at home to go out and meet others with similar interests, which would be good for their confidence and well-being."]]]},{"id":"b2x-s4","p":4,"t":"Part 4 · Tartışma","ins":"3. bölümün konusuyla ilgili daha genel sorular. Görüşünü nedenler ve örneklerle açıkla.","q":[["Do you think young people today have enough places to spend their free time?",45,["I'd say","it depends","for example","on the whole"],["On the whole, I'd say it depends on where you live. In big cities, there are plenty of options – cinemas, sports clubs, cafés – although many of them are expensive. In smaller towns, young people often end up spending time in shopping centres or online, simply because there's nothing else. I think more free, safe spaces would make a real difference, especially for teenagers who don't have much money.","Honestly, I don't think they do. Most places for young people cost money, and many teenagers don't have much. Youth centres used to be common, but a lot of them have closed. As a result, young people often just hang around in the street, and then adults complain about them! I think towns should invest in youth centres again, with free activities and staff who understand teenagers."]],["Should local governments ask young people for their opinions before making decisions?",45,["definitely","after all","otherwise","I believe"],["Definitely. After all, young people are the ones who will live with the results of decisions for the longest time. Otherwise, money might be spent on projects that nobody actually uses. Some towns have youth councils where teenagers can give their opinions, and I think every town should have one. It would also teach young people how local democracy works, which could make them more likely to vote later.","I think so, but it needs to be done properly. Sometimes governments ask young people's opinions just to look good and then ignore them. If they're going to ask, they should explain afterwards how the opinions were used. Otherwise, young people will stop taking part. Online surveys could be a simple way to reach more of them, since that's where they spend most of their time."]],["Some people say that technology has made young people less active. Do you agree?",45,["to some extent","however","on the other hand","for instance"],["I agree to some extent. Many teenagers spend hours on their phones or playing games instead of being active. However, technology can also motivate people – for instance, fitness apps and online challenges have encouraged lots of my friends to start running, and you can follow exercise videos at home. So it depends how you use it. I'd say the real problem isn't technology itself but the lack of balance.","I'd say yes, mostly. When my parents were young, they played outside until it got dark, but now children spend their free time indoors in front of screens. Schools have also reduced sport in some places. I think it's a serious problem, because it affects both physical and mental health. I think the solution is to make physical activity more attractive, for example with free sports clubs and safe places to cycle."]]]}]}},"lv":"B2","name":"B2 First"},{"id":"c1-1","t":"Sınav 1","sec":{"R":{"min":90,"parts":[{"k":"R1","nm":"Okuma 1 · Çoktan seçmeli boşluk","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 1","ins":"Metni oku ve her boşluk için en uygun kelimeyi (A, B, C ya da D) seç.","ty":"cloze","title":"The value of boredom","p":"In an age of constant stimulation, boredom has acquired a bad (1) ______. We reach for our phones the moment we have nothing to do, as if a few minutes of idleness were something to be avoided at all (2) ______. Yet psychologists are increasingly coming round to the view that boredom serves a useful purpose. When the mind is not (3) ______ by external demands, it tends to wander, and it is in this state that many of our most original ideas (4) ______. In one well-known study, participants who had first completed a deliberately tedious task went on to produce more creative answers than those who had not. Boredom, it seems, acts as a (5) ______ that our current situation is unsatisfying, prompting us to seek out new goals. The problem is not boredom itself but our (6) ______ to tolerate it. By filling every spare moment with entertainment, we may be (7) ______ ourselves of the very conditions in which creativity thrives. Perhaps the next time we feel bored, we should resist the (8) ______ to reach for a screen.","o":[[["name","fame","reputation","character"],2,["collocation"]],[["costs","prices","means","rates"],0,["collocation"]],[["engaged","involved","attached","committed"],0,["vocab"]],[["emerge","happen","result","proceed"],0,["vocab"]],[["hint","signal","symbol","notice"],1,["vocab"]],[["inability","incapability","weakness","shortage"],0,["word-formation"]],[["denying","depriving","removing","refusing"],1,["collocation"]],[["urge","wish","will","want"],0,["collocation"]]],"e":["acquire a bad reputation: kötü bir üne kavuşmak.","at all costs: ne pahasına olursa olsun.","engaged by demands: taleplerle meşgul edilmek.","ideas emerge: fikirler ortaya çıkar. happen, result ve proceed bu anlamı vermez.","act as a signal that: -nin işareti olmak.","our inability to: -e katlanamamamız (yapamama). weakness ve shortage \"to + fiil\" almaz.","deprive someone of something: birini bir şeyden yoksun bırakmak.","resist the urge to: -me dürtüsüne direnmek."]},{"k":"R2","nm":"Okuma 2 · Açık boşluk","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 2","ins":"Metni oku ve her boşluğa TEK kelime yaz.","ty":"open","one":true,"title":"The longest-running experiment","p":"In 1927, Professor Thomas Parnell set (1) ______ an experiment at the University of Queensland to show that some substances which appear solid are (2) ______ fact extremely slow-moving liquids. He placed a sample of pitch, a material derived (3) ______ tar, in a sealed funnel and waited. It took eight years for the first drop to fall. Since then, only nine drops have fallen, and remarkably, nobody has ever seen one (4) ______ so. On one occasion, the drop fell while the camera that was supposed to be recording it was not working. Parnell himself died long (5) ______ the second drop. The experiment, (6) ______ is still running today, holds a world record as the longest continuously running laboratory experiment. (7) ______ its apparent simplicity, it has fascinated millions of people, many of whom watch a live online video in the hope of finally witnessing the moment when a drop falls. Whether anyone will ever succeed (8) ______ doing so remains to be seen.","a":[["up"],["in"],["from"],["do"],["before"],["which"],["despite","for"],["in"]],"tg":[["vocab"],["vocab"],["vocab"],["vocab"],["vocab"],["relative-clauses"],["linkers-contrast-purpose"],["gerund-infinitive"]],"e":["set up an experiment: deney kurmak.","in fact: aslında.","derived from: -den elde edilen.","seen one do so: birinin bunu yaptığını görmek (see + nesne + yalın fiil).","long before: -den çok önce.","Virgüllü ek bilgi: which.","Despite: -e rağmen. (\"For all its simplicity\" da mümkün.)","succeed in doing: -mekte başarılı olmak."]},{"k":"R3","nm":"Okuma 3 · Kelime türetme","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 3","ins":"Her boşluk için verilen kök kelimeyi uygun biçime getir.","ty":"open","title":"The rise of the four-day week","st":["RECENT","SIGNIFY","ANTICIPATE","SATISFY","DOUBT","FEASIBLE","RESIST","PRESSURE"],"p":"Until (1) ______, the idea of a four-day working week would have been dismissed as unrealistic. However, a number of large-scale trials have produced results of real (2) ______. Contrary to what many had (3) ______, productivity did not fall; in many companies it actually rose. Employees reported far greater job (4) ______ and fewer days off sick. Sceptics remain, of course. Some economists (5) ______ whether the model could work in industries such as healthcare, where constant cover is required. Others question the long-term (6) ______ of the scheme for small businesses with limited staff. Nevertheless, the (7) ______ that the idea once met is beginning to fade, and governments in several countries are coming under growing (8) ______ to support further trials.","a":[["recently"],["significance"],["anticipated"],["satisfaction"],["doubt"],["feasibility"],["resistance"],["pressure"]],"tg":[["word-formation"],["word-formation"],["word-formation"],["word-formation"],["word-formation"],["word-formation"],["word-formation"],["word-formation"]],"e":["Until recently: yakın zamana kadar (zarf).","of real significance: gerçek önemi olan (isim).","had anticipated: beklemişti (past perfect, V3).","job satisfaction: iş tatmini.","doubt whether: şüphe etmek (fiil, kök aynen). Sınavda bazen kök değişmez.","the long-term feasibility: uygulanabilirlik.","the resistance that the idea met: karşılaştığı direnç.","under pressure: baskı altında. Kök aynı kalır."]},{"k":"R4","nm":"Okuma 4 · Cümle dönüştürme","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 4","ins":"İkinci cümleyi, birinciyle aynı anlamı verecek şekilde tamamla. Verilen kelimeyi değiştirmeden kullan; 3–6 kelime yaz.","ty":"kwt","q":[{"s1":"I didn't realise how difficult the job was until I started it.","key":"DID","s2":"Only when I started the job ______ how difficult it was.","a":["did i realise","did i understand","did i find out","did i discover"],"tg":["inversion","transformation"],"e":"Only when ... ile devrik yapı: Only when I started the job did I realise.","mx":6},{"s1":"People say that the castle was built in the twelfth century.","key":"SAID","s2":"The castle ______ built in the twelfth century.","a":["is said to have been"],"tg":["passive-advanced"],"e":"is said to have been + V3.","mx":6},{"s1":"It was wrong of you to tell her the secret.","key":"OUGHT","s2":"You ______ her the secret.","a":["ought not to have told","oughtn't to have told"],"tg":["modals-deduction"],"e":"Geçmiş için eleştiri: ought not to have + V3.","mx":6},{"s1":"The meeting was postponed because the manager was ill.","key":"PUT","s2":"The meeting ______ account of the manager's illness.","a":["was put off on"],"tg":["vocab"],"e":"put off = postpone; on account of = because of.","mx":6},{"s1":"I'm sure he didn't mean to upset you.","key":"CAN'T","s2":"He ______ to upset you.","a":["can't have meant"],"tg":["modals-deduction"],"e":"Geçmişe dair olumsuz çıkarım: can't have + V3.","mx":6},{"s1":"She was very kind, so we invited her to stay.","key":"SUCH","s2":"She was ______ we invited her to stay.","a":["such a kind person that","such a kind woman that","such a kind woman","such a kind person"],"tg":["vocab"],"e":"such a + sıfat + isim + that.","mx":6}]},{"k":"R5","nm":"Okuma 5 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 5","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","ty":"mc","title":"The translator's dilemma","p":["Every translator I know has a story about a sentence that refused to be translated. Mine involves a single word in a Portuguese novel: saudade. Dictionaries offer \"longing\" or \"nostalgia\", but neither comes close. Saudade is longing for something you may never have had, tinged with a strange pleasure in the longing itself. I spent three days on that sentence and eventually settled for a paraphrase that I still find unsatisfactory.","The popular image of translation is of a mechanical process: find the equivalent word, move on. Anyone who has attempted it knows otherwise. Languages do not simply attach different labels to the same reality; they divide up reality in different ways. Russian distinguishes between light blue and dark blue as separate colours. Many languages have no word for \"privacy\". A translator is therefore constantly making choices, and every choice involves a loss.","This is why I am sceptical, though not dismissive, of the claim that machine translation will soon make human translators redundant. For instruction manuals and business emails, machines already perform impressively. But literature depends on tone, rhythm and the associations that words carry – qualities that are notoriously difficult to measure, let alone reproduce. When I read machine translations of poetry, I am struck less by their errors than by their flatness. Everything is correct and nothing is alive.","That said, I have come to use these tools myself, in the way a carpenter might use a power drill. A machine can produce a rough first draft in seconds, freeing me to concentrate on the decisions that matter. The danger, I suspect, is not that machines will replace translators, but that publishers, under pressure to cut costs, will decide that \"good enough\" is good enough. Readers may not even notice what they are missing – and that, perhaps, is the greatest loss of all."],"q":[{"q":"What does the writer suggest about the word \"saudade\"?","o":["It means exactly the same as the English word \"nostalgia\".","Its meaning changes completely depending on the context.","It combines feelings that English words do not capture together.","It is used mainly by older Portuguese speakers in formal writing."],"a":2,"tg":["inference"],"e":"Özlem ve özlemin kendisinden alınan tuhaf zevk; \"longing/nostalgia\" yetmiyor."},{"q":"How does the writer feel about his translation of the sentence?","o":["proud of his creativity","still not entirely happy with it","relieved that it was finished","surprised that it took him so many days"],"a":1,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"\"a paraphrase that I still find unsatisfactory\"."},{"q":"The examples in the second paragraph are used to show that","o":["some languages are more precise than others.","translators often make careless mistakes.","languages organise experience differently.","certain concepts cannot be expressed at all."],"a":2,"tg":["gist"],"e":"\"they divide up reality in different ways\"."},{"q":"What is the writer's main criticism of machine translations of poetry?","o":["They contain too many errors.","They lack life and character.","They take too long to produce.","They change the meaning of the original."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"Everything is correct and nothing is alive\"."},{"q":"Why does the writer mention a carpenter?","o":["to show that translation is a craft that needs training","to explain how he uses technology as a helpful tool","to suggest that machines are more efficient than people","to compare translators with other manual workers"],"a":1,"tg":["purpose"],"e":"Makine çeviriyi marangozun matkabı gibi bir araç olarak kullanıyor."},{"q":"What is the writer most concerned about?","o":["Human translators will soon lose most of their jobs to machines.","Readers will prefer machine translations.","Lower standards will be accepted without people realising.","Publishers will stop translating literature."],"a":2,"tg":["inference"],"e":"\"good enough\" kabul edilecek ve okurlar neyi kaçırdığını fark etmeyecek."}]},{"k":"R6","nm":"Okuma 6 · Metinler arası eşleştirme","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 6","ins":"Aynı konuda yazılmış dört görüşü (A–D) oku. Her soru için doğru yazarı seç.","ty":"match","title":"Should children be given homework?","opts":[["A","Educational psychologist","The research on homework is more nuanced than either side admits. For older students, a moderate amount is linked to better results, but for younger children the benefits are close to zero. What matters far more than quantity is purpose: tasks that simply repeat what was done in class achieve little. Homework should prepare students to learn something new, not just keep them busy. Parents, meanwhile, should be supporters, not supervisors."],["B","Secondary school teacher","I set homework because the school day is simply too short to cover the curriculum. Without independent practice, students forget most of what they learn. I accept that some tasks are poorly designed, and I have changed my own approach over the years. However, those who call for homework to be abolished entirely underestimate how much learning depends on repetition. Practice may be dull, but it works."],["C","Parent of three","Every evening in our house is a battle. My children come home exhausted, and then spend two hours on worksheets that seem to teach them nothing new. I end up doing half of it myself. I am not against learning at home, but it should be reading together or a project the children actually care about. The evidence that endless worksheets help young children is, as far as I can tell, non-existent."],["D","Head of a primary school","Five years ago we abolished homework for under-elevens, replacing it with a simple expectation that children read for pleasure each evening. Parents are asked only to show an interest, not to check or correct. I do not claim that homework itself was harmful – we simply wanted to try something different – and the results have been encouraging: reading levels have risen, and families report less stress. Older students preparing for exams may well need more structured independent work."]],"q":[{"q":"Which writer agrees with writer C that research shows little benefit from homework for young children?","a":"A","tg":["inference"],"e":"A: küçük çocuklar için fayda \"close to zero\". C de kanıt olmadığını söylüyor. D ödevin zararlı olduğunu iddia etmiyor."},{"q":"Which writer has a different view from writers A and C on the value of repetitive practice?","a":"B","tg":["attitude"],"e":"B tekrarın işe yaradığını savunuyor; A ve C tekrar eden ödevlerin az işe yaradığını söylüyor."},{"q":"Which writer has a similar view to writer A about the role parents should play?","a":"D","tg":["inference"],"e":"A: veliler denetçi değil destekçi olmalı. D: velilerden yalnızca ilgi göstermeleri isteniyor, kontrol etmeleri değil. C ise ödevin yarısını kendisi yapıyor."},{"q":"Which writer expresses a similar view to writer C about what kind of activity is useful at home?","a":"D","tg":["inference"],"e":"C: birlikte okuma; D: ödev yerine her akşam keyif için okuma."}]},{"k":"R7","nm":"Okuma 7 · Paragraf yerleştirme","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 7","ins":"Metinden altı paragraf çıkarıldı. Her boşluk için uygun paragrafı (A–G) seç. Bir paragraf fazladır.","ty":"gap","title":"Rewilding: letting nature take over","p":"When the Knepp estate in southern England stopped farming in 2001, its owners were facing financial ruin. The heavy clay soil had never been ideal for crops, and years of intensive agriculture had left the land exhausted.\n(1) ______\nThe idea was simple, if radical: rather than managing the land, they would let nature manage itself. Fences were removed, and small herds of cattle, pigs and deer were introduced to roam freely, imitating the large wild animals that once lived in Britain.\n(2) ______\nWithin a few years, the fields had begun to transform. Thorny bushes spread across former farmland, providing shelter for young trees. Ponds formed where pigs had dug up the ground.\n(3) ______\nPerhaps the most remarkable change was the return of species that had become rare across the country. Turtle doves, nightingales and purple emperor butterflies all appeared in significant numbers.\n(4) ______\nNot everyone was pleased, however. Neighbouring farmers complained that the estate looked untidy and worried that weeds would spread onto their land.\n(5) ______\nToday, Knepp is held up as a model of what is possible. It attracts thousands of visitors a year, and its income from tourism and nature-friendly meat now exceeds what it once earned from farming.\n(6) ______\nSupporters argue that if similar projects were adopted across the country, they could help reverse the decline in wildlife that has been one of the defining features of the last century.","opts":[["A","These animals turned out to be essential. By grazing, digging and trampling, they created a constantly changing mix of habitats, something no human manager could have planned."],["B","Faced with this situation, the owners took a decision that many of their neighbours considered foolish."],["C","For some, the sight of this \"messy\" landscape was a betrayal of centuries of careful land management."],["D","Critics point out, though, that few landowners could afford to take such a risk, and that Britain still needs to produce its own food."],["E","The estate is now home to one of the largest populations of nightingales in the country, at a time when the species has declined by over 90 per cent elsewhere."],["F","Many of the animals were later sold to farms in other parts of Europe."],["G","This mixture of open ground, scrub and water proved to be exactly what many insects and birds needed."]],"a":["B","A","G","E","C","D"],"tg":[["coherence"],["reference"],["reference"],["coherence"],["coherence"],["advanced-linkers"]],"e":["Mali çöküş ve yorgun toprak → \"Faced with this situation ... decision\": B; sonraki paragraf \"The idea was simple\".","Hayvanlar tanıtıldı → \"These animals turned out to be essential\": A.","Çalılar, ağaçlar, göletler → \"This mixture of open ground, scrub and water\": G.","Nadir türler geri döndü → bülbül nüfusu: E.","Çiftçiler şikâyet etti → bazıları için \"messy\" manzara ihanet: C.","Bugün model örnek → \"Critics point out, though\": D; ardından \"Supporters argue\". F fazladır."]},{"k":"R8","nm":"Okuma 8 · Çoklu eşleştirme","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 8","ins":"Metin bölümlerini (A–E) oku. Her soru için doğru bölümü seç. Bölümler birden fazla kez seçilebilir.","ty":"match","title":"Five people describe a decision that changed their career","opts":[["A","Nadia","I spent ten years as a lawyer before admitting to myself that I dreaded going to work. Leaving was terrifying – I had a mortgage and two children – but I retrained as a primary school teacher. My income halved, and some of my former colleagues clearly thought I had lost my mind. I have never regretted it for a second."],["B","Oliver","My move into journalism happened almost by chance. I had written a few blog posts about local politics, and an editor happened to read one. She offered me a trial, and I simply said yes without thinking too hard about it. If I had thought it through, I probably would have turned it down, as I had no training at all."],["C","Priya","After my daughter was born, I wanted to work fewer hours, and my employer refused. So I set up my own consultancy. The first year was extremely tough, and there were months when I earned almost nothing. What got me through was a network of other self-employed parents who shared advice and, when necessary, childcare."],["D","Marcus","I turned down a promotion that would have meant moving to head office in another city. Everyone told me I was making a mistake. But I knew the job would leave me no time for the things I care about outside work. Ironically, three years later, I was offered an even better position without having to move."],["E","Lena","I had always been fascinated by medicine but assumed I was too old to start at thirty-five. A friend who had done exactly that persuaded me otherwise. The training was exhausting, and I often felt out of place among students half my age. But my previous experience in management turned out to be surprisingly useful."]],"q":[{"q":"Who was influenced by someone who had made a similar change?","a":"E","tg":["detail"],"e":"Lena: aynısını yapmış bir arkadaşı onu ikna etti."},{"q":"Who says that former colleagues thought their decision was foolish?","a":"A","tg":["detail"],"e":"Nadia: eski meslektaşları aklını kaçırdığını düşündü."},{"q":"Who was rewarded later for not accepting an offer?","a":"D","tg":["inference"],"e":"Marcus terfiyi reddetti; üç yıl sonra taşınmadan daha iyi bir teklif aldı."},{"q":"Who received practical support from people in a similar situation?","a":"C","tg":["detail"],"e":"Priya: serbest çalışan diğer ebeveynlerden destek."},{"q":"Who admits that they made a decision without careful consideration?","a":"B","tg":["third-conditional"],"e":"Oliver: düşünmeden evet dedi; düşünseydi reddederdi."},{"q":"Who found that skills from a previous job were valuable?","a":"E","tg":["detail"],"e":"Lena: yöneticilik deneyimi işe yaradı."},{"q":"Who accepted a significant drop in earnings?","a":"A","tg":["detail"],"e":"Nadia: geliri yarıya düştü."},{"q":"Who changed career because of a conflict with an employer?","a":"C","tg":["detail"],"e":"Priya: işvereni daha az çalışmasına izin vermedi."},{"q":"Who mentions feeling different from the people around them?","a":"E","tg":["detail"],"e":"Lena: yaşının yarısındaki öğrenciler arasında yabancı hissetti."},{"q":"Who says they have no doubts that they made the right choice?","a":"A","tg":["attitude"],"e":"Nadia: \"I have never regretted it for a second\"."}]}]},"L":{"min":40,"parts":[{"k":"L1","nm":"Dinleme 1 · Kısa kayıtlar","ti":"Listening Part 1","ins":"Üç farklı kısa kayıt dinleyeceksin; her biri için iki soru var. En uygun cevabı seç.","ty":"mc","q":[{"id":"l1a","sc":[["M","So you've finally finished the book?"],["F","Yes, after four years. The research was the enjoyable part – I loved spending weeks in archives. The writing itself was agony. I rewrote the first chapter about fifteen times."],["M","And now?"],["F","Now I'm terrified about the reviews. I keep telling myself it doesn't matter, but of course it does."]],"q":"You hear two friends talking about a book. What did the woman enjoy most about the process?","o":["writing the opening","investigating the subject","discussing it with others"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Araştırma kısmı keyifliydi; yazmak işkenceydi."},{"id":"l1b","sc":[["M","So you've finally finished the book?"],["F","Yes, after four years. The research was the enjoyable part – I loved spending weeks in archives. The writing itself was agony. I rewrote the first chapter about fifteen times."],["M","And now?"],["F","Now I'm terrified about the reviews. I keep telling myself it doesn't matter, but of course it does."]],"q":"How does she feel now that the book is finished?","o":["anxious about how it will be received","relieved that she can rest","eager to start work on her next book straight away"],"a":0,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"\"I'm terrified about the reviews\"."},{"id":"l1c","sc":[["F","The new exhibition is a brave choice for a museum that usually plays it safe. Putting contemporary pieces next to centuries-old paintings forces you to look at both differently. Admittedly, a few of the combinations feel forced. But overall I left feeling I'd seen familiar works with fresh eyes."]],"q":"You hear a critic talking about an exhibition. What does she say about it?","o":["It is too traditional.","It gives a new perspective on well-known works.","It contains too many modern pieces next to the old ones."],"a":1,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"\"I'd seen familiar works with fresh eyes\"."},{"id":"l1d","sc":[["F","The new exhibition is a brave choice for a museum that usually plays it safe. Putting contemporary pieces next to centuries-old paintings forces you to look at both differently. Admittedly, a few of the combinations feel forced. But overall I left feeling I'd seen familiar works with fresh eyes."]],"q":"What criticism does she make?","o":["Some pairings of works do not seem natural.","The museum has taken too many risks with its collection.","The older paintings are poorly displayed."],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"a few of the combinations feel forced\"."},{"id":"l1e","sc":[["M","I was sceptical when the company introduced flexible hours. I assumed people would take advantage. In fact, the opposite happened – people started working more efficiently because they felt trusted. My only concern now is that it's harder to get everyone together for meetings."]],"q":"You hear a manager talking about flexible working. Why did his opinion change?","o":["Staff became more productive.","Staff asked for more meetings.","It saved the company money."],"a":0,"tg":["inference"],"e":"İnsanlar güvenildiklerini hissedip daha verimli çalıştı."},{"id":"l1f","sc":[["M","I was sceptical when the company introduced flexible hours. I assumed people would take advantage. In fact, the opposite happened – people started working more efficiently because they felt trusted. My only concern now is that it's harder to get everyone together for meetings."]],"q":"What problem does he mention?","o":["Some staff work too many hours.","Arranging meetings is more difficult.","Customers find it harder to contact staff."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Herkesi toplantıda bir araya getirmek zorlaştı."}]},{"k":"L2","nm":"Dinleme 2 · Cümle tamamlama","ti":"Listening Part 2","ins":"Bir konuşma dinleyeceksin. Cümleleri bir kelime ya da kısa bir ifadeyle tamamla.","ty":"open","id":"l2","title":"Cümleler","sc":[["F","Hello everyone. I'm a marine biologist, and for the last six years I've been studying octopuses off the coast of Portugal. People often ask why I chose octopuses rather than dolphins or whales. Partly it was practical – octopuses live in shallow water, so they're easier to study. But mainly it's because they're so unlike us, and yet clearly intelligent. An octopus has around five hundred million neurons, and two thirds of them are in its arms rather than its head. This means each arm can, in a sense, make its own decisions. One of the things that surprised me most was how individual they are. Some are bold and curious, others extremely shy. I gave each of the octopuses I studied a name, which some colleagues find unscientific. One of them, whom I called Lola, learned to recognise me and would approach my diving mask. Octopuses are famous for escaping from aquariums. In one case, an octopus repeatedly turned off a light in its tank by squirting water at it. Sadly, octopuses have very short lives – most species live only one to two years. That's one of the great mysteries: why would evolution produce such a clever animal that lives so briefly? My current research focuses on how octopuses sleep. We've found evidence that they may even dream, as their skin changes colour during certain stages of sleep."]],"p":"The speaker chose octopuses partly because they live in (1) ______ water.\nAbout two thirds of an octopus's neurons are located in its (2) ______.\nShe was surprised by the different (3) ______ of individual octopuses.\nSome colleagues think giving octopuses names is (4) ______.\nOne octopus learned to recognise the speaker's (5) ______.\nAn octopus in an aquarium turned off a (6) ______ using water.\nMost octopuses live for between one and (7) ______ years.\nDuring sleep, an octopus's (8) ______ may change colour.","a":[["shallow"],["arms"],["personalities","characters","personality"],["unscientific"],["diving mask","mask"],["light"],["two","2"],["skin"]],"tg":[["detail"],["detail"],["inference"],["detail"],["detail"],["detail"],["spelling"],["detail"]],"e":["\"they live in shallow water\".","\"two thirds of them are in its arms\".","\"how individual they are. Some are bold ..., others extremely shy\" → kişilik.","\"which some colleagues find unscientific\".","\"would approach my diving mask\".","\"turned off a light in its tank\".","\"one to two years\".","\"their skin changes colour\"."]},{"k":"L3","nm":"Dinleme 3 · Röportaj","ti":"Listening Part 3","ins":"Bir röportaj dinleyeceksin. Her soru için en uygun cevabı seç.","ty":"mc","id":"l3","sc":[["M","My guest today is Dr Helen Park, an architect who specialises in designing schools. Helen, why schools?"],["F","I started out designing offices, and I found it rather unfulfilling. Then I was asked to work on a school extension, and I was struck by how little thought had gone into the existing building. Children were spending seven hours a day in rooms with poor light and terrible acoustics. I realised design could make a real difference to how they learn."],["M","What do architects most often get wrong?"],["F","We tend to design for how a building looks rather than how it will be used. A glass atrium may win awards, but if it's too hot in summer and too noisy all year, it's a failure. I now spend weeks in schools before I draw anything, watching how students and teachers actually move around."],["M","Is there evidence that design affects learning?"],["F","There is, though people sometimes overstate it. A good building won't turn a poor school into a great one. But studies do show that natural light, fresh air and the ability to control temperature have measurable effects on concentration. Those are relatively cheap to get right, which makes it all the more frustrating when they're ignored."],["M","How involved are students in your designs?"],["F","Very. I used to consult them as a formality, if I'm honest. But a group of eleven-year-olds once pointed out that the toilets I'd planned were too far from the playground. It seemed trivial, but it would have caused chaos at break times. Since then, I take their views far more seriously."],["M","What's the biggest challenge facing school design now?"],["F","Budgets, without doubt. Governments want buildings that are cheap to construct, but cheap buildings are often expensive to run. A bit more spent on insulation at the start saves enormous amounts on heating later. Persuading people to think long-term is the hardest part of my job."]],"q":[{"q":"Why did Helen start designing schools?","o":["She was bored with her previous work.","She had always been more interested in education than offices.","She realised existing schools were poorly designed.","She was offered a large contract."],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Mevcut binaya ne kadar az özen gösterildiğini fark etti; tasarımın fark yaratabileceğini anladı."},{"q":"What does Helen say architects often get wrong?","o":["They ignore the cost of buildings.","They focus on appearance rather than use.","They rely too much on glass.","They do not spend enough time consulting teachers."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"design for how a building looks rather than how it will be used\"."},{"q":"What is Helen's view of the research on design and learning?","o":["It shows that design is the main factor in school success.","Its findings are sometimes exaggerated.","It has not produced any clear results.","It focuses too much on expensive features."],"a":1,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"\"though people sometimes overstate it\"."},{"q":"Why does Helen feel frustrated?","o":["Simple improvements are often not made.","Schools cannot afford fresh air systems.","Teachers do not control the temperature.","Studies are ignored by governments."],"a":0,"tg":["inference"],"e":"Nispeten ucuz iyileştirmeler bile göz ardı ediliyor."},{"q":"What does the story about the toilets illustrate?","o":["Children often complain about small things.","Helen had made a serious error in a design.","Students' opinions can be genuinely useful.","Break times are difficult to manage."],"a":2,"tg":["purpose"],"e":"Bu olaydan sonra öğrencilerin görüşlerini çok daha ciddiye alıyor."},{"q":"What does Helen find most difficult about her job?","o":["working with small budgets","convincing people to consider future costs","designing efficient heating systems","meeting strict government regulations on safety"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"Persuading people to think long-term is the hardest part\"."}]},{"k":"L4","nm":"Dinleme 4 · Eşleştirme (görev 1)","ti":"Listening Part 4","ins":"Beş kişinin konuşmasını dinleyeceksin. Görev 1: Her konuşmacının bu işi seçme nedenini (A–H) bul.","ty":"match","id":"l4","sc":[["M","Speaker one. I became a paramedic because, honestly, I needed a job where every day was different. I'd tried office work and I was climbing the walls. What I find hardest isn't the emergencies themselves – you're trained for those – it's the paperwork afterwards. Hours of it."],["F","#1.2"],["F","Speaker two. I'd always been good with numbers, and my careers adviser at school suggested accountancy. I followed the advice without much thought. It's a decent career, but what I struggle with is the isolation. Some weeks I barely speak to anyone except my computer."],["M","#1.2"],["M2","Speaker three. My father was a carpenter, and I grew up in his workshop, so becoming one myself felt natural – it's in the family. The hardest part is the physical toll. At forty, my back already tells me when I've worked too long."],["F","#1.2"],["F2","Speaker four. I went into nursing after my mother was seriously ill. The nurses who looked after her were so kind, and I wanted to give something back. What I didn't anticipate was how hard it would be emotionally – you can't leave patients' problems at the hospital door."],["M","#1.2"],["M","Speaker five. To be honest, I became a pilot for the travel. I wanted to see the world, and this seemed the best way to get paid for it. The irony is that I see almost nothing of the places I fly to. The irregular sleep is the real killer, though – my body never knows what time it is."]],"opts":[["A","wanted variety in their work"],["B","followed a family tradition"],["C","was inspired by a personal experience"],["D","wanted to travel"],["E","was attracted by the salary"],["F","took someone's advice"],["G","wanted to work outdoors"],["H","wanted to be their own boss"]],"q":[{"q":"Speaker 1","a":"A","tg":["gist"],"e":"\"a job where every day was different\"."},{"q":"Speaker 2","a":"F","tg":["gist"],"e":"Kariyer danışmanının tavsiyesine uydu."},{"q":"Speaker 3","a":"B","tg":["gist"],"e":"Babası marangozdu: \"it's in the family\"."},{"q":"Speaker 4","a":"C","tg":["gist"],"e":"Annesinin hastalığı ve hemşirelerin şefkati."},{"q":"Speaker 5","a":"D","tg":["gist"],"e":"\"I became a pilot for the travel\"."}]},{"k":"L4","nm":"Dinleme 4 · Eşleştirme (görev 2)","ti":"Listening Part 4","ins":"Aynı kaydı kullan. Görev 2: Her konuşmacının işinde en zor bulduğu şeyi (A–H) bul.","ty":"match","id":"l4","noaud":true,"sc":[["M","Speaker one. I became a paramedic because, honestly, I needed a job where every day was different. I'd tried office work and I was climbing the walls. What I find hardest isn't the emergencies themselves – you're trained for those – it's the paperwork afterwards. Hours of it."],["F","#1.2"],["F","Speaker two. I'd always been good with numbers, and my careers adviser at school suggested accountancy. I followed the advice without much thought. It's a decent career, but what I struggle with is the isolation. Some weeks I barely speak to anyone except my computer."],["M","#1.2"],["M2","Speaker three. My father was a carpenter, and I grew up in his workshop, so becoming one myself felt natural – it's in the family. The hardest part is the physical toll. At forty, my back already tells me when I've worked too long."],["F","#1.2"],["F2","Speaker four. I went into nursing after my mother was seriously ill. The nurses who looked after her were so kind, and I wanted to give something back. What I didn't anticipate was how hard it would be emotionally – you can't leave patients' problems at the hospital door."],["M","#1.2"],["M","Speaker five. To be honest, I became a pilot for the travel. I wanted to see the world, and this seemed the best way to get paid for it. The irony is that I see almost nothing of the places I fly to. The irregular sleep is the real killer, though – my body never knows what time it is."]],"opts":[["A","the administrative work"],["B","the emotional pressure"],["C","the long journeys"],["D","the lack of contact with people"],["E","the physical demands"],["F","the low pay"],["G","the disrupted sleep pattern"],["H","dealing with difficult customers"]],"q":[{"q":"Speaker 1","a":"A","tg":["detail"],"e":"Acil durumlar değil, sonrasındaki evrak işi."},{"q":"Speaker 2","a":"D","tg":["detail"],"e":"\"the isolation\" – kimseyle konuşmamak."},{"q":"Speaker 3","a":"E","tg":["detail"],"e":"\"the physical toll\" – sırtı ağrıyor."},{"q":"Speaker 4","a":"B","tg":["detail"],"e":"\"how hard it would be emotionally\"."},{"q":"Speaker 5","a":"G","tg":["detail"],"e":"\"The irregular sleep is the real killer\"."}]}]},"W":{"min":90,"tasks":[{"id":"c1x-w1","part":1,"kind":"essay","t":"Part 1 · Deneme: Şehir merkezlerinde araçlar","in":"Your class has attended a panel discussion on how to make city centres better places to live. You have made the notes below. Write an essay discussing TWO of the methods in your notes. You should explain which method you think is more effective, giving reasons in support of your answer. You may, if you wish, make use of the opinions expressed in the discussion, but you should use your own words as far as possible. Write 220–260 words.","mail":"Methods for improving city centres:\n• banning private cars\n• creating more green spaces\n• encouraging people to live in the centre\n\nSome opinions expressed in the discussion:\n\"Without cars, shops in the centre will lose customers.\"\n\"Parks make people want to spend time in a city.\"\n\"City centres are empty and unsafe at night because nobody lives there.\"","pts":[["İlk yöntemi tartış",["car","traffic","vehicle","ban","pedestrian","green space","park","live in the centre","residents"]],["İkinci yöntemi tartış",["whereas","on the other hand","similarly","second","another method","by contrast","turning to"]],["Hangisinin daha etkili olduğunu söyle ve gerekçelendir",["more effective","most effective","in my view","i would argue","on balance","ultimately"]],["Akademik bağlaçlar kullan",["however","furthermore","moreover","nevertheless","consequently","in conclusion"]]],"min":220,"max":260,"model":"Many city centres have become places that people pass through rather than places where they want to spend time. Of the possible solutions, banning private cars and creating more green spaces are two of the most frequently discussed.\n\nRemoving cars from the centre has obvious benefits. Air quality improves, streets become quieter, and pedestrians can move around safely. However, opponents argue that shops will lose customers who are used to driving into town. This concern is not unreasonable, but evidence from cities such as Ghent and Pontevedra suggests that footfall often increases once streets are pedestrianised, provided that good public transport is available.\n\nCreating more green spaces addresses a different problem. Parks and tree-lined squares make a city more attractive, encouraging people to linger, meet friends and support local businesses. They also help to reduce temperatures during increasingly frequent heatwaves. The difficulty is that space in city centres is scarce and expensive, so new parks are often small or located on the edges.\n\nOn balance, I would argue that banning private cars is the more effective measure, largely because it makes the second possible. Once roads and car parks are no longer needed, they can be turned into green spaces, cycle lanes and outdoor cafés. In other words, reducing traffic does not simply remove a problem; it creates opportunities. Nevertheless, such a ban must be introduced gradually and accompanied by investment in public transport, or it risks harming the very businesses it is meant to help.","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı yöntemleri seçerek","City centres in many countries are facing a crisis. As shopping moves online and offices empty, once-busy streets are becoming lifeless, particularly after dark. Two possible remedies are creating more green spaces and encouraging people to live in the centre.\n\nGreen spaces have obvious appeal. Parks, tree-lined streets and small gardens make cities more attractive and healthier places to be, offering shade in summer and somewhere to relax during the working day. As one speaker in the discussion pointed out, parks give people a reason to spend time in a city rather than simply passing through it. However, parks alone cannot solve the problem of empty streets in the evening; once offices close, even the most beautiful square can feel deserted.\n\nEncouraging people to live in the centre addresses this issue more directly. Residents create a constant presence, supporting local shops, cafés and services at all hours, and their presence naturally makes streets feel safer. Many cities have successfully converted unused offices into flats, bringing new life to areas that had been empty for years. The main obstacle is cost, as central housing is often far more expensive than in the suburbs.\n\nIn my view, encouraging residential use is the more effective approach. Green spaces improve the quality of a city centre, but people bring it to life. Ideally, the two should go hand in hand: new housing projects should include parks and gardens, ensuring that city centres become not only busier but also more pleasant places to live."],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (C2'ye yakın)","The decline of the traditional city centre has been one of the more striking urban trends of recent decades. Among the remedies proposed, banning private cars and repopulating central areas with residents have attracted particular attention, and they merit careful comparison.\n\nA ban on private vehicles promises cleaner air, quieter streets and safer conditions for pedestrians and cyclists. Its critics, notably local retailers, fear that customers will simply take their custom elsewhere. This concern, while understandable, has not generally been borne out where such schemes have been implemented thoughtfully; on the contrary, pedestrianised streets often see an increase in footfall, as people linger rather than merely drive through.\n\nRepopulating city centres, by contrast, tackles the root of the problem: the absence of everyday life outside working hours. As one participant in the discussion observed, centres feel empty and unsafe at night precisely because nobody lives there. Converting redundant office blocks into homes would sustain local businesses, revive evening activity and reduce the need for long commutes. The difficulty lies in ensuring that such housing is affordable, rather than becoming the preserve of the wealthy.\n\nOn balance, I consider repopulation the more effective long-term strategy, since it creates the conditions in which a car-free centre could thrive. A street without cars but also without residents risks remaining just as lifeless as before. Policymakers would therefore be wise to prioritise housing, introducing traffic restrictions progressively as the resident population grows."]]},{"choice":[{"id":"c1x-w2","part":2,"kind":"proposal","t":"Part 2 · Öneri: Öğrenci kafeteryası","in":"The principal of your college has asked students to submit proposals for improving the college canteen. Write a proposal describing the current problems, suggesting improvements and explaining how these would benefit students. Write 220–260 words.","mail":"PROPOSALS INVITED\nHow can we improve our canteen?\nThe college has a small budget available to improve the canteen. Describe the main problems, suggest how the money should be spent, and explain why your suggestions would benefit students.","pts":[["Başlıklar kullan (proposal biçimi)",["introduction","current situation","recommendations","conclusion","purpose"]],["Sorunları anlat",["problem","currently","at present","queue","limited","crowded"]],["Öneriler sun",["recommend","suggest","propose","should","could"]],["Faydaları açıkla",["benefit","would allow","would encourage","as a result","improve"]]],"min":220,"max":260,"model":"Proposal for improving the college canteen\n\nIntroduction\nThe purpose of this proposal is to outline the main problems with the current canteen and to recommend how the available budget could be used to address them. It is based on a short survey of sixty students.\n\nCurrent situation\nThe most common complaint concerns the queues at lunchtime, which often take twenty minutes. Since the lunch break lasts only forty-five minutes, many students either skip lunch or eat in a hurry. In addition, the menu offers few healthy or vegetarian options, and the seating area is cramped and noisy, which makes it an unappealing place to relax.\n\nRecommendations\nFirstly, I recommend installing a second serving counter offering pre-prepared items such as salads and sandwiches. This would significantly reduce waiting times. Secondly, the menu could be expanded to include at least two vegetarian dishes each day, ideally chosen with input from students. Finally, a small part of the budget could be spent on replacing some of the large tables with smaller ones and adding plants and sound-absorbing panels.\n\nBenefits\nThese changes would allow students to eat properly during the break, which would in turn improve their concentration in afternoon classes. A more varied menu would encourage healthier eating habits, while a more pleasant environment would make the canteen a place where students choose to spend time and socialise.\n\nConclusion\nWith relatively modest investment, the canteen could become a far more efficient and welcoming space. I strongly recommend that these proposals be considered.","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı önerilerle","Proposal: improving the college canteen\n\nIntroduction\nThis proposal identifies the main weaknesses of the college canteen and suggests how the available budget could best be used. It draws on comments from students in three different year groups.\n\nThe current situation\nAlthough the food itself is generally considered acceptable, the canteen suffers from several practical problems. There are no facilities for students who bring their own lunch, such as microwaves, and drinking water is only available if bought in plastic bottles. In addition, the canteen closes at 2 p.m., leaving students with afternoon classes nowhere to buy a snack.\n\nSuggestions\nI would recommend spending part of the budget on two or three microwaves and a free water fountain. These are relatively inexpensive but would make a significant difference to students' daily lives. I would also suggest extending opening hours until 4 p.m., perhaps offering a smaller menu of sandwiches and fruit in the afternoon. Finally, introducing a monthly \"student menu day\", when dishes suggested by students are served, would encourage greater involvement.\n\nBenefits\nThese measures would save students money, reduce plastic waste and ensure that everyone has access to food throughout the day. Involving students in menu choices would also make the canteen feel more like a shared space.\n\nConclusion\nWith modest investment, the canteen could meet the needs of a far wider range of students. I hope the principal will consider these suggestions."],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (C2'ye yakın)","Proposal for a more sustainable and welcoming canteen\n\nPurpose\nThe aim of this proposal is to recommend improvements to the college canteen that would not only address students' immediate concerns but also reflect the college's stated commitment to sustainability.\n\nKey issues\nFeedback gathered from the student council highlights three recurring complaints. Firstly, the menu relies heavily on processed food, with fresh options often sold out by midday. Secondly, the amount of single-use packaging is considerable, sitting uncomfortably with the college's environmental policy. Thirdly, the canteen is perceived as a place to eat quickly rather than somewhere students would choose to spend time.\n\nRecommendations\nA proportion of the budget could be allocated to forming a partnership with a nearby farm or market, ensuring a regular supply of seasonal produce at reasonable cost. Replacing disposable cups and containers with reusable alternatives, perhaps offered at a small deposit, would substantially reduce waste. Lastly, rearranging the seating to include a quieter area with plants and natural light would encourage students to linger and socialise.\n\nAnticipated benefits\nHealthier meals would support students' concentration and well-being, while a visible reduction in waste would strengthen the college's credibility on environmental issues. A more inviting space, meanwhile, could foster a stronger sense of community across year groups.\n\nConclusion\nThese changes are achievable within the current budget and would bring benefits extending well beyond lunchtime. I would strongly encourage the principal to consider them."]]},{"id":"c1x-w3","part":2,"kind":"review","t":"Part 2 · İnceleme: Bir belgesel","in":"A website is inviting readers to submit reviews of documentaries that have changed the way they think about an issue. Write a review describing the documentary, explaining how it changed your thinking and saying whether you would recommend it. Write 220–260 words.","mail":"REVIEWS WANTED\nA documentary that changed my mind\nTell us about a documentary that made you see an issue differently. What was it about? How did it change your thinking? Would you recommend it to others?","pts":[["Belgeseli tanıt",["documentary","follows","explores","about","director"]],["Düşüncenin nasıl değiştiğini açıkla",["used to think","changed","realised","made me","before watching"]],["Güçlü / zayıf yönleri değerlendir",["however","although","powerful","weakness","strength","criticism"]],["Tavsiye et",["recommend","worth","anyone who","must-see"]]],"min":220,"max":260,"model":"The hidden cost of fast fashion\n\nBefore watching \"Threads\", I considered myself a fairly responsible consumer. I recycled, I avoided plastic bags, and I rarely gave a second thought to the clothes I bought. This quietly devastating documentary changed that completely.\n\nThe film follows a single cotton T-shirt from a field in Central Asia, through a factory in Bangladesh, to a shop window in London and, eventually, to a mountain of discarded clothing in Ghana. Rather than lecturing the audience, the director lets the people involved tell their own stories: a farmer struggling with polluted water, a seamstress working fourteen-hour days, a trader who sorts through bales of unwanted clothes from Europe.\n\nWhat struck me most was not any single shocking fact, but the cumulative effect. By the end, I could no longer see a cheap T-shirt as a bargain; I saw it as a chain of decisions whose consequences fall on people I will never meet. I have since cut down drastically on the clothes I buy and started repairing rather than replacing.\n\nIf the film has a weakness, it is that it offers few practical solutions, leaving viewers feeling somewhat powerless. A short final section on what individuals and governments can do would have made it even more effective.\n\nNevertheless, I would recommend \"Threads\" to anyone who buys clothes – which is to say, everyone. It is beautifully filmed, deeply human and, unlike many documentaries on the subject, never preachy. You may not look at your wardrobe in the same way again.","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı bir belgesel","The ocean in a single bottle\n\nI had always assumed that plastic pollution was a problem caused by careless people dropping litter on beaches. \"Blue Silence\", a documentary following a team of marine scientists across the Pacific, showed me how naive that view was.\n\nThe film traces the journey of plastic from our kitchens to the most remote parts of the ocean, where it breaks down into tiny particles that are eaten by fish and, eventually, by us. Its most memorable sequence takes place on an uninhabited island, where the beaches are covered in rubbish that has drifted thousands of kilometres.\n\nWhat changed my thinking was realising that recycling, which I had always considered the solution, is only a small part of the answer. Much of what we put in recycling bins is never actually recycled. The documentary convinced me that reducing the amount of plastic we use in the first place is far more important, and since watching it, I have changed many of my shopping habits.\n\nThe film is not perfect. At times the dramatic music feels manipulative, and some of the statistics are presented too quickly to absorb. Nonetheless, I would recommend \"Blue Silence\" to anyone who cares about the environment, and particularly to those who, like me, believe they are already doing enough. It is uncomfortable viewing, but that is precisely why it matters."],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (C2'ye yakın)","Rethinking old age: \"The Long Afternoon\"\n\nFew documentaries manage to overturn assumptions so gently, yet so thoroughly, as \"The Long Afternoon\", which follows five residents of a care home in northern Italy over the course of a year.\n\nI went into the film expecting a melancholy portrait of decline. What I found instead was a story of curiosity and reinvention. One resident, a former factory worker in her nineties, learns to paint for the first time; another, once a strict headmaster, forms an unlikely friendship with a teenage volunteer. The director never intrudes, allowing long, unhurried scenes to reveal character rather than imposing a narrative.\n\nThe film challenged my rather lazy assumption that old age is primarily a period of loss. It made me realise how often we speak about older people rather than listening to them, and how much our society loses as a result. I have since started visiting my own grandfather more regularly, not out of duty but out of genuine interest in his stories.\n\nIf the film has a flaw, it is that it largely avoids the harder realities of ageing, such as illness and loneliness, which some viewers may find evasive.\n\nEven so, I would recommend \"The Long Afternoon\" without hesitation, particularly to younger viewers. It is quietly moving, often funny and, above all, a powerful reminder that a life is never simply over."]]}]}]},"S":{"parts":[{"id":"c1x-s1","p":1,"t":"Part 1 · Tanışma","ins":"Sınav görevlisi seninle ilgili sorular soruyor. Doğal, gelişmiş ve örnekli cevaplar ver.","q":[["What do you find most rewarding about your studies or work at the moment?",30,["what I find most rewarding","I'd say","particularly"],["I'd say what I find most rewarding is the feeling of finally understanding something complex. I'm studying economics, and there's a particular satisfaction when a theory that seemed abstract suddenly makes sense of something in the news. It makes all the hours of reading feel worthwhile.","I work as a volunteer teacher at the moment, and the most rewarding thing is seeing students' confidence grow. Some of them barely spoke at the beginning of the year, and now they're happily debating with each other. That kind of progress means far more to me than any grade."]],["If you could live in any period of history, which would you choose and why?",40,["I'd be tempted","I'd have to say","largely because","having said that"],["I'd be tempted to choose the late nineteenth century, largely because so many inventions we take for granted, like electricity, the telephone and photography, were appearing for the first time. It must have been an extraordinary time to be alive. Having said that, I'm not sure I'd cope without modern medicine, and the working conditions for most people were appalling.","I'd choose Istanbul in the sixteenth century, at the height of the Ottoman Empire. The city must have been an incredible meeting point of cultures, languages and trade, and the architecture being built then still amazes people today. Of course, as an ordinary person, life would probably have been far less glamorous than I imagine."]],["How do you usually make important decisions?",30,["I tend to","weigh up","gut feeling","in the end"],["I tend to weigh up the pros and cons quite carefully, sometimes even writing them down. I also like to talk things through with people I trust. But in the end, I often find myself going with my gut feeling anyway – the list mostly helps me understand why I feel the way I do.","I'm quite impulsive, to be honest. If something feels right, I usually go for it without overthinking. That approach has led to a few mistakes, but also to some of my best experiences, like moving abroad for a year on fairly short notice."]]]},{"id":"c1x-s2","p":2,"t":"Part 2 · Fotoğraflar: zor koşullarda çalışmak","ins":"Üç fotoğraftan ikisini seç ve yaklaşık 1 dakika karşılaştır. Soruların ikisini de cevapla.","img":[["🚒🔥🧑‍🚒","Fotoğraf 1: Bir yangına müdahale eden itfaiyeciler.","c1-fire"],["🏔️🧗‍♀️❄️","Fotoğraf 2: Karlı bir dağda kurtarma ekibi.","c1-rescue"],["🏥🩺🌙","Fotoğraf 3: Gece vardiyasında yoğun bir acil serviste çalışan doktor.","c1-doctor"]],"q":[["Look at the pictures. They show people working in demanding situations. Compare two of the pictures and say what skills these people need, and how they might be feeling.",60,["whereas","both","they would need","I'd imagine","under pressure","judging by"],["I'll compare the firefighters and the doctor in the emergency department. Both are clearly working under enormous pressure, where mistakes could cost lives. The firefighters need physical strength and courage, and above all the ability to work as a team, since they rely on each other completely in a dangerous situation. The doctor, by contrast, needs to make rapid decisions while staying calm and focused, often after hours without sleep, and she also needs to communicate clearly with patients who may be frightened. I'd imagine the firefighters are feeling a rush of adrenaline, perhaps mixed with fear, whereas the doctor might be experiencing a kind of quiet exhaustion, judging by the fact that it's the middle of the night.","I'd like to compare the mountain rescue team and the firefighters. Both groups are working in hostile, unpredictable environments, and both need to be extremely fit. The rescue team, though, also needs specialist knowledge of climbing and navigation, as well as the patience to search for hours in freezing conditions. The firefighters have to act much faster, assessing risks in seconds. In terms of feelings, I imagine the rescuers might be anxious, especially if they haven't found the missing person yet and the weather is getting worse. The firefighters are probably highly focused, almost on autopilot, relying on their training, although they must be aware of the danger to their own lives."]],["Which of these jobs do you think is the most stressful? Why?",30,["I'd say","arguably","because"],["I'd say the doctor's, arguably, because the pressure never really stops. A fire eventually ends, but an emergency department is busy every single night, and doctors have to deal with the emotional weight of patients who don't survive.","I'd go for the firefighters, because they're putting their own lives at risk every time they go to work. Stress isn't just about workload; knowing you might not come home must be incredibly difficult for them and their families."]]]},{"id":"c1x-s3","p":3,"t":"Part 3 · Birlikte karar verme","ins":"Gerçek sınavda partnerle yapılır. Önce seçenekleri tartış (2 dakika), sonra karar ver (1 dakika).","opts":["📱 social media","📰 traditional newspapers","👨‍👩‍👧 family","🎓 teachers","🎬 celebrities"],"q":[["Here are some things that can influence young people's opinions. Talk together about how much influence these things have on young people's opinions today.",120,["I'd argue","to a certain extent","that's a fair point","it could be argued","whereas","undoubtedly"],["I'd argue social media is undoubtedly the biggest influence today, simply because young people spend so much time on it, and algorithms tend to show them more of what they already believe, which can reinforce their opinions without them realising. Traditional newspapers, by contrast, have much less influence than they once did, although their content is often shared online, so they still shape debates indirectly. Family is still important, especially on values and on issues like religion or politics, but perhaps less so on specific topics like technology or the environment, where young people often know more than their parents. Teachers can have a profound influence on some students, particularly in encouraging them to question things and look at evidence. And celebrities – to a certain extent they influence fashion and lifestyle choices, but I'm not sure young people take their political views very seriously, unless the celebrity has real expertise. One thing all these influences have in common, though, is that they're more powerful when they reinforce each other. If a young person hears the same message from their teachers, their family and the people they follow online, it's likely to shape their views quite profoundly. Whereas when these sources contradict each other, young people are forced to think for themselves, which is arguably healthier.","Let me start with family, because I think its influence is often underestimated. Even when teenagers rebel against their parents' views, they're still reacting to them, so family shapes their thinking either way. Teachers matter too, though it really depends on the individual teacher – a passionate history teacher, for example, can change how students see the world. Social media clearly has enormous reach, but I'd say its influence is quite shallow; it affects what young people talk about, but not necessarily what they deeply believe. Traditional newspapers seem almost irrelevant to most of my friends, sadly. As for celebrities, they can raise awareness of causes, such as climate change or mental health, which can be positive, although sometimes it feels more like publicity than genuine commitment. Overall, I'd say the influence of each depends heavily on the individual. A teenager with a close family and inspiring teachers may barely be affected by what celebrities say online, whereas someone who feels isolated may rely on social media for almost everything. That's why it's difficult to generalise, and why media literacy is so important."]],["Now you have about a minute to decide which of these has the most positive influence on young people.",60,["if we had to choose","on balance","I'd go for","even though"],["If we had to choose, I'd go for teachers. Even though their influence is less constant than social media's, it tends to be more thoughtful, and good teachers encourage young people to form their own opinions rather than simply adopting someone else's. They also teach the skills needed to evaluate information critically, which protects young people from the more negative influences on the list. Unlike social media, teachers also have a professional responsibility to be balanced, which makes their influence more trustworthy.","On balance, I'd say family has the most positive influence, at least in most cases. Parents usually have their children's best interests at heart, and the values they pass on – honesty, respect, hard work – tend to last a lifetime. Social media influences change constantly, but family influence is a foundation. Of course, this assumes a supportive family; where that isn't the case, teachers can play a crucial role in filling the gap, which is why I'd put them a close second."]]]},{"id":"c1x-s4","p":4,"t":"Part 4 · Tartışma","ins":"3. bölümün konusuyla ilgili genel sorular. Görüşünü gerekçelerle ve farklı bakış açılarını da değerlendirerek açıkla.","q":[["Do you think young people today are better or worse at thinking critically than previous generations?",45,["it's debatable","on the one hand","by the same token","I suspect"],["It's debatable. On the one hand, they have access to far more information than any previous generation and are used to questioning sources, at least in theory. By the same token, though, the sheer quantity of information can be overwhelming, and I suspect many people rely on headlines or short videos rather than reading in depth. So I'd say young people have the tools to think critically, but not always the habit. Previous generations had fewer sources, but they were perhaps more likely to read a whole article before forming an opinion.","I'd say better, in some ways. Young people today are much more aware of issues like bias and fake news, partly because they've grown up with them. They're also more willing to challenge authority than previous generations. However, critical thinking takes time and patience, and the fast pace of social media doesn't always encourage that. Schools have a role to play in teaching young people to slow down and check before sharing something."]],["Should schools teach students how to identify false information online?",45,["absolutely","it's essential","not least because","in my view"],["Absolutely. In my view, it's as essential today as learning to read, not least because false information can have real consequences, from health decisions to elections. Students should learn practical techniques, like checking the original source, looking for evidence and recognising manipulated images. Ideally, this should be part of every subject rather than a separate lesson. A science teacher could show how to read a study, for example, and a history teacher could compare different sources.","Yes, although I think it's a responsibility shared with parents and the media companies themselves. Schools can teach the basics, but technology changes so quickly that what students learn at fourteen might be outdated by the time they're twenty. So the focus should be on general habits of scepticism rather than specific tricks. Asking who created something and why is a question that will never become outdated."]],["To what extent should people with influence take responsibility for what they say?",45,["to a large extent","that said","there's a balance","freedom of expression"],["To a large extent, I think they should, because their words reach millions of people, many of them young and impressionable. If a celebrity spreads false health advice, for instance, the consequences can be serious. That said, there's a balance to be struck with freedom of expression – we can't expect everyone to be an expert on every topic, and people have the right to share opinions. Perhaps the key is transparency: influencers should make it clear when they're being paid to promote something.","I'd argue that influence brings responsibility, in the same way that we expect journalists to check their facts. Platforms could do more to label misleading content from high-profile accounts. At the same time, audiences have a responsibility too: we shouldn't automatically believe someone just because they're famous. Being critical of the people we admire is difficult, but it's an essential part of being an informed citizen."]]]}]}},"lv":"C1","name":"C1 Advanced"}],"prac":{"A2":{"R":[{"k":"R1","nm":"Okuma 1 · Kısa metinler","ti":"Reading Part 1","ins":"Her kısa metni oku ve metnin ne söylediğini en iyi anlatan seçeneği bul.","id":"a2r1","t":"Shop and School Notices","ty":"mc","q":[{"q":"What does the sign say?","o":["Young children cannot swim here alone.","Adults can only swim with children.","Children under 8 are not allowed in the pool."],"a":0,"tg":["must-have-to-should"],"e":"8 yaşından küçükler bir yetişkinle yüzmeli: tek başına yüzemez.","x":"SWIMMING POOL\nChildren under 8 must swim with an adult."},{"q":"What does Dad tell Jack?","o":["Mr Green will be late.","The lesson starts later than usual.","Mr Green can't teach the lesson today."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Ders 4 yerine 5'te: her zamankinden geç.","x":"Jack,\nYour guitar lesson is at 5 today, not 4. Mr Green phoned. Don't be late!\nDad"},{"q":"What does the shop say?","o":["T-shirts are cheaper this weekend only.","You get a free T-shirt if you buy two.","Shoes are half price this weekend."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"buy two, get one free\": iki alana bir bedava.","x":"SALE\nAll shoes 30% off this weekend.\nT-shirts: buy two, get one free!"},{"q":"Why will the library close early on Friday?","o":["The teachers have a meeting.","The library is getting new books.","Students have a holiday."],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"because of a teachers' meeting\".","x":"To all students:\nThe school library will close at 3 p.m. on Friday because of a teachers' meeting."},{"q":"What does Zoe want Mia to do?","o":["take a camera to the party","help Zoe to fix her broken camera","buy a new camera"],"a":0,"tg":["purpose"],"e":"\"Can you bring your camera\" = partiye fotoğraf makinesini getirmesini istiyor.","x":"Hi Mia,\nCan you bring your camera to the party tomorrow? Mine is broken.\nZoe"}]},{"k":"R2","nm":"Okuma 2 · Kişi eşleştirme","ti":"Reading Part 2","ins":"Üç metni oku. Her soru için doğru kişiyi (A, B ya da C) seç. Bir kişi birden fazla kez seçilebilir.","id":"a2r2","t":"New Schools","ty":"match","pl":"Yazılar","opts":[["A","Can","I moved to a new school in September. It's much bigger than my old one – there are 1,200 students! At first I got lost every day. Now I know my way around, and I've joined the photography club. The lessons are harder here, but my teachers are very helpful."],["B","Sophie","My new school is in a small village, so I take the bus for forty minutes every morning. There are only twelve students in my class, and we all know each other well. I miss my old friends, but we talk online every evening. The best thing here is the school garden – we grow our own vegetables."],["C","Leo","I started at my new school last month. I was nervous on the first day, but a boy called Sam showed me around and now he's my best friend. The school has a great sports hall, and I play basketball there after school. The only problem is lunch – the food isn't very good!"]],"q":[{"q":"Who travels a long way to school?","a":"B","tg":["detail"],"e":"Sophie her sabah 40 dakika otobüsle gidiyor."},{"q":"Who doesn't like the school food?","a":"C","tg":["detail"],"e":"Leo: \"the food isn't very good\"."},{"q":"Who finds the lessons more difficult now?","a":"A","tg":["comparatives-superlatives"],"e":"Can: \"The lessons are harder here\"."},{"q":"Who stays in contact with old friends?","a":"B","tg":["detail"],"e":"Sophie eski arkadaşlarıyla her akşam internetten konuşuyor."},{"q":"Who made a friend on the first day?","a":"C","tg":["detail"],"e":"Sam, Leo'ya okulu gezdirdi ve en iyi arkadaşı oldu."},{"q":"Who had problems finding places in the school?","a":"A","tg":["inference"],"e":"Can: \"At first I got lost every day\"."}]},{"k":"R3","nm":"Okuma 3 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading Part 3","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","id":"a2r3","t":"A Young Swimmer","ty":"mc","title":"A young swimmer","p":["Maya Lee is fourteen and she is one of the best young swimmers in the country. She started swimming when she was five, because her parents wanted her to be safe in the water. She wasn't very good at first, and she was afraid of putting her face in the water.","Now Maya trains six days a week. She gets up at half past five and swims for two hours before school. After school she goes to the gym. \"It's difficult,\" she says, \"but I love the feeling when I win a race.\"","Maya doesn't have much time to see her friends, but they often come to watch her races. Her best friend Hana makes big signs with Maya's name on them. Last year Maya won a silver medal at the national championships. Next year she hopes to swim for her country."],"q":[{"q":"Why did Maya start swimming?","o":["Her friends were in a swimming club.","Her parents wanted her to be safe.","She wanted to win medals."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"because her parents wanted her to be safe in the water\"."},{"q":"What was Maya's problem when she started?","o":["She was afraid of the water.","She didn't like her teacher.","She was too young for lessons."],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Yüzünü suya sokmaktan korkuyordu."},{"q":"What does Maya do before school?","o":["She goes to the gym.","She sleeps until half past seven.","She swims for two hours."],"a":2,"tg":["detail","present-simple"],"e":"5.30'da kalkıp okuldan önce iki saat yüzüyor. Spor salonu okuldan sonra."},{"q":"How does Hana help Maya?","o":["She trains with her.","She makes signs at her races.","She drives her to races at the weekend."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Hana üzerinde Maya'nın adı olan büyük pankartlar yapıyor."},{"q":"What does Maya want to do next year?","o":["win a silver medal","spend more time with friends","swim for her country"],"a":2,"tg":["will-going-to"],"e":"\"Next year she hopes to swim for her country\"."}]},{"k":"R4","nm":"Okuma 4 · Çoktan seçmeli boşluk","ti":"Reading Part 4","ins":"Metni oku ve her boşluk için doğru kelimeyi seç.","id":"a2r4","t":"Coffee","ty":"cloze","title":"Coffee","p":"People (1) ______ coffee for hundreds of years. Today it is one of the (2) ______ popular drinks in the world. Coffee comes from a plant that grows in hot countries, (3) ______ as Brazil and Ethiopia. Brazil produces more coffee (4) ______ any other country. Many people drink coffee in the morning because it helps them (5) ______ awake. But doctors say you (6) ______ drink too much of it.","o":[[["have drunk","drink","are drinking"],0,["present-perfect"]],[["more","most","much"],1,["comparatives-superlatives"]],[["like","such","so"],1,["vocab"]],[["then","that","than"],2,["comparatives-superlatives"]],[["stay","be staying","staying"],0,["vocab"]],[["mustn't","shouldn't","don't have to"],1,["must-have-to-should"]]],"e":["for hundreds of years: geçmişten bugüne süren durum → present perfect.","the most popular: en popüler.","such as: örneğin, gibi.","more ... than: -den daha çok.","help someone (to) do: birine bir şeyi yapmada yardım etmek; stay awake.","Tavsiye: shouldn't (doktorlar söylüyor; yasak değil)."]},{"k":"R5","nm":"Okuma 5 · Açık boşluk","ti":"Reading Part 5","ins":"Metni oku ve her boşluğa TEK kelime yaz.","id":"a2r5","t":"A Message from Camp","ty":"open","one":true,"title":"Hi Dan","p":"Hi Dan,\nI'm writing this message (1) ______ the campsite! We arrived (2) ______ Tuesday. The weather is great and the lake is beautiful. Yesterday we went canoeing and I fell (3) ______ the water! It was very cold. Tomorrow we (4) ______ going to climb the mountain. I hope I won't be too tired. What are you doing this week? Write (5) ______ me soon!\nSee you,\n(6) ______ friend Alex","a":[["from","at"],["on","last"],["into","in"],["are"],["to"],["your"]],"tg":[["prepositions-in-on-at"],["prepositions-in-on-at"],["vocab"],["will-going-to"],["vocab"],["possessives"]],"e":["from/at the campsite: kamp yerinden / kamp yerinde.","Günlerle on: on Tuesday.","fall into the water: suya düşmek.","we are going to: plan.","Write to me: bana yaz.","your friend: arkadaşın."]}],"L":[{"k":"L1","nm":"Dinleme 1 · Kısa konuşmalar","ti":"Listening Part 1","ins":"Beş kısa konuşma dinleyeceksin. Her soru için doğru cevabı seç.","id":"a2l1","t":"Everyday Situations","ty":"mc","q":[{"id":"a2l1a","sc":[["M","How much are these apples?"],["F","They're two pounds a kilo. But the green ones are cheaper – one pound fifty."],["M","I'll have a kilo of the green ones, please."]],"q":"How much will the man pay?","o":["£2.00","£1.50","£3.50"],"a":1,"tg":["spelling"],"e":"Yeşil elmalar kilosu 1,50."},{"id":"a2l1b","sc":[["F","What did you do on Saturday?"],["M","I wanted to go to the beach, but it was raining. So I stayed at home and cleaned my room."]],"q":"What did the boy do on Saturday?","o":["He went to the beach.","He cleaned his room.","He went shopping."],"a":1,"tg":["past-simple"],"e":"Yağmur yüzünden evde kalıp odasını temizledi."},{"id":"a2l1c","sc":[["M","Excuse me, where's the post office?"],["F","Go straight on, then turn left at the bank. It's next to the supermarket."]],"q":"Where is the post office?","o":["next to the bank","opposite the supermarket","next to the supermarket"],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"It's next to the supermarket\"."},{"id":"a2l1d","sc":[["F","Is your sister older than you?"],["M","No, she's younger. I'm twelve and she's nine. My brother is the oldest – he's sixteen."]],"q":"How old is the boy's sister?","o":["nine","twelve","sixteen"],"a":0,"tg":["comparatives-superlatives"],"e":"Kız kardeşi 9 yaşında."},{"id":"a2l1e","sc":[["M","What would you like to drink?"],["F","I usually have coffee, but it's so hot today. Can I have some orange juice with ice, please?"]],"q":"What will the woman drink?","o":["a cup of coffee","tea","orange juice"],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Sıcak olduğu için buzlu portakal suyu istiyor."}]},{"k":"L2","nm":"Dinleme 2 · Not tamamlama","ti":"Listening Part 2","ins":"Bir kayıt dinleyeceksin. Her boşluğa bir kelime, sayı ya da tarih yaz.","id":"a2l2","t":"A Bike Tour","ty":"open","title":"Notlar","sc":[["M","Good morning, and welcome to City Bike Tours. Our tour today starts at ten o'clock from the front of the railway station. The tour takes three hours, and we'll stop for lunch at a café by the river. The price is eighteen pounds, and that includes your bike and a helmet. Our guide today is called Jenny – that's J, E, double N, Y. Please wear comfortable shoes and bring a bottle of water. Enjoy your tour!"]],"p":"City Bike Tours\nMeeting place: in front of the (1) ______ station\nLength of tour: (2) ______ hours\nLunch: at a café by the (3) ______\nPrice: £(4) ______\nGuide's name: (5) ______","a":[["railway","train"],["3","three"],["river"],["18","eighteen"],["Jenny"]],"tg":[["detail"],["spelling"],["detail"],["spelling"],["spelling"]],"e":["\"from the front of the railway station\".","\"The tour takes three hours\".","\"a café by the river\".","\"eighteen pounds\".","J-E-N-N-Y."]},{"k":"L3","nm":"Dinleme 3 · Uzun konuşma","ti":"Listening Part 3","ins":"İki kişinin konuşmasını dinle ve soruları cevapla.","id":"a2l3","t":"The New Sports Club","ty":"mc","sc":[["M","Hi Lucy. I heard you joined the new sports club."],["F","Yes, I joined last week. It's great. It's near the school, so I can walk there."],["M","What sports can you do?"],["F","There's tennis, basketball and swimming. I wanted to try tennis, but the classes were full, so I'm doing basketball."],["M","Is it expensive?"],["F","It's twenty-five pounds a month for students. Adults pay forty."],["M","When do you go?"],["F","On Tuesdays and Thursdays after school. Why don't you come with me on Thursday?"],["M","I can't on Thursday, I have a piano lesson. But I could come on Tuesday."]],"q":[{"q":"How does Lucy get to the sports club?","o":["by bus","on foot","by bike"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Okula yakın, yürüyerek gidiyor."},{"q":"Which sport is Lucy doing?","o":["tennis","swimming","basketball"],"a":2,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Tenis dolu olduğu için basketbol yapıyor."},{"q":"How much does Lucy pay each month?","o":["£25","£40","£15"],"a":0,"tg":["spelling"],"e":"Öğrenciler ayda 25 sterlin."},{"q":"Why can't the boy go on Thursday?","o":["He has a piano lesson.","He has basketball practice.","He has to study."],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Perşembe piyano dersi var."},{"q":"When might the boy go to the club?","o":["on Monday","on Tuesday","on Thursday"],"a":1,"tg":["may-might"],"e":"\"I could come on Tuesday\"."}]},{"k":"L4","nm":"Dinleme 4 · Kısa kayıtlar","ti":"Listening Part 4","ins":"Beş kısa kayıt dinleyeceksin. Her soru için doğru cevabı seç.","id":"a2l4","t":"Short Messages","ty":"mc","q":[{"id":"a2l4a","sc":[["F","Hi Mark, it's Sally. I'm at the shops. Do you want me to buy anything for dinner tonight? Call me back."]],"q":"Why is Sally calling?","o":["to invite Mark to dinner","to offer to buy something","to ask for directions to the shops"],"a":1,"tg":["purpose"],"e":"Akşam yemeği için bir şey almasını isteyip istemediğini soruyor."},{"id":"a2l4b","sc":[["M","I went to the new Chinese restaurant last night. The food was delicious, but it was very expensive, and we waited forty minutes for a table."]],"q":"What did the man like about the restaurant?","o":["the prices","the food","the service"],"a":1,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Yemek lezzetliydi; fiyat ve bekleme olumsuz."},{"id":"a2l4c","sc":[["F","Attention please. The museum will close in fifteen minutes. Please make your way to the main exit. The gift shop is open until six."]],"q":"Where is the woman speaking?","o":["in a museum","in a shop","at a station"],"a":0,"tg":["inference"],"e":"\"The museum will close in fifteen minutes\"."},{"id":"a2l4d","sc":[["M","I usually get up at seven, but today I got up at six because I had to finish my homework before school."]],"q":"Why did the boy get up early today?","o":["He had a test.","He had homework to do.","He wanted to go running."],"a":1,"tg":["detail","past-simple"],"e":"Ödevini bitirmesi gerekiyordu."},{"id":"a2l4e","sc":[["F","You'll love this film. It's about a family who travel around the world in a small boat. It's funny, but it's also quite sad at the end."]],"q":"What is the film about?","o":["a family's journey","a sad love story","a funny school story"],"a":0,"tg":["gist"],"e":"Küçük bir teknede dünyayı dolaşan bir aile."}]},{"k":"L5","nm":"Dinleme 5 · Eşleştirme","ti":"Listening Part 5","ins":"Bir konuşma dinleyeceksin. Her kişiyi doğru seçenekle (A–H) eşleştir. Üç seçenek fazladır.","id":"a2l5","t":"Birthday Presents","ty":"match","sc":[["F","Dad, it's Grandma's birthday on Sunday. What is everyone giving her?"],["M","Well, your uncle Peter is giving her some flowers from his garden."],["F","And Aunt Rose? She usually gives her a book."],["M","Not this year. She's bought her a scarf, a red one."],["F","What about Tom?"],["M","Tom's made a cake. He's been practising for weeks!"],["F","And Mum?"],["M","Mum's giving her some theatre tickets. They're going together next month."],["F","I want to give her something special too. Maybe I'll paint a picture of her garden."],["M","She'll love that."]],"opts":[["A","a book"],["B","a cake"],["C","flowers"],["D","a painting"],["E","a scarf"],["F","theatre tickets"],["G","chocolates"],["H","a photo album"]],"q":[{"q":"Uncle Peter","a":"C","tg":["detail"],"e":"Bahçesinden çiçek."},{"q":"Aunt Rose","a":"E","tg":["detail"],"e":"Bu yıl kitap değil, kırmızı bir atkı."},{"q":"Tom","a":"B","tg":["detail","present-perfect"],"e":"Tom pasta yaptı."},{"q":"Mum","a":"F","tg":["detail"],"e":"Tiyatro biletleri."},{"q":"The girl","a":"D","tg":["detail","will-going-to"],"e":"Büyükannesinin bahçesinin resmini yapacak."}]}],"W":[{"id":"a2w1","part":6,"t":"A Birthday Party","in":"You are having a birthday party next Saturday. Write an email to your English friend Sam. In your email:\n• invite Sam to the party\n• say where the party is\n• say what Sam should bring.\nWrite 25 words or more.","mail":"To: Sam\nSubject: My party","pts":[["Partiye davet et",["party","come","would you like","invite"]],["Partinin yerini söyle",["at my house","at the","in the","park","café","restaurant","home"]],["Ne getirmesi gerektiğini söyle",["bring","please bring","can you bring"]]],"min":25,"max":60,"model":"Hi Sam,\nI'm having a birthday party next Saturday and I'd love you to come! It's at my house at 4 o'clock. Please bring your favourite music, and some snacks if you can.\nSee you,\nAyşe","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı fikirlerle","Hi Sam,\nGuess what? It's my birthday next Saturday and I'm having a party in the garden of our flat. Would you like to come? It starts at 5 o'clock and finishes at about 9. Can you bring some music? Your speaker is much better than mine!\nSee you soon,\nBurak"],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (A2 üstü)","Dear Sam,\nI'm having a party for my fifteenth birthday next Saturday and I'd really love you to come! We're going to have it at Pizza House, the new restaurant opposite the cinema, from 6 to 9 p.m. My mum is paying for the pizza, so you don't need to bring any money. But could you bring your guitar? Everyone wants to hear you play!\nLove,\nDefne"]]},{"id":"a2w2","part":6,"t":"Borrowing a Book","in":"Your English friend Jo has a book you want to read. Write an email to Jo. In your email:\n• ask to borrow the book\n• say why you want to read it\n• say when you will give it back.\nWrite 25 words or more.","mail":"To: Jo\nSubject: Your book","pts":[["Kitabı ödünç iste",["borrow","can i","could i","lend"]],["Neden okumak istediğini söyle",["because","i want","i love","interested"]],["Ne zaman geri vereceğini söyle",["give it back","return","next week","on monday","on friday","in two weeks","bring it back"]]],"min":25,"max":60,"model":"Hi Jo,\nCould I borrow your book about space, please? I want to read it because we're studying the planets at school and it looks really interesting. I'll give it back next Friday.\nThanks,\nMert","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı fikirlerle","Hi Jo,\nCan I borrow your cookbook for a few days? My mum's birthday is on Sunday and I want to make a special cake for her. I saw a great chocolate cake recipe in your book. I promise I'll give it back on Monday, and I'll bring you a piece of cake too!\nThanks,\nEce"],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (A2 üstü)","Hi Jo,\nI hope you're well. Could I borrow that book about football you showed me last week? I'm writing a project about famous players for my English class, and your book has lots of interesting information and photos. I'll be very careful with it, and I'll give it back to you at school next Friday, after I finish my project.\nThanks a lot,\nKerem"]]},{"id":"a2w3","part":6,"t":"My New Phone","in":"You have just bought a new phone. Write an email to your English friend Chris. In your email:\n• say where you bought it\n• say what colour it is\n• say what you like about it.\nWrite 25 words or more.","mail":"To: Chris\nSubject: My new phone","pts":[["Nereden aldığını söyle",["bought it","from","at the","shop","online","centre"]],["Rengini söyle",["black","white","blue","red","green","silver","gold","pink","purple","grey"]],["Neyini sevdiğini söyle",["like","love","camera","screen","battery","fast","because"]]],"min":25,"max":60,"model":"Hi Chris,\nGuess what? I've got a new phone! I bought it at the shopping centre last weekend. It's dark blue. I really like it because the camera is excellent and the battery lasts all day.\nBye,\nSelin","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı fikirlerle","Hi Chris,\nI've got a new phone! My grandparents gave me money for my birthday, so I bought it online. It's white with a silver back. The best thing about it is the screen – it's really big, so I can watch films and play games on it. I'll send you a photo!\nTalk soon,\nMelis"],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (A2 üstü)","Hi Chris,\nI finally bought a new phone yesterday! I got it from the big electronics shop in the city centre because it was on sale. It's green, which is my favourite colour. I really love it because the battery lasts for two days and the camera takes amazing photos, even at night. My old phone was so slow, so this one is much better.\nBye for now,\nArda"]]},{"id":"a2w4","part":7,"kind":"story","t":"The Lost Dog","in":"Look at the three pictures. Write the story shown in the pictures.\nWrite 35 words or more.","mail":"Picture 1: 👧🐕🌳  (Bir kız parkta köpeğini gezdiriyor.)\nPicture 2: 🐕💨🐿️  (Köpek bir sincabın peşinden koşup kayboluyor.)\nPicture 3: 👧👮🐕  (Bir polis memuru köpeği kıza geri getiriyor.)","pts":[["Kızı ve köpeği anlat",["dog","walk","walking","park"]],["Köpeğin kaçtığını anlat",["ran","run","squirrel","lost","away"]],["Sonucu anlat",["police","found","brought","back","happy"]],["Geçmiş zaman kullan",["was","went","ran","found","brought","looked"]]],"min":35,"max":80,"model":"One afternoon, Zeynep was walking her dog, Max, in the park. Suddenly Max saw a squirrel and ran after it. Zeynep looked everywhere, but she couldn't find him. She was very worried. An hour later, a police officer brought Max back to her. Zeynep was really happy.","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı fikirlerle","Last Sunday, Elif took her dog, Pamuk, for a walk in the park. Suddenly, Pamuk saw a squirrel and ran after it into the trees. Elif called him again and again, but he didn't come back. She was very sad and started to cry. Then a police officer arrived with Pamuk. He had found him near the lake. Elif thanked him and hugged her dog."],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (A2 üstü)","It was a sunny afternoon, so Selin decided to take her dog, Max, to the park. While they were walking near the trees, Max saw a squirrel and ran after it as fast as he could. Selin looked everywhere for him for an hour, but she couldn't find him and she was very worried. In the end, a kind police officer found Max and brought him back. Selin was so happy that she gave him a big hug."]]},{"id":"a2w5","part":7,"kind":"story","t":"A Day at the Beach","in":"Look at the three pictures. Write the story shown in the pictures.\nWrite 35 words or more.","mail":"Picture 1: 👨‍👩‍👦🚗🏖️  (Bir aile arabayla plaja gidiyor.)\nPicture 2: 🌧️☔😮  (Plajda birden yağmur başlıyor.)\nPicture 3: 🍕🏠😄  (Aile evde pizza yiyip oyun oynuyor.)","pts":[["Plaja gidişi anlat",["beach","car","drove","went"]],["Yağmuru anlat",["rain","rained","raining","suddenly"]],["Sonucu anlat",["home","pizza","game","played","happy","fun"]],["Geçmiş zaman kullan",["was","went","drove","started","ate","played"]]],"min":35,"max":80,"model":"Last summer, Can and his parents drove to the beach. They wanted to swim and play volleyball. But after ten minutes, it suddenly started to rain! They ran back to the car and went home. In the evening they ate pizza and played board games. It was a fun day after all.","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı fikirlerle","Last Saturday, Mert and his family went to the beach by car. When they arrived, they put up their umbrella and Mert ran into the sea. But after a few minutes, black clouds came and it started to rain very hard. They were all wet! So they went home. In the evening, they ordered pizza and played cards. Mert said it was the best rainy day ever."],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (A2 üstü)","One hot day in July, Ada and her parents drove to the beach. They were planning to swim and have a picnic. However, just after they arrived, the sky went dark and it started to rain heavily. Everyone on the beach ran to their cars. Ada was disappointed, but her dad had a good idea. They went home, made pizza together and played board games all evening. In the end, it was a really fun day."]]}],"S":[{"id":"a2s1","p":1,"t":"School and Home","ins":"Kısa sorulara tam cümlelerle cevap ver.","q":[["What's your favourite subject at school? Why?",25,["my favourite","because","I like"],["My favourite subject at school is science. I like it because we do lots of experiments in the lab, and I like learning how things work. Our teacher is very funny, too.","I really like art. I'm not very good at maths, but I love drawing and painting. In art lessons I can relax and be creative, and the time goes very quickly."]],["How do you get to school?",20,["I go","by bus","on foot","I walk","it takes"],["I usually go to school by bus. The bus stop is near my house, and the journey takes about twenty minutes. Sometimes my dad drives me when it's raining.","I walk to school with my best friend. It's not very far – only ten minutes – and we talk all the way. I think walking is good for me."]],["What do you usually have for breakfast?",20,["I usually have","I eat","I drink"],["I usually have bread with cheese, olives and tomatoes, and a glass of tea. At the weekend my mum makes eggs, and we eat together.","On school days I don't have much time, so I usually just have a bowl of cereal with milk and a banana. Sometimes I have orange juice too."]],["Tell me about your bedroom.",30,["there is","there are","my bedroom","I have"],["My bedroom is quite small, but I love it. There's a bed next to the window, a desk where I do my homework, and a big cupboard for my clothes. On the wall there are posters of my favourite football players. I share it with my little brother, so it's sometimes messy!","My bedroom is my favourite room in the house. It's blue and white, and it has a big window, so it's very bright. I've got a comfortable bed, a desk with a computer and lots of books on a shelf. I often listen to music there in the evenings."]]]},{"id":"a2s2","p":1,"t":"Weekends and Holidays","ins":"Kısa sorulara tam cümlelerle cevap ver.","q":[["What did you do last weekend?",25,["last weekend","I went","I visited","I watched"],["Last weekend I visited my grandparents in their village. We had a big lunch together in the garden, and then I played football with my cousins. In the evening we watched a film.","Last weekend I stayed at home because I had a lot of homework. On Sunday afternoon I went to the cinema with my friends and we watched a funny film. It was great."]],["Where did you go on your last holiday?",25,["I went to","we stayed","it was"],["On my last holiday I went to Antalya with my family. We stayed in a hotel near the sea for a week. It was very hot, so we swam every day and ate lots of ice cream.","Last summer we went to my uncle's house in Trabzon. It's in the mountains and it's very green. We went walking every day and we visited a beautiful lake."]],["What are you going to do next summer?",25,["I'm going to","I want to","I hope"],["Next summer I'm going to work in my uncle's shop for a month, because I want to save some money. After that, I hope to go camping with my friends near the sea.","Next summer I'm going to take swimming lessons, because I can't swim very well. I also want to visit my cousin in Istanbul. We're going to go to a concert."]],["Tell me about your best friend.",30,["my best friend","he is","she is","we","because"],["My best friend is called Arda. We've known each other since primary school, so for about eight years. He's tall and he's got short black hair. He's very funny and kind, and he always helps me with maths. We play computer games together every weekend.","My best friend's name is Zeynep. She lives in the same building as me, so we see each other every day. She's quite quiet, but she's very clever and she gives good advice. We both love music, and we often sing together in her room."]]]},{"id":"a2s3","p":2,"t":"Types of Holiday","ins":"Resimlerdeki tatil türleri hakkında kendi görüşünü söyle.","opts":["⛺ camping","🏙️ city holiday","🏖️ beach holiday","⛷️ skiing","🚢 boat trip"],"q":[["Here are some pictures of different holidays. Do you like these different holidays? Say why or why not.",60,["I like","I don't like","because","I think"],["Let me talk about these holidays. I like camping because you can sleep outside, cook on a fire and see the stars at night. A city holiday is interesting too, because you can visit museums and go shopping, but it can be expensive. I love beach holidays – swimming in the sea is my favourite thing in summer. Skiing looks exciting, but I've never tried it, and it's very cold. A boat trip would be fun, but I sometimes feel sick on boats, so I don't think I'd enjoy it very much.","Well, I don't really like camping, because it's uncomfortable and there are lots of insects. I prefer city holidays, because there are so many things to do and see, like old buildings and restaurants. Beach holidays are nice, but they can be boring after a few days. I'd love to try skiing one day because it looks really exciting. And a boat trip sounds great – you can see lots of places and maybe dolphins too!"]],["Which of these holidays would you like best? Why?",30,["I'd like","best","because"],["I'd like a beach holiday best, because I love swimming and relaxing in the sun. I can also play volleyball on the sand with my friends, and the food near the sea is always delicious.","I think I'd choose skiing, because I've never done it and I want to try something new and exciting. I'd also like to see a lot of snow, because it doesn't snow much where I live."]],["Do you prefer holidays with your family or with friends?",30,["I prefer","because"],["I prefer holidays with my family, because my parents plan everything and we have a lot of fun together. My little brother is very funny on holidays, and my dad always finds good restaurants.","I prefer going with my friends, because we like the same things and we can stay up late. With my family I sometimes have to visit places I don't like, for example museums."]]]},{"id":"a2s4","p":2,"t":"Food and Drink","ins":"Resimlerdeki yiyecekler hakkında kendi görüşünü söyle.","opts":["🍕 pizza","🥗 salad","🍣 sushi","🍲 soup","🍰 cake"],"q":[["Here are some pictures of different food. Do you like these different kinds of food? Say why or why not.",60,["I like","I don't like","because","delicious","healthy"],["OK, I love pizza – it's my favourite food! I eat it every Friday with my family. Salad is healthy, but I don't eat it very often because I don't like some vegetables. I've never tried sushi, but my sister likes it a lot. I think raw fish is a bit strange. Soup is nice in winter when it's cold outside, especially my grandmother's lentil soup. And cake is delicious, but I only eat it on special days like birthdays, because it has a lot of sugar.","I don't eat pizza very often because it's not very healthy, but I like it sometimes. I really like salad, especially with lemon and olive oil. Sushi is my favourite – there's a good sushi restaurant near my school and I go there with my friends. I don't really like soup, it's boring for me. Cake is great, especially chocolate cake. My mum makes a very good one."]],["Which of these do you like best? Why?",30,["I like ... best","because","my favourite"],["I like pizza best, because there are so many different kinds and I can eat it with my friends. My favourite is pizza with cheese and mushrooms. We often order it when we watch football.","I think I like cake best. I know it isn't healthy, but it makes me happy! My favourite is strawberry cake, and I always have one on my birthday."]],["Do you like cooking? Why or why not?",30,["I like","I don't","because","sometimes"],["Yes, I like cooking a little. I sometimes help my mother in the kitchen at the weekend. I can make pasta, eggs and a simple salad. I'd like to learn to make my grandmother's soup.","Not really. I don't have much time because of school, and I'm not very good at it. Once I tried to make a cake, but I burned it! My dad does most of the cooking in our house."]]]},{"id":"a2s5","p":2,"t":"Places in Town","ins":"Resimlerdeki yerler hakkında kendi görüşünü söyle.","opts":["🏬 shopping centre","🏞️ park","🏛️ museum","🎬 cinema","🏟️ stadium"],"q":[["Here are some pictures of places in a town. Do you like going to these places? Say why or why not.",60,["I like","I don't like","because","sometimes"],["I like going to the shopping centre with my friends, because we can look at clothes and eat something, but it's often very crowded at the weekend. Parks are great because they're quiet and green, and you can walk or play sport. Museums can be interesting, but some are boring for me. I love the cinema – I go every month and I always buy popcorn. I've been to the stadium twice to watch football, and it was very exciting because everyone was singing.","I don't really like shopping centres, because they're noisy and I don't like shopping. I prefer parks – I go running in the park near my house every morning. I think museums are interesting, especially history museums. The cinema is OK, but tickets are expensive now. I've never been to a stadium, but I'd like to go to a basketball match one day."]],["Which place do you go to most often? Why?",30,["most often","because","usually"],["I go to the park most often, because it's near my house and I can play basketball there with my friends after school. It's free, too! In summer we also have picnics there.","I go to the shopping centre most often, because my friends and I meet there on Saturdays. There's a café we like, and sometimes we watch a film there."]],["Is it better to live in a city or in the countryside? Why?",30,["I think","better","because"],["I think it's better to live in a city, because there are more things to do, like cinemas, cafés and sports centres. But the countryside is cleaner and quieter, and people are friendlier there.","I think the countryside is better, because there's no traffic and the air is clean. You can grow vegetables and have animals. Cities are exciting, but they're too noisy for me."]]]}]},"B1":{"R":[{"k":"R1","nm":"Okuma 1 · Kısa metinler","ti":"Reading Part 1","ins":"Her kısa metni oku ve metnin ne söylediğini en iyi anlatan seçeneği bul.","ty":"mc","q":[{"x":"LIBRARY\nBooks must be returned within three weeks. You can renew them online if nobody else has asked for them.","q":"What does the notice say?","o":["You can keep a book for longer if no one is waiting for it.","You must return all books in person after exactly three weeks.","You can only borrow three books at a time."],"a":0,"tg":["detail","must-have-to-should"],"e":"\"renew them online if nobody else has asked for them\" = kimse beklemiyorsa süreyi uzatabilirsin."},{"x":"Dad,\nThe plumber phoned. He can't come today, but he could come tomorrow at 8 a.m. if someone is at home. Can you call him back?\nKate","q":"What does Kate want her father to do?","o":["stay at home today","contact the plumber","repair the problem himself"],"a":1,"tg":["purpose"],"e":"\"Can you call him back?\" = tesisatçıyı geri araması isteniyor."},{"x":"FOR SALE\nChildren's bike, 2 years old, hardly used. Suitable for ages 6–9. Price can be discussed.","q":"What does the advert say?","o":["The bike has never been used by anyone.","The seller might accept a lower price.","The bike is for adults."],"a":1,"tg":["inference","vocab"],"e":"\"Price can be discussed\" = fiyat konuşulabilir, yani daha düşük bir fiyat kabul edilebilir."},{"x":"Gym members: From 1st June, you will need your membership card to enter the building. Cards can be collected from reception.","q":"What must gym members do?","o":["pay for a new card","show a card to get in","arrive before 1st June"],"a":1,"tg":["detail","passive-voice"],"e":"1 Haziran'dan itibaren binaya girmek için üyelik kartı gerekiyor."},{"x":"Hey Paul, I've finished with your camera, thanks! I'd have returned it yesterday but I was ill. I'll drop it round after work. Jack","q":"What is Jack going to do?","o":["buy a new camera","return the camera today","borrow the camera again"],"a":1,"tg":["inference","third-conditional"],"e":"Dün getirecekti ama hastaydı; bugün iş çıkışı getirecek."}],"id":"b1r1","t":"Notices and Messages"},{"k":"R2","nm":"Okuma 2 · Eşleştirme","ti":"Reading Part 2","ins":"Aşağıdaki beş kişi bir kitap arıyor. Her kişi için en uygun kitabı (A–H) seç.","ty":"match","pl":"Metinler (A–H)","opts":[["A","The Last Signal","A thriller about a girl who finds a mysterious radio message. Full of surprises from start to finish, but suitable for all ages."],["B","Midnight House","A horror story that will keep you awake at night. Not for anyone who scares easily!"],["C","Small Space, Big Harvest","How to grow tomatoes, beans and herbs in pots on a balcony or windowsill."],["D","The Perfect Garden","Ideas for designing large gardens, with plans for lawns, trees and ponds."],["E","Mountain Rescue","The true story of a team who saved climbers in a storm. Includes many photographs from the rescue."],["F","Brave Hearts","A novel about two friends who sail around the world. Beautiful writing, but quite long."],["G","My Crazy Dog","A comic story told in very short chapters, perfect for young readers who don't enjoy long books."],["H","Italy on a Budget","Where to sleep, eat and travel in Italy without spending too much. Maps and prices included."]],"q":[{"q":"Leyla: Leyla is going on a long train journey. She wants an exciting story that will keep her reading, but nothing too frightening.","a":"A","tg":["detail"],"e":"Heyecanlı ama korkutucu değil: A (B korku)."},{"q":"Oscar: Oscar wants to learn how to grow vegetables in a small space. He has a balcony but no garden.","a":"C","tg":["detail"],"e":"Balkon, sebze: C (D büyük bahçe)."},{"q":"Nina: Nina loves true stories about people who did something brave. She prefers books with photographs.","a":"E","tg":["detail"],"e":"Gerçek cesaret hikâyesi, fotoğraflı: E (F roman)."},{"q":"Ryan: Ryan is 14 and finds reading difficult. He wants something funny with short chapters.","a":"G","tg":["detail"],"e":"Komik, kısa bölümler: G."},{"q":"Mei: Mei is planning a trip to Italy. She wants practical information about cheap places to stay and eat.","a":"H","tg":["detail"],"e":"İtalya, ucuz kalacak yer ve yemek: H."}],"id":"b1r2","t":"Choosing a Book"},{"k":"R3","nm":"Okuma 3 · Uzun metin","ti":"Reading Part 3","ins":"Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.","ty":"mc","title":"Learning to play the drums","p":["I'd always wanted to play the drums, but when I was younger my parents said they were too noisy for our flat. So when I moved into my own house at the age of thirty-two, the first thing I bought was a drum kit.","At first I tried to teach myself using videos on the internet. I learned a few simple rhythms, but I soon realised I was making the same mistakes again and again. A friend suggested that I take lessons, and she gave me the number of a teacher called Marcus.","Marcus was patient but strict. He made me practise slowly with a metronome, which I found boring at first. \"If you can't play it slowly, you can't play it fast,\" he kept saying. After a couple of months, I understood what he meant: my playing had become much more confident.","Six months later, Marcus invited me to join a group of his students who played together every Friday. I was nervous, but it was the best thing I have ever done. Playing with other musicians is completely different from playing alone. Now I can't imagine my life without music."],"q":[{"q":"Why didn't the writer play the drums when he was young?","o":["He wasn't interested in music.","His family thought they were too loud.","Drum lessons were too expensive for his parents.","He didn't have time."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Ailesi davulun daire için çok gürültülü olduğunu söyledi."},{"q":"What problem did the writer have when he learned from videos?","o":["The videos were too difficult.","He kept repeating errors.","He couldn't find good videos.","He got bored quickly."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"making the same mistakes again and again\""},{"q":"What did Marcus believe?","o":["Students should play fast from the start.","Practising slowly helps you play well.","Using a metronome is boring.","Lessons should be short."],"a":1,"tg":["inference","first-conditional"],"e":"\"If you can't play it slowly, you can't play it fast\" – yavaş çalışmak iyi çalmayı sağlar."},{"q":"How did the writer feel about joining the group?","o":["bored","nervous but glad he did it","disappointed","confident from the beginning"],"a":1,"tg":["attitude","present-perfect"],"e":"\"I was nervous, but it was the best thing I have ever done\""},{"q":"What would be the best title for the text?","o":["Why drums are too noisy","How to find a good teacher when you are older","A dream that came true later in life","The problems of living alone"],"a":2,"tg":["gist"],"e":"Çocukken çalamadığı davulu yetişkinken öğrenip müziği hayatının parçası yaptı."}],"id":"b1r3","t":"Learning the Drums"},{"k":"R4","nm":"Okuma 4 · Cümle yerleştirme","ti":"Reading Part 4","ins":"Metinden beş cümle çıkarıldı. Her boşluk için uygun cümleyi (A–H) seç. Üç cümle fazladır.","ty":"gap","title":"A school with no homework","p":"Last year, Westfield School stopped giving homework to students under fourteen. The head teacher, Mr Collins, says the decision was not easy. (1) ______ But after reading many studies, he decided to try it.\nInstead of homework, students now spend the last hour of the school day on projects. (2) ______ Teachers are there to help if anyone gets stuck.\nParents had mixed feelings at the start. (3) ______ Others were happy that family evenings were less stressful.\nAfter one year, the results are interesting. Exam results have stayed the same. (4) ______ Students say they feel less tired and more interested in learning.\nThe school will continue the experiment for another two years. (5) ______ If the results are still positive, other schools in the area may follow.","opts":[["A","Some were worried that their children would fall behind."],["B","Many teachers were afraid that students would learn less."],["C","They can choose what to work on, and they often work in small groups."],["D","However, the number of students who say they enjoy school has gone up."],["E","During this time, researchers will collect more information."],["F","Homework is still compulsory for older students at weekends."],["G","The students wrote a letter to the newspaper about it."],["H","Exam results fell sharply in the first term."]],"a":["B","C","A","D","E"],"tg":[["coherence"],["coherence"],["reference"],["linkers-contrast-purpose"],["reference"]],"e":["Karar kolay değildi çünkü öğretmenler endişeliydi; \"But after reading many studies\" ile devam ediyor: B.","Projelerle ilgili ayrıntı; \"Teachers are there to help\": C.","\"Some ... Others ...\" yapısı: A.","Sınav sonuçları aynı kaldı; \"However\" ile olumlu fark: D (H çelişir).","\"During this time\" = iki yıllık süre: E."],"id":"b1r4","t":"A School Without Homework"},{"k":"R5","nm":"Okuma 5 · Çoktan seçmeli boşluk","ti":"Reading Part 5","ins":"Metni oku ve her boşluk için doğru kelimeyi seç.","ty":"cloze","title":"The village festival","p":"Every summer, our village (1) ______ a festival to raise money for the local school. It started in 1985 and it has become more popular (2) ______ year. This year more than two thousand people came, (3) ______ the weather was cold and cloudy. There were lots of (4) ______ for children, including face painting and a treasure hunt. My favourite part was the music competition, which was (5) ______ by a local band. We raised over five thousand pounds, which will be (6) ______ on new computers for the school.","o":[[["holds","takes","makes","gives"],0,["vocab"]],[["all","every","each other","whole"],1,["vocab"]],[["despite","however","even though","because"],2,["linkers-contrast-purpose"]],[["activities","activity","actions","acts"],0,["countable-uncountable"]],[["won","beaten","earned","gained"],0,["passive-voice"]],[["spent","paid","bought","used"],0,["passive-advanced"]]],"e":["hold a festival: festival düzenlemek.","every year: her yıl.","even though + cümle: -mesine rağmen (despite + isim alır).","lots of + çoğul sayılabilir isim: activities.","win a competition: yarışmayı kazanmak (pasif: was won).","spend money on something: bir şeye para harcamak (pasif: will be spent on)."],"id":"b1r5","t":"The Village Festival"},{"k":"R6","nm":"Okuma 6 · Açık boşluk","ti":"Reading Part 6","ins":"Metni oku ve her boşluğa TEK kelime yaz.","ty":"open","title":"Dear Mr Lee","p":"Dear Mr Lee,\nThank you for your email. I am very interested (1) ______ the summer job at your café. I have worked in a restaurant before, so I know how busy it can be. I am available from 1st July (2) ______ the end of August. I can work at weekends, but I am not able to work (3) ______ Monday mornings because I have a language class. I would be grateful (4) ______ you could tell me what time the job starts each day. I am sending my CV with this email. I look forward to (5) ______ from you.\nYours sincerely,\nAyşe Kaya\nP.S. Could you also tell me (6) ______ I need to bring a uniform?","a":[["in"],["until","to","till"],["on"],["if"],["hearing"],["if","whether"]],"tg":[["prepositions-in-on-at"],["prepositions-in-on-at"],["prepositions-in-on-at"],["second-conditional"],["gerund-infinitive"],["reported-speech"]],"e":["interested in: -e ilgili.","from ... until / to: -den ... -e kadar.","Gün adıyla on: on Monday mornings.","I would be grateful if you could ...: nazik rica.","look forward to + -ing: hearing.","Dolaylı evet / hayır sorusu: if / whether."],"id":"b1r6","t":"A Letter to Mr Lee"}],"L":[{"k":"L1","nm":"Dinleme 1 · Kısa kayıtlar","ti":"Listening Part 1","ins":"Yedi kısa kayıt dinleyeceksin. Her soru için doğru cevabı seç.","ty":"mc","q":[{"id":"l1a","sc":[["F","Hello, I'd like to book a table for Friday evening."],["M","Certainly. For how many people?"],["F","There'll be six of us. Oh, actually, my brother can't come, so five."]],"q":"How many people will be at the table?","o":["four","five","six"],"a":1,"tg":["detail","spelling"],"e":"Önce altı dedi, sonra kardeşi gelemeyeceği için beş."},{"id":"l1b","sc":[["M","What are you doing this weekend?"],["F","I was going to go camping, but the forecast says it's going to rain. So I'll probably visit my cousins in London instead."]],"q":"What will the woman probably do this weekend?","o":["go camping","stay at home and study","visit her cousins"],"a":2,"tg":["detail","will-going-to"],"e":"Kampa gidecekti ama yağmur yüzünden kuzenlerini ziyaret edecek."},{"id":"l1c","sc":[["F","Excuse me, where can I find the shampoo?"],["M","It's not with the soap any more. We've moved it next to the toothpaste, at the end of aisle four."]],"q":"Where is the shampoo now?","o":["next to the soap","next to the toothpaste","at the end of aisle three"],"a":1,"tg":["detail","present-perfect"],"e":"Artık sabunların yanında değil, diş macunlarının yanında."},{"id":"l1d","sc":[["M","Hi Jo, it's Dan. The football match on Saturday starts at three, but the coach wants us there forty-five minutes early to warm up. See you then!"]],"q":"What time should Jo arrive?","o":["2.15","2.45","3.00"],"a":0,"tg":["detail","must-have-to-should"],"e":"Maç 3'te, 45 dakika önce orada olmalılar: 2.15."},{"id":"l1e","sc":[["F","How did you get here so quickly?"],["M","Normally I take the bus, but today my neighbour gave me a lift in his car."],["F","Lucky you!"]],"q":"How did the man travel today?","o":["by bus","by car","on foot"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Bugün komşusu arabasıyla bıraktı."},{"id":"l1f","sc":[["M","Did you like the jacket?"],["F","The colour was perfect, but it was too small, and they didn't have a bigger size. So I bought a jumper instead."]],"q":"Why didn't the woman buy the jacket?","o":["She didn't like the colour.","It was the wrong size.","It was too expensive."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Rengi güzeldi ama küçüktü, büyük bedeni yoktu."},{"id":"l1g","sc":[["F","This is a message for passengers on the twelve fifteen train to Manchester. This train will now leave from platform seven, not platform three. We apologise for any inconvenience."]],"q":"Which platform will the train leave from?","o":["3","7","12"],"a":1,"tg":["detail","spelling"],"e":"\"from platform seven, not platform three\""}],"id":"b1l1","t":"Short Recordings"},{"k":"L2","nm":"Dinleme 2 · Kısa konuşmalar","ti":"Listening Part 2","ins":"Altı kısa konuşma dinleyeceksin. Her soru için doğru cevabı seç.","ty":"mc","q":[{"id":"l2a","sc":[["M","How was the concert?"],["F","The band were great, but we were so far from the stage that we couldn't see anything."],["M","What a shame."]],"q":"What was the woman's problem at the concert?","o":["The music was bad.","Her seat was too far away.","There were too many people there."],"a":1,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Sahneden çok uzaktaydılar, hiçbir şey göremediler."},{"id":"l2b","sc":[["F","I hear you're learning Japanese."],["M","Yes. Speaking isn't too bad, but I find the writing system really hard to remember."]],"q":"What does the man find difficult?","o":["speaking","writing","listening"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Yazı sistemini hatırlamak zor."},{"id":"l2c","sc":[["M","Did you enjoy the hotel?"],["F","The room was lovely and the staff were friendly. I just wish it had been closer to the beach. We had to take a bus every day."]],"q":"What would the woman change about the hotel?","o":["the room","the staff at reception","the location"],"a":2,"tg":["attitude","wish-if-only"],"e":"\"I wish it had been closer to the beach\" – konum."},{"id":"l2d","sc":[["F","Are you going to the party tonight?"],["M","I'd like to, but I have an exam tomorrow morning. If I go out, I won't be able to concentrate."]],"q":"Why won't the man go to the party?","o":["He hasn't been invited.","He needs to study.","He feels ill."],"a":1,"tg":["inference","first-conditional"],"e":"Yarın sınavı var; dışarı çıkarsa odaklanamaz."},{"id":"l2e","sc":[["M","What do you think of the new sports centre?"],["F","It's modern and there's lots of equipment, but it costs twice as much as the old one. I don't think I'll renew my membership."]],"q":"What does the woman think about the sports centre?","o":["It is too expensive.","It is too old.","It doesn't have enough equipment."],"a":0,"tg":["attitude","comparatives-superlatives"],"e":"\"costs twice as much as the old one\" – çok pahalı."},{"id":"l2f","sc":[["F","You didn't answer my calls yesterday."],["M","Sorry. I left my phone at work and didn't realise until I got home. It must have been in my desk drawer."]],"q":"Why didn't the man answer his phone?","o":["The battery had completely run out.","He didn't have it with him.","He was too busy."],"a":1,"tg":["detail","modals-deduction"],"e":"Telefonunu işte unutmuştu."}],"id":"b1l2","t":"Opinions"},{"k":"L3","nm":"Dinleme 3 · Not tamamlama","ti":"Listening Part 3","ins":"Bir yaz kampı duyurusunu dinle. Her boşluğa bir kelime, sayı ya da tarih yaz.","ty":"open","id":"b1l3","sc":[["M","Hello, and thanks for your interest in Green Valley Summer Camp. This year the camp runs from the fourteenth of July for two weeks. It is open to young people aged eleven to sixteen. Every morning there are outdoor activities, such as canoeing and climbing. In the afternoon, campers can choose between art, music and drama. The price is three hundred and fifty pounds, which includes all meals and equipment. Campers sleep in wooden cabins, with six people in each one. Please remember to bring a sleeping bag and a torch. You do not need to bring any sports equipment. To book a place, visit our website before the end of May. We look forward to seeing you!"]],"title":"Notlar","p":"Green Valley Summer Camp\nStart date: (1) ______ July\nAge: 11 to (2) ______\nAfternoon choices: art, music and (3) ______\nPrice: £(4) ______\nCampers sleep in wooden (5) ______\nRemember to bring a sleeping bag and a (6) ______","a":[["14","14th","fourteenth","fourteen"],["16","sixteen"],["drama"],["350","three hundred and fifty"],["cabins","cabin"],["torch"]],"tg":[["spelling"],["spelling"],["detail"],["spelling"],["detail"],["detail"]],"e":["\"from the fourteenth of July\"","\"aged eleven to sixteen\"","\"art, music and drama\"","\"three hundred and fifty pounds\"","\"sleep in wooden cabins\"","\"bring a sleeping bag and a torch\""],"t":"Summer Camp"},{"k":"L4","nm":"Dinleme 4 · Röportaj","ti":"Listening Part 4","ins":"Bir gönüllüyle yapılan röportajı dinle ve soruları cevapla.","ty":"mc","id":"b1l4","sc":[["F","My guest today is Tom Hughes, who spent a year volunteering on a farm in New Zealand. Tom, why did you decide to go?"],["M","I'd just finished university and I didn't know what job I wanted. A friend had done something similar and said it had changed her life, so I thought, why not?"],["F","What was a typical day like?"],["M","We started at six, which was hard at first because I'm not a morning person! We fed the animals, fixed fences and helped in the fields. The work was physical, but I got used to it after a few weeks."],["F","Was there anything you didn't enjoy?"],["M","Honestly, the work was fine. What I found difficult was being so far from my family. The farm was in the middle of nowhere, and the internet was terrible, so I could only call home once a week."],["F","What did you learn from the experience?"],["M","I learned that I really enjoy working outdoors. Before I went, I thought I wanted an office job, but now I'm training to be a park ranger."],["F","Would you recommend it to others?"],["M","Definitely, but I'd tell people to be prepared. It isn't a holiday. If you want to relax on the beach, it's not for you."]],"q":[{"q":"Why did Tom go to New Zealand?","o":["A friend told him it was a good experience.","He wanted to study farming at university later.","His university organised it."],"a":0,"tg":["detail","reported-speech"],"e":"Arkadaşı benzer bir şey yapmış ve hayatını değiştirdiğini söylemişti."},{"q":"What was difficult for Tom at the beginning?","o":["the heavy work","getting up early","looking after animals"],"a":1,"tg":["detail","used-to"],"e":"Sabah altıda başlamak ilk başta zordu; \"not a morning person\"."},{"q":"What did Tom find hardest during the year?","o":["the physical work","missing his family","the bad weather in winter"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Ailesinden bu kadar uzak olmak."},{"q":"Why could Tom only call home once a week?","o":["Phone calls home were far too expensive.","The internet connection was poor.","He was too busy."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"the internet was terrible\""},{"q":"How has the experience changed Tom's plans?","o":["He wants to work outside now.","He wants to live in New Zealand.","He has decided to get an office job."],"a":0,"tg":["inference"],"e":"Ofis işi isterken artık park bekçisi olmak için eğitim alıyor."},{"q":"What advice does Tom give?","o":["Take a holiday first.","Understand that it is hard work.","Go with a friend so you are not lonely."],"a":1,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"\"be prepared. It isn't a holiday.\""}],"t":"Volunteering Abroad"},{"k":"L3","id":"b1l5","t":"A Cookery Course","nm":"Dinleme 3 · Not tamamlama","ti":"Listening Part 3","ty":"open","title":"Notlar","ins":"Bir yemek kursu tanıtımını dinle. Her boşluğa bir kelime, sayı ya da tarih yaz.","sc":[["M","Hello, and thanks for calling the Green Kitchen Cookery School. Here is some information about our summer course for young people. The course is for students aged fourteen to eighteen, and it runs for two weeks, starting on the third of July. Classes take place every weekday morning from ten until one. In the first week, students learn how to make different kinds of bread, and in the second week we focus on Italian dishes, especially pasta. The course costs one hundred and twenty pounds, and that includes all the food. Students only need to bring an apron. At the end of the course, there is a special lunch, and every student can invite two members of their family. To book a place, please visit our website or send us an email. We look forward to cooking with you!"]],"p":"Green Kitchen summer course\nAge: 14 to 18\nStarts on: (1) ______ July\nClasses every weekday: 10 a.m. to (2) ______ p.m.\nWeek 1: different kinds of (3) ______\nWeek 2: Italian food, especially (4) ______\nPrice: £(5) ______, including all the food\nStudents must bring: an (6) ______","a":[["3","3rd","third"],["1","one","1.00"],["bread"],["pasta"],["120","one hundred and twenty"],["apron"]],"tg":[["spelling"],["spelling"],["detail"],["detail"],["spelling"],["detail"]],"e":["\"starting on the third of July\"","\"from ten until one\"","\"how to make different kinds of bread\"","\"Italian dishes, especially pasta\"","\"one hundred and twenty pounds\"","\"Students only need to bring an apron\""]}],"W":[{"id":"b1w1","part":1,"t":"The New Sports Club","in":"Read this email from your English friend Alex and the notes you have made. Write your email to Alex using all the notes.","mail":"From: Alex\nSubject: Sports club\n\nHi!\nI've just joined a new sports club and I want you to come with me next Saturday. [Great!]\nWe can play tennis or go swimming – which would you prefer? [Tell Alex]\nThe club opens at 9 a.m. What time can you meet? [Suggest a time]\nAfter that, shall we have lunch together? [No, because ...]\nAlex","pts":[["Teşekkür et / sevindiğini söyle",["great","thanks","thank you","love to","sounds","fantastic","brilliant"]],["Tenis mi yüzme mi, seçimini söyle",["tennis","swim"]],["Bir buluşma saati öner",["o'clock","a.m","am ","p.m","half past","quarter","meet at","10","11","9.30","ten","eleven"]],["Öğle yemeğine gelemeyeceğini ve nedenini söyle",["because","can't","cannot","afraid","sorry","unfortunately"]]],"min":80,"max":130,"model":"Hi Alex,\nThanks for your email – it's great news that you've joined a sports club! I'd love to come with you on Saturday.\nI'd prefer to go swimming, because I haven't played tennis for years and I'm not very good at it. Swimming is also more relaxing.\nThe club opens at 9, but I have to take my little brother to his football match first. Shall we meet at half past ten outside the club?\nI'm afraid I can't have lunch with you after that, because my grandparents are visiting us and my mum wants me to be at home by one o'clock. Maybe we can have lunch next time?\nSee you on Saturday!\nDeniz","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı fikirlerle","Hi Alex,\nGreat news about the sports club! Of course I'll come with you on Saturday – I've wanted to try something new for a while.\nI'd rather play tennis than go swimming. I swim every week at school, so tennis would be a nice change, and I've got my brother's old racket.\nCould we meet at 9.30 at the bus stop near your house? Then we can walk to the club together.\nI'm afraid I can't have lunch with you, because it's my grandmother's birthday and the whole family is eating together at one o'clock. Maybe next time!\nSee you on Saturday,\nCan"],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (B2'ye yakın)","Hi Alex,\nThanks for your message – I'm really pleased you've found a club you like, and I'd love to come along on Saturday.\nTo be honest, I'd prefer swimming. I hurt my wrist playing volleyball last month, and I don't think tennis would be a good idea until it's completely better.\nAs the club opens at nine, why don't we meet at quarter past outside the main entrance? That way we'll have plenty of time before it gets crowded.\nUnfortunately, I won't be able to stay for lunch, because I've promised to help my dad move some furniture in the afternoon. Shall we go for a pizza another day instead?\nSee you soon,\nNehir"]]},{"id":"b1w2","part":1,"t":"The Class Trip","in":"Read this email from your English teacher Mrs Brown and the notes you have made. Write your email to Mrs Brown using all the notes.","mail":"From: Mrs Brown\nSubject: Class trip\n\nDear students,\nNext month our class will go on a day trip. [Yes, I'd like to go]\nWe can visit either the science museum or the zoo. Which do you think the class would enjoy more? [Say which and why]\nWe also need to decide how to travel. [Suggest ...]\nIs there anything else you would like to do on the trip? [Tell Mrs Brown]\nBest wishes,\nMrs Brown","pts":[["Geziye katılmak istediğini söyle",["like to","love to","would like","excited","looking forward","great idea","join"]],["Müze mi hayvanat bahçesi mi, nedeniyle söyle",["museum","zoo"]],["Ulaşım önerisi yap",["bus","train","coach","car","walk","travel by","go by"]],["Gezide yapmak istediğin başka bir şeyi söyle",["also","picnic","photos","shop","lunch","park","visit","would like to"]]],"min":80,"max":130,"model":"Dear Mrs Brown,\nThank you for your email. I would really like to go on the class trip – it sounds like a great idea!\nI think the class would enjoy the science museum more than the zoo. Most of us went to the zoo last year, and the museum has a new space exhibition with lots of interactive activities.\nI suggest that we travel by train, because the station is near the school and it is cheaper than hiring a coach.\nAlso, it would be nice to have a picnic in the park next to the museum at lunchtime, if the weather is good.\nBest wishes,\nAyşe","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı fikirlerle","Dear Mrs Brown,\nThank you for telling us about the trip. Yes, I would definitely like to go!\nI think the class would enjoy the zoo more. We studied animals in science this term, and it would be great to see them in real life. Also, the zoo is outside, so we could have lunch in the park there.\nI suggest that we go by coach, because it's cheaper for a big group and we can all travel together.\nI would also like to visit the gift shop and buy some postcards for my little sister.\nBest wishes,\nEmre"],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (B2'ye yakın)","Dear Mrs Brown,\nThank you for your email. I'd be delighted to come on the class trip – it's a great way to end the term.\nIn my opinion, the science museum would be the better choice. Not only is it indoors, which is useful if it rains, but it also has a planetarium, which most of us have never been to.\nAs for travelling, I would suggest taking the train. The station is only five minutes from school, and it would be faster than a coach because there's always heavy traffic on the motorway.\nFinally, if there's time, it would be lovely to have a picnic in the gardens next to the museum.\nKind regards,\nIrmak"]]},{"id":"b1w3","part":2,"kind":"article","t":"Free Time","in":"You see this announcement on an English-language website for teenagers. Write your article in about 100 words.","mail":"ARTICLES WANTED!\nMy favourite way to spend free time\nWhat do you enjoy doing in your free time? Why do you enjoy it? Is it better to spend free time alone or with other people?\nThe best articles will appear on our website.","pts":[["Ne yaptığını anlat",["enjoy","love","like","favourite","spend"]],["Neden sevdiğini açıkla",["because","so","reason","makes me","helps me"]],["Yalnız mı başkalarıyla mı, görüşünü söyle",["alone","with friends","with other","others","on my own","together"]],["Bir başlık kullan",["@title"]]],"min":80,"max":140,"model":"Music: the best way to relax\nIn my free time, I love playing the guitar. I started when I was twelve, and now I practise almost every day after school.\nI enjoy it because it helps me forget about exams and homework. When I play a song well, I feel really proud of myself.\nSome people think hobbies are better with friends, but I think it depends on the hobby. I usually practise alone because I need to concentrate. However, at weekends I play with two friends in a small band, and that is the most fun of all. So, for me, the answer is both!","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı fikirlerle","Cooking: my secret hobby\nMost of my friends are surprised when I tell them my favourite free-time activity is cooking. Every Sunday, I choose a recipe from the internet and make lunch for my family.\nI enjoy it because it's creative and the result is always delicious – well, almost always! It also helps me relax after a busy week at school.\nI think some hobbies are better alone, but cooking is more fun with other people. When my cousins visit, we cook together, and the kitchen is full of noise and laughter. Food tastes better when you share it!"],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (B2'ye yakın)","Why I spend my weekends on two wheels\nIf you ask me how I prefer to spend my free time, the answer is simple: cycling. Every Saturday morning, I ride along the coast road with a small group from my neighbourhood.\nWhat I love most is the feeling of freedom. When I'm cycling, I forget about exams and problems, and I notice things I would never see from a car or bus, like tiny beaches and old fishing boats.\nAlthough some people prefer to exercise alone, I think it's much more motivating to go with others. On cold mornings, I would probably stay in bed if my friends weren't waiting for me! So my advice is: find a hobby you love, and find people to share it with."]]},{"id":"b1w4","part":2,"kind":"article","t":"Technology at School","in":"You see this announcement in an English-language magazine for students. Write your article in about 100 words.","mail":"ARTICLES WANTED!\nShould students use phones in class?\nDo you use technology at school? How does it help you? Are there any problems with using phones in class?\nWrite an article answering these questions.","pts":[["Okulda teknoloji kullanımını anlat",["use","tablet","computer","phone","laptop","internet"]],["Faydasını açıkla",["help","useful","easier","quickly","find information"]],["Sorunlarını söyle",["problem","however","but","distract","cheat","games","social media"]],["Bir başlık kullan",["@title"]]],"min":80,"max":140,"model":"Phones in class: friend or enemy?\nAt my school, we often use tablets in lessons, and sometimes our teachers let us use our phones to look things up.\nTechnology helps us in many ways. For example, we can find information quickly, watch short videos about science and practise English with apps.\nHowever, there are also problems. Some students play games or check social media instead of listening to the teacher. Phones can also make it easier to cheat in tests.\nIn my opinion, phones are useful if teachers have clear rules. Without rules, they cause more problems than they solve.","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı fikirlerle","Technology: a useful tool, not a toy\nIn my class, we use laptops in most lessons, and sometimes we use our phones to take photos of experiments.\nTechnology helps me a lot. When I don't understand something, I can watch a short video about it, and online quizzes help me prepare for tests.\nHowever, phones can cause problems. Some students send messages during lessons or play games when the teacher isn't looking. I think phones should only be used when the teacher says so. Then they can be a great tool instead of a distraction."],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (B2'ye yakın)","Should phones be banned from classrooms?\nAt my school, we're allowed to use tablets in lessons, but phones must stay in our bags unless a teacher gives us permission.\nThere's no doubt that technology makes learning easier. We can look up information in seconds, practise vocabulary with apps and work on projects together online, even from home.\nOn the other hand, phones can be a serious distraction. It only takes one notification for a student to stop listening, and some people even use them to cheat in tests.\nIn my view, banning phones completely isn't the answer. Instead, schools should teach us how to use them responsibly – after all, we'll need those skills for the rest of our lives."]]},{"id":"b1w5","part":2,"kind":"story","t":"The Midnight Phone Call","in":"Your English teacher has asked you to write a story. Your story must begin with this sentence. Write your story in about 100 words.","mail":"Your story must begin with this sentence:\nWhen the phone rang at midnight, Sam knew something was wrong.","pts":[["Verilen cümleyle başla",["when the phone rang at midnight"]],["Geçmiş zamanla olayları anlat",["was","were","went","said","told","saw","had"]],["Bir sorun ve çözüm olsun",["but","suddenly","then","finally","in the end","luckily"]],["Hikâyeyi bitir (sonuç)",["in the end","finally","at last","never","happy","relieved","home"]]],"min":80,"max":140,"model":"When the phone rang at midnight, Sam knew something was wrong. It was his sister, Lily, and she sounded worried.\n\"My car has broken down on the mountain road,\" she said. \"It's dark and my phone battery is almost dead.\"\nSam jumped out of bed, put on his coat and drove towards the mountains. It was raining heavily and he could hardly see the road. After forty minutes, he finally saw a small light by the side of the road. Lily was sitting in her car, cold but safe.\nThey drove home together, and in the end they laughed about the adventure over hot chocolate.","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı fikirlerle","When the phone rang at midnight, Sam knew something was wrong. It was his neighbour, Mrs Clark, and she sounded frightened.\n\"I can hear someone in my garden,\" she whispered.\nSam put on his shoes, took a torch and went next door. His heart was beating fast as he walked slowly around the house. Suddenly, he heard a noise behind the bins. He pointed his torch and saw two bright eyes looking at him.\nIt was a fox! It was eating the rubbish that the wind had knocked over. Sam laughed, and Mrs Clark made him a cup of hot chocolate to say thank you."],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (B2'ye yakın)","When the phone rang at midnight, Sam knew something was wrong. Nobody ever called him that late. He picked it up and heard his best friend, Leo, who was clearly upset.\n\"I've missed the last train home,\" Leo explained. \"I'm at the station and my wallet has been stolen.\"\nWithout hesitating, Sam grabbed his car keys. The streets were empty and it was raining heavily, so the journey took longer than usual. When he finally arrived, he found Leo sitting alone on a bench, wet and shivering.\nOn the way home, Leo kept apologising, but Sam just smiled. \"That's what friends are for,\" he said. A week later, Leo surprised him with concert tickets to say thank you."]]},{"id":"b1w6","part":2,"kind":"story","t":"The Missing Key","in":"Your English teacher has asked you to write a story. Your story must begin with this sentence. Write your story in about 100 words.","mail":"Your story must begin with this sentence:\nAs soon as Elif opened her bag, she realised the key was missing.","pts":[["Verilen cümleyle başla",["as soon as elif opened her bag"]],["Geçmiş zamanla olayları anlat",["was","were","went","looked","found","had","decided"]],["Bir sorun ve çözüm olsun",["but","suddenly","then","luckily","so"]],["Hikâyeyi bitir (sonuç)",["in the end","finally","at last","happy","relieved","laughed"]]],"min":80,"max":140,"model":"As soon as Elif opened her bag, she realised the key was missing. She was standing outside her flat, and her parents were on holiday for a week.\nShe tried to remember where she had been that day. She had gone to school, then to the library, and finally to a café with her friends.\nFirst, she called the café, but nobody had found a key. Then she went back to the library. Luckily, the librarian smiled and held up a small silver key. \"Is this yours?\" she asked.\nElif was so relieved that she bought the librarian a box of chocolates the next day.","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı fikirlerle","As soon as Elif opened her bag, she realised the key was missing. She had to open the school sports hall for the basketball team, and the match started in twenty minutes!\nShe ran back to the classroom and looked under her desk, but it wasn't there. Then she remembered that she had taken her bag to the canteen at lunchtime. She hurried there and asked the cook.\n\"Is this yours?\" the cook asked with a smile, holding up a small silver key.\nElif thanked her and ran to the sports hall. She opened the door just as the team arrived."],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (B2'ye yakın)","As soon as Elif opened her bag, she realised the key was missing. It was the key to her grandfather's old wooden box, which he had given her before he moved abroad, and she had promised to keep it safe.\nShe tried to stay calm and thought about everything she had done that day. She had been to the library, then to the park with her friends. Feeling worried, she went back to the park and searched every bench, but there was no sign of it.\nAs she was walking home, disappointed, a little boy ran up to her. \"Did you drop this?\" he asked, holding out the key. He had seen it fall from her bag and followed her.\nElif was so relieved that she bought him an ice cream. That evening, she put the key on a chain and wore it around her neck."]]}],"S":[{"id":"b1s1","p":1,"t":"About You (1)","ins":"Examiner seni tanımak için kısa sorular soruyor. Her soruya 2–3 cümleyle cevap ver.","q":[["What's your name? Where do you live?",20,["my name is","i live in","i'm from"],["My name's Deniz Kaya, and I live in Eskişehir, a student city in the middle of Turkey. I live with my parents and my younger sister in a flat near the river.","I'm Mert. I come from a small town near Bursa, but at the moment I live in Istanbul, because I'm studying there. I share a flat with two friends from university."]],["Do you work or are you a student?",20,["i'm a student","i study","i work","at the moment"],["I'm a student. I'm in my last year at high school, so I'm studying very hard for the university exam. My favourite subjects are biology and chemistry.","I work as a receptionist in a hotel. I've been there for two years. I like it because I meet people from different countries and I can practise my English."]],["What do you like doing in your free time?",20,["i like","i enjoy","in my free time","because"],["I really enjoy playing basketball. I play with my friends in the park two or three times a week. I also like watching series in the evenings to relax.","In my free time, I usually read novels or draw. I'm quite a quiet person, so I like hobbies I can do at home. At the weekend I sometimes go to art exhibitions."]],["Tell us about your best friend.",25,["my best friend","we","known","since"],["My best friend is called Ayşe. We met on our first day at secondary school, and we've been friends ever since. She's very funny and honest, and she always listens to my problems. We love going to concerts together.","My best friend's name is Tolga. He lives next door, so we see each other almost every day. He's really good at football and computers, and he's helped me a lot with my homework. We're planning a trip together next summer."]],["What did you do last weekend?",25,["last weekend","went","visited","watched"],["Last weekend I went to my cousin's wedding in Izmir. It was in a big garden by the sea, and there was lots of music and dancing. I didn't get home until two in the morning!","Last weekend was quite quiet. On Saturday I tidied my room and did my homework, and on Sunday I went for a long walk with my dog in the forest. In the evening I watched a football match."]],["What are you going to do next summer?",25,["going to","i'd like","i hope","plan"],["Next summer I'm going to work in a café near the beach, because I want to save money for a new laptop. If I have time, I'd also like to go camping with my friends.","I'm going to visit my aunt in Germany next summer. I've never been abroad before, so I'm really excited. I'm hoping to see Berlin and maybe practise a bit of German."]]]},{"id":"b1s2","p":1,"t":"About You (2)","ins":"Examiner seni tanımak için kısa sorular soruyor. Her soruya 2–3 cümleyle cevap ver.","q":[["How do you usually travel to school or work?",20,["usually","by bus","on foot","takes"],["I usually go to school by bus. It takes about twenty-five minutes, so I listen to music or read on the way. When the weather's nice, I sometimes cycle instead.","I walk to work, because my office is only fifteen minutes from my flat. I think it's a good way to start the day, and it means I don't have to worry about traffic."]],["What kind of music do you like?",20,["i like","favourite","because","listen"],["I like lots of different music, but my favourite is rock. I love bands with loud guitars and good drummers. I also listen to Turkish pop when I'm with my friends.","I mostly listen to jazz and classical music, because it helps me relax and concentrate when I'm studying. My parents listened to it a lot when I was a child."]],["Tell us about your home.",25,["live in","flat","house","there is","there are"],["I live in a flat on the fourth floor of a building in the city centre. It has three bedrooms, a small kitchen and a balcony where we have breakfast in summer. The best thing about it is the view of the mountains.","My home is a small house with a garden in a quiet village. It's quite old, but it's very comfortable. My favourite room is the living room, because it has a big fireplace and we sit there together in winter."]],["What's your favourite food? Why?",20,["favourite","because","delicious","my mum"],["My favourite food is mantı, a kind of Turkish pasta with meat and yoghurt. My grandmother makes the best mantı in the world, and it always reminds me of family dinners.","I love sushi. I tried it for the first time two years ago and I loved it immediately. It's light and healthy, and there are so many different kinds to try."]],["Have you ever been to another country?",25,["have been","haven't been","never","last year","i'd like"],["Yes, I have. Two years ago I went to Greece with my family. We visited Athens and a small island. I loved the old buildings and the food, which was similar to Turkish food in many ways.","No, I haven't been abroad yet, but I'd love to go to Japan one day. I'm really interested in Japanese culture, and I've been watching lots of videos about Tokyo."]],["Is English important for you? Why?",25,["important","because","job","travel","future"],["Yes, it's very important for me, because I want to study at university abroad. English also helps me understand films, songs and websites, and I can talk to tourists in my city.","English is important for my job, because many of our customers are from other countries. Also, I like travelling, and English helps me communicate wherever I go."]]]},{"id":"b1s3","p":2,"t":"A Favourite Place","ins":"PET'te bu bölümde bir fotoğrafı anlatırsın. Burada fotoğraf yerine konu kartı var: 1 dakika boyunca anlat.","q":[["Describe a place in your town where people like to spend time. Talk about where it is, what people do there, what you can see and hear, and why people like it.",60,["there is","there are","people are","in the middle","on the left","i can see","because","looks"],["I'd like to talk about the seaside promenade in my town. It's a long path next to the sea, near the city centre. In the evenings, especially in summer, it's full of people. Families walk with their children, young people ride bikes or skateboards, and older people sit on benches and chat. You can see fishing boats, seagulls and, at sunset, the sky turns orange and pink. You can hear the waves, music from the cafés and people selling corn and tea. I think people like it because it's free, it's close to the water and it's a relaxing place to meet friends after a busy day.","One place that's very popular in my town is the central park. It's next to the old castle, so it's easy to find. At the weekend, families have picnics on the grass, children play in the playground and teenagers play football or volleyball. There's also a small lake with ducks, and people often feed them. You can hear children laughing, birds singing and sometimes a street musician playing the guitar. I think people love it because it's green and peaceful, and it's one of the few places where you can forget you're in a busy city."]]]},{"id":"b1s4","p":2,"t":"A Busy Moment","ins":"Fotoğraf yerine konu kartı: 1 dakika boyunca, sanki fotoğrafa bakıyormuş gibi anlat.","q":[["Imagine a photo of a busy train station. Describe the people, what they are doing, what they are wearing and how they might be feeling.",60,["in the picture","i can see","is wearing","are waiting","looks","might be","probably","in the background"],["In this photo, I can see a busy train station, probably in the morning. There are lots of people walking quickly in different directions. On the left, a man in a dark suit is looking at his watch – I think he's late for work. In the middle, a young couple with big suitcases are checking the departure board, so maybe they're going on holiday. They look excited. On the right, a woman is buying a coffee from a small café. Most people are wearing coats and scarves, so it's probably cold outside. In general, people seem a bit stressed, because everyone is in a hurry.","This photo shows the inside of a large station. It looks very modern, with a high glass roof. Near the front, a group of students with backpacks are sitting on the floor and laughing – maybe they're waiting for a school trip. Behind them, there's an old man holding a newspaper and looking at the trains. He looks relaxed. A mother is holding her little boy's hand, and he's pointing at a train. People are wearing summer clothes, like T-shirts and sunglasses, so I think it's a hot day. Most of them look quite happy and calm."]]]},{"id":"b1s5","p":3,"t":"Making New Friends","ins":"PET'te bu bölüm bir partnerle yapılır. Burada iki tarafı da sen düşün: seçenekleri karşılaştır ve bir karar ver. 1 dakika.","q":[["A friend is moving to a new city and doesn't know anyone. Talk about these ideas and decide which one is the best way to make new friends: joining a sports club, taking an evening class, volunteering, using a social app, talking to neighbours.",60,["what about","i think","because","it would be","on the other hand","i agree","the best","let's"],["Let's think about each idea. Joining a sports club is good because you meet people regularly and you have something to talk about. Taking an evening class is also useful, because people there are interested in the same subject. Volunteering is a great idea, because you help others and you work in a team. Using a social app is easy, but it can be a bit dangerous to meet strangers from the internet. Talking to neighbours is nice, but in big cities many people don't know their neighbours. In my opinion, the best way is joining a sports club, because you see the same people every week and it's easy to become friends.","I'll start with the social app. It's probably the quickest way to find people with similar interests, but I'm not sure the friendships last. Talking to neighbours is friendly, but you might not have much in common. A sports club is great if you like sport, but some people don't. An evening class is a good choice because it's relaxed and you can talk during breaks. I think volunteering is the best idea, though, because you meet kind people, you feel useful, and working together on something important brings people closer."]]]},{"id":"b1s6","p":4,"t":"Friendship","ins":"Examiner konuyla ilgili görüşünü soruyor. Her soruya fikrini ve nedenini söyle.","q":[["Do you prefer spending time with a big group of friends or with one or two close friends? Why?",40,["i prefer","because","for example","in my opinion","it depends"],["I prefer spending time with one or two close friends. In a big group it's difficult to have a real conversation, and some people don't get the chance to speak. With close friends, I can talk about anything, even my problems, and I feel more relaxed. But I think big groups are fun for parties or birthdays.","I prefer big groups, because there's always someone to talk to and there's more energy. For example, when we go to the beach with ten people, we can play volleyball and lots of games. With only one friend, it can get boring after a while."]],["Is it easier to make friends at school or online?",40,["i think","easier","because","however","on the other hand"],["I think it's easier at school, because you see the same people every day and you do things together, like projects and sports. Online, it's easy to start chatting, but you don't really know if people are honest about themselves. Real friendship needs time together.","In some ways, it's easier online. Shy people, like me, find it less stressful to write than to speak. Also, online you can find people who love the same things as you, even if they live far away. I met one of my best friends in an online game."]],["How can people keep in touch with friends who live far away?",40,["can","video call","message","visit","for example"],["Today it's quite easy. You can send messages every day, share photos and have video calls. I talk to my cousin in Germany every Sunday on video. But I think it's also important to visit each other when you can, because meeting face to face is different.","People can use social media to see what their friends are doing, and they can play online games together. Some people still send letters or postcards, which I think is very special. Planning a holiday together once a year is also a good way to stay close."]]]}]},"B2":{"R":[{"k":"R1","nm":"Okuma 1 · Çoktan seçmeli boşluk","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 1","ins":"Metni oku ve her boşluk için en uygun kelimeyi (A, B, C ya da D) seç.","id":"b2r1","t":"Why Volunteer?","ty":"cloze","title":"Why volunteer?","p":"Volunteering is often seen as something people do purely to help others, but research shows that volunteers (1) ______ a great deal themselves. Regular volunteers tend to report higher (2) ______ of happiness and lower stress. Volunteering also gives people the chance to (3) ______ new skills, which can be useful when looking for a job. Many employers (4) ______ great importance on this kind of experience. Of course, not everyone has much spare time. (5) ______, even a few hours a month can make a difference. The important thing is to choose an activity that (6) ______ your interests, so that you are more likely to stick (7) ______ it. Some organisations are happy to (8) ______ volunteers for a single day.","o":[[["win","gain","earn","reach"],1,["collocation"]],[["levels","grades","marks","amounts"],0,["collocation"]],[["catch","acquire","reach","achieve"],1,["vocab"]],[["put","give","place","set"],2,["collocation"]],[["Therefore","Moreover","Otherwise","However"],3,["linkers-contrast-purpose"]],[["joins","equals","matches","agrees"],2,["vocab"]],[["on","with","at","to"],1,["collocation"]],[["take on","take up","take over","take in"],0,["vocab"]]],"e":["gain from: kazanç sağlamak (faydalanmak).","levels of happiness: mutluluk düzeyi.","acquire skills: beceri edinmek.","place importance on: önem vermek.","However: zıtlık; boş zamanı yok → yine de.","match your interests: ilgi alanlarına uymak.","stick with it: devam etmek, bırakmamak.","take on volunteers: gönüllü almak (işe almak anlamında)."]},{"k":"R2","nm":"Okuma 2 · Açık boşluk","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 2","ins":"Metni oku ve her boşluğa TEK kelime yaz.","id":"b2r2","t":"Teenagers and Sleep","ty":"open","one":true,"title":"Teenagers and sleep","p":"Parents often complain that their teenage children stay up late and are impossible to wake (1) ______ in the morning. But according to scientists, this is not simply a matter (2) ______ laziness. During adolescence, the body clock shifts, (3) ______ means that teenagers naturally feel sleepy later at night. If they have (4) ______ get up early for school, they may get far (5) ______ sleep than they need. Some schools have responded by starting lessons later, and the results have been impressive. Students' grades have improved, and (6) ______ have their attendance and mood. (7) ______ some people argue that later start times cause problems for families, many experts believe the benefits are too important (8) ______ ignore.","a":[["up"],["of"],["which"],["to"],["less"],["so"],["although","though","while","whilst"],["to"]],"tg":[["vocab"],["vocab"],["relative-clauses"],["must-have-to-should"],["comparatives-superlatives"],["vocab"],["linkers-contrast-purpose"],["gerund-infinitive"]],"e":["wake up: uyanmak / uyandırmak.","a matter of: meselesi.","Tüm cümleye gönderme yapan which.","have to: zorunda olmak.","far less ... than: çok daha az.","\"and so have ...\" : -ları da (öyle).","Although: zıtlık bağlacı + cümle.","too ... to + fiil."]},{"k":"R3","nm":"Okuma 3 · Kelime türetme","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 3","ins":"Metni oku. Her boşluk için verilen kök kelimeyi uygun biçime getir (isim, sıfat, zarf, olumsuz vb.).","id":"b2r3","t":"Working from Home","ty":"open","title":"Working from home","st":["INCREASE","FLEXIBLE","EMPLOY","PRODUCE","COMMUNICATE","LONELY","EXPECT","HAPPY"],"p":"An (1) ______ number of people now work from home at least part of the week. For many, the (2) ______ to organise their own time is the main attraction. (3) ______ have also discovered benefits: some studies suggest that remote workers are more (4) ______ than their office-based colleagues. However, working from home is not without its problems. Poor (5) ______ between team members can lead to misunderstandings, and some people suffer from (6) ______ because they miss the social side of office life. It is also (7) ______ difficult for some people to separate work from home life. Those who fail to do so often report (8) ______ with their work-life balance.","a":[["increasing"],["flexibility"],["employers"],["productive"],["communication"],["loneliness"],["unexpectedly"],["unhappiness"]],"tg":[["word-formation"],["word-formation"],["word-formation"],["word-formation"],["word-formation"],["word-formation"],["word-formation"],["word-formation"]],"e":["an + sıfat + number: increasing (artan).","the + isim: flexibility (esneklik).","Fiil \"have discovered\" öznesi; anlam: işverenler (çoğul).","more + sıfat: productive.","Poor + isim: communication.","suffer from + isim: loneliness (yalnızlık).","Sıfatı niteleyen zarf, anlam \"beklenmedik şekilde\": unexpectedly.","report + isim; olumsuz anlam: unhappiness."]},{"k":"R4","nm":"Okuma 4 · Cümle dönüştürme","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 4","ins":"İkinci cümleyi, birinciyle aynı anlamı verecek şekilde tamamla. Verilen kelimeyi değiştirmeden kullan; 2–5 kelime yaz.","id":"b2r4","t":"Key Word Transformations","ty":"kwt","q":[{"s1":"I haven't been to the cinema for months.","key":"SINCE","s2":"It's been months ______ to the cinema.","a":["since I last went","since I went","since I have been","since I've been"],"tg":["present-perfect"],"e":"It's been months since + geçmiş zaman."},{"s1":"\"Don't forget to lock the door,\" Mum told me.","key":"REMINDED","s2":"Mum ______ the door.","a":["reminded me to lock"],"tg":["reporting-verbs"],"e":"remind someone to do something."},{"s1":"The concert was cancelled because of the storm.","key":"CALLED","s2":"They ______ because of the storm.","a":["called off the concert","called the concert off"],"tg":["vocab"],"e":"call off = cancel."},{"s1":"It was such a boring film that I fell asleep.","key":"SO","s2":"The film ______ I fell asleep.","a":["was so boring that","was so boring"],"tg":["vocab"],"e":"so + sıfat + that."},{"s1":"I regret not taking that job.","key":"WISH","s2":"I ______ that job.","a":["wish I had taken","wish I'd taken"],"tg":["wish-if-only"],"e":"Geçmişe pişmanlık: wish + had + V3."},{"s1":"Someone stole my bike last night.","key":"HAD","s2":"I ______ last night.","a":["had my bike stolen"],"tg":["causative"],"e":"have something done (başına gelen olay): had my bike stolen."}]},{"k":"R6","nm":"Okuma 6 · Cümle yerleştirme","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 6","ins":"Metinden altı cümle çıkarıldı. Her boşluk için uygun cümleyi (A–G) seç. Bir cümle fazladır.","id":"b2r6","t":"A Year in Antarctica","ty":"gap","title":"A year in Antarctica","p":"When I accepted a job as a research assistant at a base in Antarctica, my friends thought I was mad. (1) ______ I had never even seen snow until I was twelve.\nThe first thing that struck me was the silence. (2) ______ After a few weeks, though, I started to find it peaceful rather than frightening.\nLife at the base was highly organised. (3) ______ This was necessary because in such an extreme environment, a single careless mistake could be dangerous.\nThe hardest period was the winter, when the sun did not rise for months. (4) ______ To stay positive, we organised film nights, cooking competitions and even a small music festival.\nI had expected to feel isolated from the rest of the world. (5) ______ We had internet access for a few hours a day, and I spoke to my family every week.\nWhen I finally returned home, I found ordinary life strangely overwhelming. (6) ______ It took me months to get used to it again.","opts":[["A","In fact, I felt more connected than I had imagined."],["B","Everyone had a list of duties, from cleaning to checking equipment."],["C","There was no traffic, no birds, not even the sound of wind some days."],["D","I could understand why: I had grown up in a hot country."],["E","The noise and crowds in the city seemed unbearable."],["F","The temperature never rose above minus twenty."],["G","Many of us found it hard to sleep and felt low."]],"a":["D","C","B","G","A","E"],"tg":[["reference"],["coherence"],["coherence"],["coherence"],["linkers-contrast-purpose"],["reference"]],"e":["Arkadaşları deli sandı → \"I could understand why\" + 12 yaşına kadar kar görmemiş: D.","\"silence\" → trafik yok, kuş yok: C.","\"highly organised\" → herkesin görev listesi: B.","Güneş doğmadı → uyuyamadık, moral bozuk; sonra \"To stay positive\": G.","\"expected to feel isolated\" → \"In fact, I felt more connected\": A.","\"ordinary life overwhelming\" → şehirdeki gürültü ve kalabalık: E. F fazladır."]}],"L":[{"k":"L1","nm":"Dinleme 1 · Kısa kayıtlar","ti":"Listening Part 1","ins":"Sekiz farklı durumda kısa kayıtlar dinleyeceksin. Her soru için en uygun cevabı seç.","id":"b2l1","t":"Short Extracts (1)","ty":"mc","q":[{"id":"b2l1a","sc":[["M","I wasn't sure about joining the drama club – I'm quite shy. But it's actually helped a lot. I'm still nervous before we perform, but I've learned to speak up in class, which I never used to do."]],"q":"You hear a student talking about a drama club. What does he say about it?","o":["He no longer feels nervous when he performs on stage.","It has made him more confident in other situations.","He joined because his friends were members."],"a":1,"tg":["inference","used-to"],"e":"Sahnede hâlâ gergin ama sınıfta konuşmayı öğrendi."},{"id":"b2l1b","sc":[["F","We were going to drive, but with the price of petrol and the traffic in summer, the train made more sense. It's a bit slower, but at least we can relax and read."]],"q":"You hear a woman talking about a journey. Why did she choose the train?","o":["It was faster.","It was more practical.","Her car had broken down."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Benzin ve trafik yüzünden tren daha mantıklı."},{"id":"b2l1c","sc":[["M","Honestly, the new manager has some good ideas, but she doesn't listen. She makes decisions without asking anyone, and then wonders why people aren't enthusiastic."],["F","Maybe give her time. It's only been a month."]],"q":"You hear two colleagues talking. What does the man criticise about the manager?","o":["her lack of experience","the new ideas she has suggested","the way she makes decisions"],"a":2,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Kimseye sormadan karar veriyor."},{"id":"b2l1d","sc":[["F","If you're going to the market, could you get some tomatoes? Not the big ones, they're tasteless. The small cherry ones. And if they haven't got any, don't bother – we'll manage without."]],"q":"You hear a woman talking to a family member. What does she want him to do?","o":["buy whatever tomatoes are cheapest","buy a particular kind of tomatoes","go to a different shop"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Sadece küçük kiraz domatesler; yoksa hiç alma."}]},{"k":"L2","nm":"Dinleme 2 · Cümle tamamlama","ti":"Listening Part 2","ins":"Bir konuşma dinleyeceksin. Cümleleri bir kelime ya da kısa bir ifadeyle tamamla.","id":"b2l2","t":"Beekeeping","ty":"open","title":"Cümleler","sc":[["M","Good evening. My name's David, and I've been keeping bees for about fifteen years. I started after my doctor suggested I find a hobby that would get me outdoors. I bought my first hive from a retired teacher in my village. Many people think beekeeping is mainly about honey, but for me the most rewarding part is simply watching the bees work. A healthy hive in summer can contain around fifty thousand bees. The biggest threat to bees today, in my opinion, isn't disease but the loss of wild flowers. If you want to help bees, the easiest thing you can do is plant lavender in your garden. Most new beekeepers worry about being stung, but it rarely happens if you move slowly and stay calm. Last year my bees produced about thirty kilos of honey, which I share with my neighbours."]],"p":"David started keeping bees on the advice of his (1) ______.\nHe bought his first hive from a former (2) ______.\nFor David, the best part of beekeeping is (3) ______ the bees.\nIn summer, a hive may have about (4) ______ bees.\nDavid thinks the main danger to bees is the loss of (5) ______.\nHe recommends planting (6) ______ to help bees.\nLast year his bees made about (7) ______ kilos of honey.","a":[["doctor"],["teacher"],["watching"],["50,000","50000","fifty thousand"],["wild flowers","wildflowers","flowers"],["lavender"],["30","thirty"]],"tg":[["detail"],["detail"],["detail"],["spelling"],["detail"],["spelling"],["spelling"]],"e":["\"my doctor suggested\".","\"a retired teacher\" – former = eski, emekli.","\"simply watching the bees work\".","\"around fifty thousand bees\".","\"the loss of wild flowers\".","\"plant lavender\".","\"about thirty kilos of honey\"."]},{"k":"L3","nm":"Dinleme 3 · Çoklu eşleştirme","ti":"Listening Part 3","ins":"Beş kişinin konuşmasını dinleyeceksin. Her konuşmacı için doğru seçeneği (A–H) seç. Üç seçenek fazladır.","id":"b2l3","t":"Moving to a New City","ty":"match","sc":[["F","Speaker one. When I moved to London for work, I expected it to be exciting, and it was. What I hadn't expected was how expensive everything would be. I spent half my salary on rent for a tiny room."],["M","#1"],["M","Speaker two. I moved to a small town in the north, and people were so welcoming. Within a week my neighbours had invited me for dinner. I'd heard northerners were friendly, but this was something else."],["F","#1"],["F2","Speaker three. Moving to Berlin was hard because I didn't speak German. I took evening classes, and after six months I could manage. Now I dream in German sometimes!"],["M","#1"],["M2","Speaker four. I'd always lived in big cities, so moving to the countryside was a shock. At first the quiet kept me awake at night. Now I can't imagine going back to the noise."],["F","#1"],["F","Speaker five. My company moved me to Madrid. The work was the same, but the lifestyle was completely different. People eat dinner at ten at night! It took me months to adjust my routine."]],"opts":[["A","I was surprised by the cost of living."],["B","I had to learn a new language."],["C","I found it hard to make friends."],["D","People were even kinder than I had heard."],["E","I had to change my daily habits."],["F","I regret the move."],["G","I found the silence difficult at first."],["H","I missed my family."]],"q":[{"q":"Speaker 1","a":"A","tg":["gist"],"e":"Her şeyin ne kadar pahalı olduğunu beklemiyordu."},{"q":"Speaker 2","a":"D","tg":["gist"],"e":"Duyduğundan bile daha misafirperver."},{"q":"Speaker 3","a":"B","tg":["gist"],"e":"Almanca öğrenmek zorunda kaldı."},{"q":"Speaker 4","a":"G","tg":["gist"],"e":"Sessizlik başta uyutmadı."},{"q":"Speaker 5","a":"E","tg":["gist"],"e":"Günlük rutinini değiştirmesi aylar sürdü."}]},{"k":"L4","nm":"Dinleme 4 · Röportaj","ti":"Listening Part 4","ins":"Bir röportaj dinleyeceksin. Her soru için en uygun cevabı seç.","id":"b2l4","t":"A Wildlife Photographer","ty":"mc","sc":[["F","With me today is Marco Ruiz, a wildlife photographer. Marco, what got you interested in photographing animals?"],["M","My grandmother, actually. She was a keen birdwatcher, and she gave me her old camera when I was ten. I spent hours in her garden trying to photograph birds. Most of the pictures were terrible, but I was hooked."],["F","What's the most difficult part of your job?"],["M","People assume it's the danger, but I've rarely felt unsafe. It's the waiting. I once spent eleven days in a hide to photograph a single bird. You need enormous patience."],["F","Has technology changed wildlife photography?"],["M","Hugely. Cameras are so good now that almost anyone can take a sharp picture. That means the competition is tougher. What makes a photo stand out today isn't technical quality – it's the story it tells."],["F","Do you think photography can help protect animals?"],["M","I'd like to think so. A single powerful image can make people care about a species they'd never heard of. But photos alone aren't enough. They have to be followed by action."]],"q":[{"q":"Who first encouraged Marco's interest in photography?","o":["his grandmother","a teacher","a professional photographer"],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Büyükannesi ona eski fotoğraf makinesini verdi."},{"q":"What does Marco find hardest about his work?","o":["the physical danger","having to wait for a long time","travelling to remote and dangerous places"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"It's the waiting\"."},{"q":"According to Marco, what makes a wildlife photo special today?","o":["the quality of the camera","the story behind it","how rare the animal is"],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"it's the story it tells\"."},{"q":"What is Marco's view on photography and conservation?","o":["Photos can raise interest, but action is also needed.","Photos are the most effective way to protect endangered animals.","Photography has little effect on people."],"a":0,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"\"photos alone aren't enough. They have to be followed by action\"."}]},{"k":"L1","nm":"Dinleme 1 · Kısa kayıtlar","ti":"Listening Part 1","ins":"Sekiz farklı durumda kısa kayıtlar dinleyeceksin. Her soru için en uygun cevabı seç.","id":"b2l5","t":"Short Extracts (2)","ty":"mc","q":[{"id":"b2l5a","sc":[["F","I've been using the new gym for a month. The equipment's excellent, and it's never crowded. My only problem is that it closes at eight, which is too early for people who work late like me."]],"q":"You hear a woman talking about a gym. What does she dislike about it?","o":["the equipment","the opening hours","the number of people"],"a":1,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Saat sekizde kapanıyor; geç çalışanlar için erken."},{"id":"b2l5b","sc":[["M","I'm calling about the advert for the flat in Green Street. Is it still available? And could you tell me whether pets are allowed? I've got a small dog."]],"q":"You hear a man leaving a message. What does he want to know?","o":["how much the rent is each month","whether he can keep an animal","where the flat is"],"a":1,"tg":["purpose"],"e":"Evcil hayvana izin olup olmadığını soruyor."},{"id":"b2l5c","sc":[["F","Have you tried that new podcast about history?"],["M","I listened to one episode. The presenters clearly know their stuff, but they kept interrupting each other. I found it really hard to follow."]],"q":"What does the man think of the podcast?","o":["The presenters are not knowledgeable.","It is difficult to follow.","The episodes are too long."],"a":1,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"Sunucular birbirinin sözünü kesiyor; takip etmesi zor."},{"id":"b2l5d","sc":[["M","We'd planned to go camping, but the forecast was awful, so we stayed in a cottage instead. In the end it was sunny all weekend! Still, the cottage was lovely, so I'm not complaining."]],"q":"How does the man feel about the weekend?","o":["disappointed with the weather","satisfied with how it went","annoyed about the forecast"],"a":1,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"\"I'm not complaining\" – memnun."}]}],"W":[{"id":"b2w1","part":1,"kind":"essay","t":"Social Media and Young People","in":"In your English class you have been talking about technology. Now your English teacher has asked you to write an essay. Write your essay using all the notes and giving reasons for your point of view. Write 140–190 words.","mail":"Has social media done more harm than good for young people?\n\nNotes – write about:\n1. staying in touch with friends\n2. mental health\n3. ............ (your own idea)","pts":[["Arkadaşlarla iletişimi tartış",["in touch","friends","contact","communicate","connect"]],["Ruh sağlığını tartış",["mental health","anxiety","stress","self-esteem","depressed","compare"]],["Kendi fikrini ekle",["also","another","in addition","moreover","furthermore"]],["Sonuç / görüş",["in conclusion","to sum up","overall","on balance","in my opinion"]]],"min":140,"max":190,"model":"Social media is now a central part of most teenagers' lives, and opinions are divided about whether this is a positive development.\n\nOn the one hand, social media makes it easy to stay in touch with friends, even those who live far away. Young people can share experiences instantly and find others with similar interests, which can be especially valuable for those who feel isolated.\n\nOn the other hand, there is growing evidence that heavy use can damage mental health. Teenagers constantly compare themselves with the perfect images they see online, and this can lead to anxiety and low self-esteem.\n\nAnother important issue is the spread of false information. Many young people get their news from social media without checking whether it is reliable.\n\nOn balance, I believe social media is not harmful in itself; the problem is how it is used. If schools teach young people to use it critically and to limit their screen time, its benefits can outweigh its disadvantages.","more":[["Örnek 2 · karşı görüşle","Social media has changed the way young people communicate, learn and spend their free time. In my view, it has done more harm than good.\n\nIt is true that social media makes it easier to stay in touch with friends. However, many teenagers now spend hours scrolling through messages instead of meeting face to face, and these online conversations are often shallow.\n\nThe effect on mental health is even more worrying. Young people are constantly exposed to edited photos and seemingly perfect lives, which can make them feel that they are not good enough. Studies have linked heavy use to anxiety, poor sleep and low self-esteem.\n\nA further problem is that social media is designed to be addictive. Endless feeds and notifications make it extremely difficult to concentrate on homework or reading, and many students admit that they check their phones dozens of times an hour.\n\nIn conclusion, although social media has some benefits, I believe its negative effects on young people's well-being and concentration are far more significant. Parents and schools need to do much more to help teenagers use it in moderation."],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (C1'e yakın)","Few inventions have transformed teenage life as dramatically as social media, and its impact remains fiercely debated.\n\nAdmittedly, it has made staying connected almost effortless. Friendships no longer end when someone moves to another city, and young people who feel isolated – perhaps because of their interests or identity – can find communities where they are accepted.\n\nNevertheless, the cost to mental health cannot be ignored. Platforms reward appearance and popularity, encouraging constant comparison. Many teenagers measure their worth in likes, and when the likes do not come, the consequences can be painful.\n\nWhat is perhaps less often discussed is the impact on attention. Short videos and endless feeds train the brain to expect constant stimulation, making it harder to focus on longer tasks such as reading a novel or revising for an exam.\n\nOn balance, I would argue that social media itself is neither good nor bad; the harm comes from unlimited, uncritical use. If young people were taught from an early age how these platforms work and how to set limits, they could enjoy the advantages while avoiding the worst of the risks."]]},{"id":"b2w2","part":1,"kind":"essay","t":"Tourism","in":"In your English class you have been talking about travel. Now your English teacher has asked you to write an essay. Write your essay using all the notes and giving reasons for your point of view. Write 140–190 words.","mail":"Is tourism good for the places that tourists visit?\n\nNotes – write about:\n1. jobs and money\n2. the environment\n3. ............ (your own idea)","pts":[["İş ve gelirden bahset",["jobs","money","economy","income","employment"]],["Çevreden bahset",["environment","pollution","rubbish","nature","damage"]],["Kendi fikrini ekle",["also","another","in addition","moreover","culture","local people"]],["Sonuç / görüş",["in conclusion","to sum up","overall","on balance","in my opinion"]]],"min":140,"max":190,"model":"Tourism is one of the largest industries in the world, but its effects on popular destinations are not always positive.\n\nThe most obvious advantage is economic. Tourism creates jobs in hotels, restaurants and shops, and the money visitors spend can be used to improve roads, hospitals and schools. For many small towns, it is the main source of income.\n\nHowever, mass tourism can seriously damage the environment. Beaches become polluted, wildlife is disturbed and huge amounts of water and energy are used in areas where they are already scarce.\n\nIn addition, local people sometimes suffer. When too many apartments are rented to tourists, housing becomes too expensive for residents, and traditional neighbourhoods lose their character.\n\nIn conclusion, I believe tourism can be good for a place, but only if it is carefully managed. Limiting visitor numbers and investing tourist money in protecting nature would allow destinations to enjoy the benefits without destroying what makes them special.","more":[["Örnek 2 · karşı görüşle","Every summer, millions of tourists visit popular destinations, and local people are increasingly asking whether this is really good for them.\n\nOf course, tourism brings money and jobs. Hotels, restaurants and tour companies employ thousands of people, and many small businesses depend entirely on visitors. Without tourism, some coastal towns would have very little income.\n\nHowever, this money comes at a price. Popular beaches are often covered in rubbish, and new hotels are built on land that was once home to wildlife. In some places, there is not even enough water for residents during the busy season.\n\nAnother serious problem is the effect on everyday life. As more flats are rented to tourists, rents rise and young local people can no longer afford to live in their own neighbourhoods. Traditional shops are replaced by souvenir stalls.\n\nIn my opinion, tourism in its current form does more harm than good to many places. Governments should limit the number of visitors to the most crowded destinations and make sure that tourist money is used to protect the environment and support local communities."],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (C1'e yakın)","Tourism is often presented as a straightforward success story: visitors arrive, spend money and leave happy. The reality, however, is considerably more complicated.\n\nThere is no denying the economic benefits. Tourism generates employment, supports local crafts and can fund the restoration of historic buildings that might otherwise fall into ruin. For developing regions in particular, it can be a lifeline.\n\nYet the environmental consequences are often severe. Fragile ecosystems such as coral reefs and mountain trails are damaged by overuse, while the flights that bring tourists contribute significantly to climate change.\n\nPerhaps the most overlooked issue is cultural. When a town reshapes itself entirely around visitors' expectations, it risks losing the very authenticity that attracted them in the first place. Festivals become performances, and residents begin to feel like extras in their own home.\n\nUltimately, I believe tourism can benefit the places people visit, but only if it is managed with the long term in mind. Limiting numbers, encouraging travel outside peak season and ensuring that profits stay in the local community would go a long way towards achieving this balance."]]},{"id":"b2w3","part":2,"kind":"article","t":"My Perfect Weekend","in":"You see this announcement in an English-language magazine. Write your article in 140–190 words.","mail":"ARTICLES WANTED\nMy perfect weekend\nDescribe your idea of a perfect weekend. Where would you be? Who would you spend it with? What would you do?\nThe best articles will be published in our next issue.","pts":[["Nerede olacağını anlat",["would be","in a","by the","at a","somewhere"]],["Kiminle olacağını söyle",["with my","friends","family","alone","on my own"]],["Ne yapacağını anlat",["would","start","spend","then","after that"]],["Bir başlık kullan",["@title"]]],"min":140,"max":190,"model":"Mountains, books and no phone signal\n\nAsk most people about their perfect weekend and they will talk about parties or shopping. Mine would be very different.\n\nI would spend it in a small wooden cabin in the mountains, ideally somewhere without a phone signal. The only people I would invite are my two closest friends, who share my love of walking and long conversations.\n\nOn Saturday, we would get up early and climb to the top of the nearest peak, taking a picnic with us. After lunch, we would lie in the grass and watch the clouds – something I never have time for during the week. In the evening, we would cook a simple meal on a fire and talk until midnight.\n\nSunday would be a lazy day. I would sleep late, read a good novel by the window and maybe go for a swim in a cold mountain lake.\n\nIt may not sound exciting, but for me, a weekend like this would be the best way to recharge before another busy week.","more":[["Örnek 2 · karşı görüşle","A weekend in the city that never sleeps\n\nWhile some people dream of peaceful beaches, my perfect weekend would be the opposite: two days in a big, busy city like Istanbul with my three closest friends.\n\nWe would arrive on Friday evening and go straight to a rooftop restaurant with a view of the Bosphorus. After dinner, we would wander through the narrow streets, stopping for tea and listening to street musicians.\n\nOn Saturday, we would get up early – well, not too early! – and visit the Grand Bazaar, where we could practise our bargaining skills. In the afternoon, we would take a ferry to the Asian side, eat fish sandwiches by the water and watch the sunset.\n\nSunday would be for museums and, of course, more food. I would make sure we tried at least five different desserts.\n\nIt might sound exhausting, but for me there is nothing more exciting than exploring a new place with people who make me laugh. By Sunday night, I would be tired but completely happy."],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (C1'e yakın)","The art of doing absolutely nothing\n\nWe are constantly told to make the most of our free time: learn a language, run a marathon, see the world. My idea of a perfect weekend, however, is gloriously unproductive.\n\nI would spend it at my grandparents' house in a small village in the Black Sea mountains, where the phone signal is unreliable and nobody seems to be in a hurry. My only company would be my grandparents, their elderly cat and perhaps my younger brother, provided he left his games console at home.\n\nI would wake up without an alarm to the smell of fresh bread and spend the morning reading in the garden, surrounded by tea plants and mist. In the afternoon, I might help my grandfather in his vegetable garden, although I suspect I would mostly be listening to his stories about the village fifty years ago.\n\nEvenings would be spent around the stove, playing cards and eating far too much.\n\nIt may not sound exciting, but in a world that never stops, I think the greatest luxury of all is having time to slow down."]]},{"id":"b2w4","part":2,"kind":"review","t":"A TV Series","in":"You see this announcement on an English-language website. Write your review in 140–190 words.","mail":"REVIEWS WANTED\nA TV series worth watching\nHave you watched a TV series recently that you think others would enjoy? Write a review telling us what it is about, what you liked or disliked, and who you would recommend it to.","pts":[["Konusunu anlat",["about","story","follows","plot","set in"]],["Beğendiklerini / beğenmediklerini söyle",["liked","loved","enjoyed","however","disappointing","weak"]],["Kime tavsiye ettiğini söyle",["recommend","anyone who","fans of","if you"]]],"min":140,"max":190,"model":"A gripping mystery: The Lighthouse\n\nIf you are looking for something to watch on a long winter evening, The Lighthouse is an excellent choice.\n\nThe series is set on a small island off the coast of Scotland, where a young detective is sent to investigate the disappearance of a lighthouse keeper. As she talks to the island's few residents, she discovers that everyone seems to be hiding something.\n\nWhat I enjoyed most was the atmosphere. The wild landscapes and constant storms create a sense of isolation that keeps you on the edge of your seat. The acting is also superb, particularly the performance of the main character, who is both clever and vulnerable.\n\nMy only criticism is that the middle episodes are a little slow, and one or two storylines are never fully explained.\n\nOverall, I would highly recommend The Lighthouse to anyone who enjoys mysteries with complex characters. Just do not watch it alone at night!","more":[["Örnek 2 · karşı görüşle","Breaking news, broken hearts: Newsroom\n\nNewsroom, a drama about a group of young journalists working for a struggling newspaper, is one of the most gripping series I have watched this year.\n\nEach episode follows a different news story, from a political scandal to a missing child, but the real focus is on the characters and the difficult choices they face. Should they publish a story that is true but could ruin someone's life? How far should they go to get the truth?\n\nThe writing is sharp and often surprisingly funny, and the cast is excellent. I particularly enjoyed the relationship between the experienced editor and the ambitious new reporter, which is both touching and realistic.\n\nOn the negative side, the final season feels rushed, and some storylines are resolved too quickly. A few of the newsroom scenes are also rather unrealistic – I doubt real journalists have time for so many dramatic arguments!\n\nDespite these weaknesses, I would strongly recommend Newsroom to anyone who enjoys intelligent dramas with strong characters. It will also make you think twice about the news you read every day."],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (C1'e yakın)","Small town, big secrets: a review of Hollow Creek\n\nAt first glance, Hollow Creek appears to be yet another crime series set in a sleepy village. Within the first ten minutes, however, it becomes clear that this is something rather more ambitious.\n\nThe story begins when a respected local teacher disappears on the night of the annual summer festival. Rather than following the investigation from the police's point of view, each episode is told through the eyes of a different villager, gradually revealing how their lives – and lies – are connected.\n\nWhat makes the series stand out is its patience. The director is not afraid of long, silent scenes, and the stunning landscape becomes almost a character in itself. The performances are uniformly strong, although the young actor playing the teacher's son deserves particular praise for a remarkably subtle performance.\n\nThat said, the slow pace will not suit everyone, and the final twist, while clever, stretches belief somewhat.\n\nAll in all, Hollow Creek is a thoughtful, beautifully made series that rewards viewers who are willing to pay attention. Fans of fast-moving action may be disappointed, but anyone who enjoys complex characters will be hooked."]]},{"id":"b2w5","part":2,"kind":"email","t":"Advice on a Summer Job","in":"You have received this email from your English-speaking friend Chris. Write your email in 140–190 words.","mail":"From: Chris\nI'm thinking of getting a summer job this year, but I'm not sure what kind of job would suit me. You had a summer job last year, didn't you? What did you do, and what did you learn from it? What advice would you give me?\nThanks,\nChris","pts":[["Ne iş yaptığını anlat",["worked","job","last summer","as a"]],["Ne öğrendiğini söyle",["learned","learnt","taught me","realised"]],["Tavsiye ver",["should","advice","recommend","if i were you","why don't you","make sure"]]],"min":140,"max":190,"model":"Hi Chris,\nGreat to hear from you! Getting a summer job is a brilliant idea – I'm sure you won't regret it.\n\nLast summer I worked as a waiter in a busy seaside restaurant. To be honest, the first week was a nightmare. I kept forgetting orders and once I dropped a whole tray of drinks! But I soon got the hang of it.\n\nThe job taught me a lot. I learned how to stay calm under pressure and how to deal with difficult customers politely. I also realised how important teamwork is – we only survived the busy evenings because we helped each other.\n\nAs for advice, I think you should choose a job where you meet lots of people, because you're so sociable. If I were you, I'd start applying now, as the best jobs go quickly. And make sure you ask about the working hours before you accept anything – some places expect you to work very late.\n\nGood luck, and let me know what you decide!\nBest wishes,\nDeniz","more":[["Örnek 2 · karşı görüşle","Hi Chris,\nThanks for your email. It's great that you're thinking about getting a summer job – I learned so much from mine.\n\nLast summer I worked at a children's summer camp near the lake. I helped organise games, took the kids swimming and made sure everyone was safe. It was exhausting, especially when it rained and thirty children were stuck indoors!\n\nThe most important thing I learned was patience. Some children were homesick or didn't want to join in, and I had to find ways to help them feel comfortable. I also became much more confident about speaking in front of groups.\n\nIf I were you, I'd think about what you enjoy before choosing. You love being outdoors, so maybe a job at a campsite or a sports centre would suit you. Also, try to get some experience that will look good on your CV – employers really value it.\n\nOne last piece of advice: don't forget to have some fun too. Make sure you have at least a couple of weeks free at the end of summer.\n\nGood luck!\nSelin"],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (C1'e yakın)","Hi Chris,\nWhat a great idea! I'm sure a summer job will be a brilliant experience, even if it's hard work at times.\n\nAs you know, last summer I worked as an assistant in a small bookshop in the old town. I mainly served customers and arranged displays, but I also helped organise a couple of events with local authors, which was the highlight for me.\n\nThe job taught me far more than I expected. I discovered that I'm better at dealing with people than I thought, even the occasional rude customer! I also learned how a small business actually works – how much planning goes into ordering stock, for example – which has made me consider studying business at university.\n\nAs for advice, my main suggestion would be to choose something that might help you in the future, rather than simply the job that pays the most. Since you're interested in design, why not ask local studios or print shops whether they need help? Even if they don't advertise, they might be happy to take on someone enthusiastic.\n\nWhatever you choose, I'm sure you'll do brilliantly. Let me know how it goes!\nBest wishes,\nDeniz"]]}],"S":[{"id":"b2s1","p":1,"t":"Habits and Routines","ins":"Her soruya birkaç cümleyle, nedenler ve örnekler vererek cevap ver.","q":[["How do you usually spend your evenings?",30,["usually","tend to","unless"],["On weekdays, I tend to spend most evenings at home studying, although I always try to take a break to have dinner with my family. If I've finished my homework early, I might go for a walk or meet a friend for a coffee. On Fridays, we usually watch a film together, which is a nice way to start the weekend.","My evenings are quite busy, actually. Three times a week I go to football training, which finishes at about eight. After that, I'm usually too tired to do much, so I just eat, chat with my friends online and maybe watch an episode of a series before bed."]],["Do you prefer reading books or watching films? Why?",30,["I prefer","whereas","because"],["I'd say I prefer reading, mainly because I can use my imagination. When I read, I picture the characters and places in my own way, whereas films sometimes disappoint me if they're different from what I expected. Having said that, films are better when I'm tired and just want to relax.","To be honest, I prefer films. I spend a lot of time reading for school, so in my free time I want something more relaxing. I also love the music and the visual effects in films – a good soundtrack can make a story so much more powerful."]],["What's something new you'd like to learn in the future?",30,["I'd love to","I've always wanted","one day"],["I've always wanted to learn to sail. My uncle has a small boat on the coast, and every summer he promises to teach me, but we never seem to find the time. Being out on the open sea, completely in control of the boat, must be an amazing feeling.","I'd really like to learn Japanese. I'm a big fan of Japanese films and food, and I'm hoping to visit Japan after university. I know it's a difficult language, especially the writing, but I think the challenge would make it even more rewarding."]],["How important is it for you to have a routine?",30,["quite important","otherwise","I find that"],["Quite important, actually. I find that if I don't have a routine, I waste a lot of time deciding what to do and end up stressed before exams. Having fixed times for studying, exercise and sleep makes me more productive, and paradoxically gives me more free time.","Not very, to be honest. I get bored if every day is the same, so I prefer to plan my week loosely and decide things as I go. Of course, I need some routine for school, but at the weekends I like to be spontaneous."]]]},{"id":"b2s2","p":2,"t":"Ways of Learning","ins":"İki fotoğrafı 1 dakika karşılaştır ve soruyu cevapla.","img":[["👩‍🏫📚🧑‍🎓","Fotoğraf 1: Bir sınıfta öğretmen tahtada ders anlatıyor, öğrenciler not alıyor.","b2-classroom"],["🧑‍💻🎧🏠","Fotoğraf 2: Bir genç evde kulaklıkla bilgisayardan çevrim içi ders izliyor.","b2-online"]],"q":[["Your photographs show people learning in different ways. Compare the photographs, and say what the advantages of learning in each way might be.",60,["both photos","whereas","in contrast","might","I suppose","seems to"],["Both photos show people learning, but in quite different settings. In the first one, there's a traditional classroom, with a teacher explaining something on the board and students taking notes, whereas in the second, a young man seems to be following an online lesson at home, wearing headphones. One advantage of the classroom is that students can ask questions immediately and learn from each other through discussion. The teacher can also see when someone is confused. In contrast, learning online is much more flexible. The young man can probably study whenever he wants and watch the lesson again if he doesn't understand something. I suppose he also saves time and money on travelling. However, he might find it harder to stay motivated without other people around him.","These two photographs both show ways of learning, but the atmosphere is very different. The classroom looks lively and quite formal – the students are sitting in rows and paying attention to the teacher. By contrast, the young man at home looks relaxed, sitting at his desk with a cup of coffee. I think the main advantage of learning in a classroom is the social side: you make friends, you can work in groups and you feel part of a community. Learning online, on the other hand, allows you to work at your own speed and choose exactly what you want to study. It's probably ideal for people who work or live far from a school."]],["Which way of learning do you prefer? Why?",30,["I'd go for","I prefer","because"],["I'd go for the classroom, mainly because I find it hard to concentrate on my own – I get distracted by my phone far too easily. I also enjoy discussing ideas with my classmates, which helps me remember things better.","Personally, I prefer learning online. I'm quite an independent person, and I like being able to pause the lesson, take notes and go back over the difficult parts as many times as I need. It also means I can fit my studies around other things, like sport or a part-time job."]]]},{"id":"b2s3","p":2,"t":"Celebrations","ins":"İki fotoğrafı 1 dakika karşılaştır ve soruyu cevapla.","img":[["🎉🎂👨‍👩‍👧‍👦","Fotoğraf 1: Evde, kalabalık bir ailenin doğum günü kutlaması.","b2-birthday"],["🎆🎶🏙️","Fotoğraf 2: Şehir meydanında havai fişekli büyük bir yılbaşı kutlaması.","b2-fireworks"]],"q":[["Your photographs show people celebrating in different ways. Compare the photographs, and say why the people might be celebrating like this.",60,["both","while","perhaps","might be","I imagine"],["Both photographs show people celebrating, but the scale and atmosphere are completely different. The first one shows a family birthday party at home, with a big cake, balloons and lots of relatives around a table, while the second shows a huge crowd watching fireworks in a city square, probably on New Year's Eve. The family might be celebrating at home because it feels more personal – they can talk, share food and spend quality time together. It also seems quite relaxed and informal. The people in the city, on the other hand, perhaps want to feel part of something bigger. There's something exciting about counting down with thousands of strangers, and the fireworks and music create an atmosphere you can't get at home.","In the first picture, a family is celebrating a birthday in a living room, whereas the second picture shows a big public celebration with fireworks over a city. I imagine the family chose to celebrate at home because they want to make the person whose birthday it is feel special, and perhaps because there are young children and elderly relatives who might not enjoy a crowd. The people in the city are probably celebrating a national holiday or New Year, and they've come out to enjoy the spectacle together. Big events like that make people feel connected to their community, even if they don't know anyone around them."]],["Which kind of celebration would you prefer? Why?",30,["I'd rather","I prefer","because"],["I'd rather celebrate at home with my family. Big crowds make me a bit nervous, and I prefer having real conversations with people I care about. Also, the food is always better at home! And honestly, I think the best memories come from small, personal moments rather than big public events.","I'd probably choose the city celebration. I love the energy of big events, and watching fireworks with thousands of people is an experience you remember for years. Home celebrations are lovely, but they can feel quite similar from year to year."]]]},{"id":"b2s4","p":3,"t":"A School Trip","ins":"Önce seçenekleri tartış (2 dakika), sonra karar ver (1 dakika).","opts":["🏛️ a history museum","🏭 a factory","🌲 a national park","🎭 a theatre","🔬 a science centre"],"q":[["Imagine your school is planning a day trip for students. Here are some places they could visit. Talk about what students could learn from visiting these places.",120,["let's start with","what about","I think","that's true, but","students would learn"],["Let's start with the history museum. Students could learn about the past in a much more memorable way than from a textbook, especially if there are interactive exhibitions. A factory visit might sound boring, but it would show students how everyday things are actually made and what kinds of jobs exist, which could help them think about their future careers. The national park would be great for learning about nature and the environment, and students would get some fresh air and exercise too. A theatre trip could help students understand literature better – seeing a play performed is very different from reading it in class. Finally, a science centre is usually very hands-on, so students learn by doing experiments themselves, which is probably the most effective way to learn. I think each place has something to offer, but it depends on the age of the students and what they're studying at the moment.","I'll begin with the theatre. It's a great way to make literature come alive, and many students have never been to a real theatre, so it would be a new experience for them. The science centre is fun and educational, though some can be a bit childish for older students. The history museum is a classic choice; it's informative, but students might get bored if they just look at objects behind glass. A factory is interesting because it connects school subjects like chemistry or engineering to the real world. And the national park would teach students about ecosystems and environmental protection, and it would also be good for team building, since students would have to work together on activities like orienteering. So there are clear benefits to all of them. The question is really which one would offer the most to the greatest number of students, and whether the cost and travel time would be reasonable. A trip that's too far away might mean students spend more time on the bus than actually learning."]],["Now decide which place would be the best for a school trip.",60,["I think we should","the best option","because","although"],["I think the best option would be the science centre, because it's both educational and entertaining, and students of all ages would enjoy it. Students learn best when they're actively involved, and a science centre allows them to experiment and ask questions. The national park would be my second choice, although it depends a lot on the weather. Another advantage of the science centre is that it works well for mixed groups: students who aren't very academic often enjoy practical activities more than lessons, so everyone would get something out of it.","For me, the national park is the clear winner. Students spend most of their time indoors looking at screens, so a day outside would be really valuable. They'd learn about nature, get some exercise and probably talk to classmates they don't normally spend time with. It would also be relatively cheap, since entrance to most national parks is free, and teachers could organise activities linked to biology and geography lessons so that the day still has a clear educational purpose."]]]},{"id":"b2s5","p":4,"t":"Education","ins":"Görüşünü nedenler ve örneklerle açıkla.","q":[["Do you think students learn more outside the classroom than inside it?",45,["in some ways","however","for instance","I'd argue"],["In some ways, yes. For instance, you learn teamwork from playing sport, responsibility from part-time jobs and confidence from travelling or meeting new people. These are things that are difficult to teach in a classroom. However, I'd argue that the classroom is still essential for building basic knowledge, like maths, science and languages. Ideally, schools should combine both.","I don't think so. Outside the classroom, learning is often random – you might learn useful things, but you might also learn nothing at all. In a classroom, teachers plan lessons carefully so that knowledge builds step by step. Without that structure, most students would have big gaps in their education. Of course, experiences outside school are valuable, but they work best when they build on what students have already learned in class."]],["Should school days be shorter so that students have more free time?",45,["I'm not convinced","on the one hand","on the other hand"],["I'm not convinced. On the one hand, students would be less tired and have more time for hobbies and sport, which is good for their mental health. On the other hand, many of them would probably just spend the extra time on their phones. Also, if the school day were shorter, teachers would have to cover the same material in less time, which could increase stress.","Yes, I think they should. Many students are at school until late afternoon and then have hours of homework, so they have almost no free time. Shorter days would give them time to rest, exercise and pursue interests like music or art, which are just as important for their development. Some countries with shorter school days, like Finland, still achieve excellent results, so it's clearly possible."]],["How might education change in the next twenty years?",45,["I imagine","is likely to","will probably","might"],["I imagine technology is likely to play a much bigger role. Lessons will probably be more personalised, with programs adapting to each student's level and speed. Students might also learn more through virtual reality, for example visiting ancient Rome in a history lesson. But I think teachers will still be needed to motivate students and help them develop social skills.","I think there'll be a bigger focus on practical skills, like problem solving, communication and managing money, rather than memorising facts, since everyone can look up information instantly. Exams might also change, with more projects and less pressure on a single test at the end of the year. I think that would be fairer, because some students perform badly under exam pressure even when they understand the subject well."]]]}]},"C1":{"R":[{"k":"R1","nm":"Okuma 1 · Çoktan seçmeli boşluk","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 1","ins":"Metni oku ve her boşluk için en uygun kelimeyi (A, B, C ya da D) seç.","id":"c1r1","t":"Digital Minimalism","ty":"cloze","title":"Digital minimalism","p":"A growing number of people are choosing to (1) ______ back on their use of technology. So-called digital minimalists do not reject technology outright; rather, they are (2) ______ about which tools they allow into their lives. The movement has (3) ______ particular popularity among young professionals who feel that constant notifications are taking a (4) ______ on their concentration. Advocates argue that we have drifted into habits without ever consciously (5) ______ whether they serve us. Critics, (6) ______, contend that the movement is something of a luxury, available mainly to those whose jobs do not (7) ______ them to be constantly reachable. Whatever its limitations, digital minimalism has undoubtedly (8) ______ a nerve.","o":[[["cut","set","hold","take"],0,["collocation"]],[["selective","elective","select","selected"],0,["vocab"]],[["got","gained","reached","made"],1,["collocation"]],[["cost","price","toll","charge"],2,["collocation"]],[["questioning","arguing","disputing","objecting"],0,["vocab"]],[["meanwhile","therefore","likewise","furthermore"],0,["advanced-linkers"]],[["demand","insist","require","press"],2,["vocab"]],[["struck","beaten","reached","met"],0,["collocation"]]],"e":["cut back on: azaltmak.","selective about: seçici.","gain popularity: popülerlik kazanmak.","take a toll on: olumsuz etkilemek, yıpratmak.","question whether: -ip -mediğini sorgulamak.","meanwhile: öte yandan (karşı görüşe geçiş).","require someone to do: -mesini gerektirmek.","strike a nerve: hassas bir noktaya dokunmak."]},{"k":"R3","nm":"Okuma 3 · Kelime türetme","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 3","ins":"Her boşluk için verilen kök kelimeyi uygun biçime getir.","id":"c1r3","t":"Bilingualism and the Brain","ty":"open","title":"Bilingualism and the brain","st":["ABLE","ADVANTAGE","STAND","FLEXIBLE","CONVINCE","APPEAR","COMPARE","DENY"],"p":"For much of the twentieth century, raising children with two languages was thought to hinder their (1) ______ to learn. Today, the picture looks very different, and many researchers argue that bilingualism confers clear cognitive (2) ______. Bilingual people, it is claimed, have an (3) ______ ability to switch between tasks, a sign of greater mental (4) ______. However, recent studies have failed to replicate some of the more (5) ______ early findings. In some cases, the (6) ______ benefits seem to disappear when researchers take into account factors such as income and education. Still, even sceptics agree that, (7) ______ speaking, learning another language does no harm, and the cultural benefits are (8) ______.","a":[["ability"],["advantages"],["outstanding"],["flexibility"],["convincing"],["apparent"],["comparatively","relatively"],["undeniable"]],"tg":[["word-formation"],["word-formation"],["word-formation"],["word-formation"],["word-formation"],["word-formation"],["word-formation"],["word-formation"]],"e":["their + isim: ability.","confer advantages: avantaj sağlamak (çoğul).","an + sıfat: outstanding (üstün).","greater + isim: flexibility.","more + sıfat: convincing (ikna edici).","the + sıfat + benefits: apparent (görünürdeki).","comparatively speaking: göreceli olarak.","are + sıfat, olumlu anlam: undeniable (inkâr edilemez)."]},{"k":"R4","nm":"Okuma 4 · Cümle dönüştürme","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 4","ins":"İkinci cümleyi, birinciyle aynı anlamı verecek şekilde tamamla. Verilen kelimeyi değiştirmeden kullan; 3–6 kelime yaz.","id":"c1r4","t":"Key Word Transformations","ty":"kwt","q":[{"s1":"I had never seen such a beautiful sunset before.","key":"HAD","s2":"Never before ______ such a beautiful sunset.","a":["had i seen"],"tg":["inversion"],"e":"Never before + devrik: had I seen.","mx":6},{"s1":"It's possible that the train has been delayed.","key":"MAY","s2":"The train ______ delayed.","a":["may have been"],"tg":["modals-deduction"],"e":"may have been + V3.","mx":6},{"s1":"He was extremely tired, but he kept working.","key":"DESPITE","s2":"He kept working ______ extremely tired.","a":["despite being","despite the fact that he was"],"tg":["linkers-contrast-purpose"],"e":"despite + -ing / despite the fact that.","mx":6},{"s1":"\"You should really try the fish,\" the waiter said to us.","key":"RECOMMENDED","s2":"The waiter ______ the fish.","a":["recommended that we try","recommended trying","recommended that we should try","recommended us to try"],"tg":["reporting-verbs"],"e":"recommend + -ing / recommend that + özne + (should) fiil.","mx":6},{"s1":"As soon as she arrived, the meeting started.","key":"SOONER","s2":"No ______ than the meeting started.","a":["sooner had she arrived"],"tg":["inversion"],"e":"No sooner had + özne + V3 + than.","mx":6},{"s1":"It doesn't matter how hard you try, you won't convince him.","key":"HOWEVER","s2":"You won't convince him, ______ try.","a":["however hard you","however hard you may"],"tg":["advanced-linkers"],"e":"however + sıfat/zarf: ne kadar ... olursa olsun.","mx":6}]},{"k":"R7","nm":"Okuma 7 · Paragraf yerleştirme","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 7","ins":"Metinden altı paragraf çıkarıldı. Her boşluk için uygun paragrafı (A–G) seç. Bir paragraf fazladır.","id":"c1r7","t":"Walking the Length of a Country","ty":"gap","title":"Walking the length of a country","p":"Last spring I set out to walk the entire length of Britain, from Land's End to John o' Groats, a distance of roughly 1,200 miles. I had no particular reason, other than a growing sense that I had spent too much of my life looking at screens.\n(1) ______\nThe first week was brutal. My boots, which I had foolishly bought just before setting off, gave me blisters so painful that I considered giving up on the third day.\n(2) ______\nBy the second week, however, something had shifted. My body had adjusted, and I began to notice things I would normally have rushed past.\n(3) ______\nThe people I met along the way were another revelation. Farmers invited me in for tea; a retired couple let me sleep in their spare room when a storm made camping impossible.\n(4) ______\nThere were low moments, of course. Somewhere in the north of England, after five consecutive days of rain, I sat in a bus shelter and wondered what on earth I was doing.\n(5) ______\nWhen I finally reached the northern tip of Scotland, I felt less triumph than a strange sadness that it was over.\n(6) ______","opts":[["A","A kind man in a nearby pharmacy, seeing me limp in, spent half an hour showing me how to tape my feet properly. I have never been so grateful to a stranger."],["B","I had imagined that the walk would be a solitary experience, but it turned out to be anything but."],["C","The way the landscape changed gradually from one region to the next, the different accents, even the shapes of the hedges – all of it became fascinating."],["D","It was a text from my sister, telling me she was proud of me, that persuaded me to keep going."],["E","Friends were sceptical. Most assumed I would last a week before catching a train home."],["F","Since returning, I have tried to hold on to the slower pace of those months, with mixed success."],["G","I had trained for months beforehand, walking twenty miles every weekend."]],"a":["E","A","C","B","D","F"],"tg":[["coherence"],["reference"],["coherence"],["coherence"],["coherence"],["coherence"]],"e":["Yola çıkış nedeni → arkadaşlar şüpheliydi: E.","Su toplamış ayaklar → eczacı ayaklarını bantlamayı gösterdi: A.","\"notice things I would normally have rushed past\" → manzara, aksanlar: C.","Yardımsever insanlar paragrafından sonra: \"Yalnız bir deneyim olacağını sanmıştım ama hiç de öyle olmadı\": B.","Durakta ne yaptığını sorguladı → kız kardeşinin mesajı devam ettirdi: D.","Bitiş → dönünce yavaş tempoyu korumaya çalıştı: F. G (aylarca antrenman) aceleyle alınmış botlarla çelişir; fazladır."]},{"k":"R8","nm":"Okuma 8 · Çoklu eşleştirme","ti":"Reading and Use of English Part 8","ins":"Metin bölümlerini (A–E) oku. Her soru için doğru bölümü seç. Bölümler birden fazla kez seçilebilir.","id":"c1r8","t":"Four Cities","ty":"match","opts":[["A","Lisbon","I moved to Lisbon for the light and stayed for the people. What I hadn't expected was how difficult it would be to find affordable housing – prices have risen sharply as more foreigners like me have arrived, which makes me feel slightly uncomfortable."],["B","Seoul","Seoul is extraordinarily efficient; everything from the metro to online banking works seamlessly. The pace, though, can be exhausting, and it took me a long time to build friendships, partly because colleagues tend to socialise only within work."],["C","Vancouver","Living in Vancouver means having mountains and ocean on your doorstep. I spend almost every weekend hiking. The downside is the rain – months of it – which affects my mood far more than I anticipated."],["D","Cape Town","Cape Town is breathtakingly beautiful, and I have never felt so creative. But the inequality is impossible to ignore, and I have had to think hard about my own position as a relatively privileged newcomer."]],"q":[{"q":"Who feels some guilt about their effect on the local area?","a":"A","tg":["inference"],"e":"Yabancılar geldikçe fiyatlar arttı; kendini rahatsız hissediyor."},{"q":"Who was surprised by how the climate affected them?","a":"C","tg":["detail"],"e":"Yağmur moralini beklediğinden çok etkiliyor."},{"q":"Who found it hard to form social connections?","a":"B","tg":["detail"],"e":"Arkadaşlık kurmak uzun sürdü."},{"q":"Who mentions a positive effect on their work or creativity?","a":"D","tg":["detail"],"e":"\"I have never felt so creative\"."},{"q":"Who praises the city's practical systems?","a":"B","tg":["detail"],"e":"Metro ve bankacılık sorunsuz."},{"q":"Who has reflected on social differences in the city?","a":"D","tg":["inference"],"e":"Eşitsizlik ve kendi ayrıcalıklı konumu."}]}],"L":[{"k":"L1","nm":"Dinleme 1 · Kısa kayıtlar","ti":"Listening Part 1","ins":"Üç farklı kısa kayıt dinleyeceksin; her biri için iki soru var. En uygun cevabı seç.","id":"c1l1","t":"A Podcast","ty":"mc","q":[{"id":"c1l1a","sc":[["F","When we started the podcast, we assumed listeners wanted expert interviews. It turned out they much preferred episodes where we just talked through our own confusion about a topic. It made the whole thing feel more like a conversation between friends."],["M","Which, honestly, made it a lot less work to prepare."]],"q":"What did the speakers discover about their listeners?","o":["They preferred more informal episodes.","They wanted more expert guests.","They found the expert interviews too difficult to follow."],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"Uzman röportajı yerine kendi kafa karışıklıklarını konuştukları bölümleri tercih ettiler."},{"id":"c1l1b","sc":[["F","When we started the podcast, we assumed listeners wanted expert interviews. It turned out they much preferred episodes where we just talked through our own confusion about a topic. It made the whole thing feel more like a conversation between friends."],["M","Which, honestly, made it a lot less work to prepare."]],"q":"What does the man suggest about this discovery?","o":["It was disappointing.","It had a practical advantage.","It reduced their number of listeners."],"a":1,"tg":["inference"],"e":"Hazırlığı çok kolaylaştırdı."}]},{"k":"L2","nm":"Dinleme 2 · Cümle tamamlama","ti":"Listening Part 2","ins":"Bir konuşma dinleyeceksin. Cümleleri bir kelime ya da kısa bir ifadeyle tamamla.","id":"c1l2","t":"An Ancient Shipwreck","ty":"open","title":"Cümleler","sc":[["M","The wreck we discovered lies at a depth of about forty metres off the coast of Sicily. It's a Roman merchant ship, probably dating from the first century. What makes it exceptional is the state of the cargo: hundreds of clay jars, known as amphorae, most of them still sealed. Initially, we assumed they would contain wine, which was the most commonly traded goods. In fact, analysis showed they were filled with fish sauce, a kind of Roman ketchup that was hugely popular. The ship was probably heading for Spain when it sank, possibly in a sudden storm. Excavation is extremely slow – each dive lasts only twenty minutes because of the depth. The biggest threat to the site isn't the sea, surprisingly, but illegal divers who steal objects to sell to collectors. To prevent this, the site is now monitored by underwater cameras."]],"p":"The ship was found at a depth of around (1) ______ metres.\nThe clay jars on board are called (2) ______.\nThe team first thought the jars contained (3) ______.\nThe jars actually contained a kind of (4) ______.\nThe ship may have sunk during a (5) ______.\nEach dive lasts only (6) ______ minutes.\nThe main threat to the site comes from (7) ______.\nThe site is now protected by underwater (8) ______.","a":[["40","forty"],["amphorae"],["wine"],["fish sauce","sauce"],["storm","sudden storm"],["20","twenty"],["illegal divers","divers"],["cameras"]],"tg":[["spelling"],["spelling"],["detail"],["detail"],["detail"],["spelling"],["detail"],["detail"]],"e":["\"a depth of about forty metres\".","\"known as amphorae\".","\"we assumed they would contain wine\".","\"filled with fish sauce\".","\"possibly in a sudden storm\".","\"each dive lasts only twenty minutes\".","\"illegal divers who steal objects\".","\"monitored by underwater cameras\"."]},{"k":"L3","nm":"Dinleme 3 · Röportaj","ti":"Listening Part 3","ins":"Bir röportaj dinleyeceksin. Her soru için en uygun cevabı seç.","id":"c1l3","t":"Procrastination","ty":"mc","sc":[["F","Today I'm talking to psychologist Dr Sam Okafor about procrastination. Sam, why do we put things off?"],["M","The common assumption is that procrastinators are lazy, but that's rarely the case. Procrastination is mainly about managing emotions. We avoid tasks that make us feel anxious, bored or inadequate, and we get a short-term sense of relief."],["F","So it's not a time-management problem?"],["M","Not primarily. You can give someone the best planner in the world, and if the task still makes them feel bad, they'll find a way to avoid it. That's why so many productivity apps fail."],["F","What actually helps?"],["M","Self-compassion, surprisingly. Studies show that students who forgave themselves for procrastinating on one exam procrastinated less on the next. Being harsh on yourself just adds to the negative feelings that caused the problem."],["F","Is there any upside to procrastination?"],["M","Some people argue that a bit of delay allows ideas to develop, and there's something to that. But I'd be wary of using it as an excuse. Delaying because you're thinking is very different from delaying because you're avoiding."]],"q":[{"q":"According to Sam, procrastination is mainly caused by","o":["a lack of motivation and self-discipline.","a desire to avoid unpleasant feelings.","poor planning skills.","having too much work."],"a":1,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"Procrastination is mainly about managing emotions\"."},{"q":"What does Sam say about productivity apps?","o":["They are useful for most people.","They often fail to address the real problem.","They make procrastination worse by adding pressure.","They are too complicated."],"a":1,"tg":["inference"],"e":"Görev kötü hissettirdiği sürece en iyi planlayıcı da işe yaramaz."},{"q":"What does Sam recommend?","o":["being kinder to oneself","setting stricter deadlines","working with other people","rewarding oneself after work"],"a":0,"tg":["detail"],"e":"\"Self-compassion\"."},{"q":"What is Sam's attitude to the idea that procrastination can be useful?","o":["He completely rejects it.","He accepts it without reservation.","He sees some truth in it but has doubts.","He thinks it applies only to creative people."],"a":2,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"\"there's something to that. But I'd be wary\"."}]},{"k":"L4","nm":"Dinleme 4 · Eşleştirme (görev 1)","ti":"Listening Part 4","ins":"Beş kişinin konuşmasını dinleyeceksin. Görev 1: Her konuşmacının bu işi seçme nedenini (A–H) bul.","id":"c1l4","t":"A New Hobby","ty":"match","sc":[["F","Speaker one. I took up pottery after a stressful year at work. A colleague mentioned a class and I thought, why not? What I love is that you can't think about anything else while you're working the clay."],["M","#1"],["M","Speaker two. I started rock climbing because my son wanted to try it and needed an adult with him. Now I go more than he does! It's the problem-solving I enjoy – every route is like a puzzle."],["F","#1"],["F2","Speaker three. Learning the violin at fifty was something I'd promised myself for years. I'm not very good, but I love the challenge of slowly getting better."],["M","#1"],["M2","Speaker four. I joined a choir after moving to a new town, mainly to meet people. And it worked – most of my friends here are from the choir."],["F","#1"],["F","Speaker five. I began sea swimming after reading about its health benefits. It's freezing, but the feeling afterwards is incredible – I feel more alive than at any other time."]],"opts":[["A","to keep a promise to themselves"],["B","to make friends"],["C","to improve their health"],["D","to accompany a family member"],["E","to relieve stress"],["F","to impress others"],["G","to start a new career"],["H","to spend less money"]],"q":[{"q":"Speaker 1","a":"E","tg":["gist"],"e":"Stresli bir yıldan sonra başladı."},{"q":"Speaker 2","a":"D","tg":["gist"],"e":"Oğlunun yanında bir yetişkin olması gerekiyordu."},{"q":"Speaker 3","a":"A","tg":["gist"],"e":"Yıllardır kendine verdiği söz."},{"q":"Speaker 4","a":"B","tg":["gist"],"e":"Yeni bir kasabada insanlarla tanışmak için."},{"q":"Speaker 5","a":"C","tg":["gist"],"e":"Sağlık faydalarını okuduktan sonra."}]},{"k":"L1","nm":"Dinleme 1 · Kısa kayıtlar","ti":"Listening Part 1","ins":"Üç farklı kısa kayıt dinleyeceksin; her biri için iki soru var. En uygun cevabı seç.","id":"c1l5","t":"Two Colleagues","ty":"mc","q":[{"id":"c1l5a","sc":[["M","I read your report on the new software. I agree with your conclusions, but I think you were a bit too diplomatic about the problems."],["F","Maybe. I didn't want to upset the IT team – they've worked so hard on it."],["M","I understand, but if the board doesn't realise how serious the issues are, they won't fund the fixes."]],"q":"What does the man think about the woman's report?","o":["It was not critical enough.","Its conclusions were wrong.","It was too long."],"a":0,"tg":["attitude"],"e":"\"a bit too diplomatic about the problems\"."},{"id":"c1l5b","sc":[["M","I read your report on the new software. I agree with your conclusions, but I think you were a bit too diplomatic about the problems."],["F","Maybe. I didn't want to upset the IT team – they've worked so hard on it."],["M","I understand, but if the board doesn't realise how serious the issues are, they won't fund the fixes."]],"q":"Why is the man concerned?","o":["The IT team might be offended.","The problems may not receive enough money.","The whole software project may be cancelled."],"a":1,"tg":["inference","first-conditional"],"e":"Yönetim sorunların ciddiyetini anlamazsa düzeltmeleri finanse etmeyecek."}]}],"W":[{"id":"c1w1","part":1,"kind":"essay","t":"Public Support for the Arts","in":"Your class has watched a discussion about how governments should support the arts. Write an essay discussing TWO of the methods in your notes, explaining which you think is more important and giving reasons. Write 220–260 words.","mail":"Ways governments can support the arts:\n• funding museums and galleries\n• providing arts education in schools\n• giving grants to individual artists\n\nOpinions expressed:\n\"Museums should be free so that everyone can enjoy them.\"\n\"Children who never learn an instrument miss out on so much.\"\n\"Artists shouldn't depend on government money.\"","pts":[["İlk yöntemi tartış",["museum","gallery","education","school","grant","artist"]],["İkinci yöntemi tartış",["whereas","on the other hand","by contrast","turning to","second"]],["Hangisinin daha önemli olduğunu gerekçelendir",["more important","in my view","i would argue","on balance","ultimately"]],["Akademik bağlaçlar",["however","furthermore","moreover","nevertheless","consequently"]]],"min":220,"max":260,"model":"Public money for the arts is often among the first things to be cut when budgets are tight. Yet there are strong arguments for continued support, particularly through funding museums and providing arts education in schools.\n\nFunding museums and galleries ensures that a country's cultural heritage is preserved and accessible. When entry is free, as it is in many British museums, visitor numbers rise dramatically and include people who would never otherwise attend. However, free museums mainly benefit those who live nearby and already have an interest in culture; they do little to create new audiences.\n\nArts education in schools, by contrast, reaches every child, regardless of background. Learning an instrument, acting in a play or studying painting develops creativity, confidence and the ability to collaborate – skills that are valuable far beyond the arts. Moreover, children who discover a passion for the arts early are likely to become the museum visitors, concert-goers and even artists of the future.\n\nOn balance, I would argue that arts education is the more important of the two. While museums preserve the culture of the past, education creates the culture of the future. Without it, the arts risk becoming the preserve of a privileged minority who can afford private lessons. That is not to say museums should be neglected, but if governments must prioritise, investing in children's creative development is likely to have the greater long-term impact on society as a whole.","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı yöntemleri seçerek","When public budgets are under pressure, funding for the arts is often regarded as an easy target. However, there are compelling reasons for governments to continue supporting culture, notably through grants to individual artists and arts education in schools.\n\nGrants to individual artists allow creative work to be produced that the market alone would not support. Experimental theatre, contemporary music and poetry rarely generate enough income to sustain their creators, yet they often shape the culture of the future. Critics argue that artists should not depend on public money, and there is some truth in this: grants can encourage work that appeals to funding committees rather than audiences. Nevertheless, without some form of support, only those with private wealth could afford to pursue an artistic career.\n\nArts education, by contrast, benefits society as a whole. Every child, regardless of background, gains the opportunity to discover a talent or simply develop the confidence that comes from performing or creating. These skills – creativity, collaboration, self-expression – are increasingly valued in the workplace.\n\nOf the two, I believe education is the more important priority. Grants support a relatively small number of professionals, whereas arts education reaches millions of young people and creates the audiences, and indeed the artists, of tomorrow. If governments must choose, investing in schools is likely to produce the broader and more lasting benefit, although ideally a modest grant system should be maintained alongside it to ensure that talent from all backgrounds can flourish."],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (C2'ye yakın)","It has become fashionable in some quarters to dismiss public spending on the arts as a luxury that hard-pressed governments can ill afford. Such a view, however, underestimates the extent to which a nation's cultural life depends on deliberate investment. Two forms of support – funding museums and galleries, and subsidising individual artists – illustrate the dilemma particularly well.\n\nPublicly funded museums are, in many respects, the cornerstone of cultural democracy. Free admission, where it exists, has been shown to transform visitor numbers, opening collections to schoolchildren, pensioners and families who might never otherwise cross the threshold. The objection that free museums chiefly benefit the educated middle classes is not without merit, yet it argues for better outreach rather than for withdrawing funding altogether.\n\nGrants to individual artists raise thornier questions. Supporters maintain that they protect creative risk-taking from the tyranny of the market; detractors counter that they foster dependency and reward those adept at filling in application forms rather than those with genuine talent.\n\nWeighing the two, I am persuaded that museums represent the more valuable use of limited funds. They preserve a shared heritage, serve as places of learning and reflection, and – crucially – remain accessible to everyone, rather than benefiting a select few. That said, the most enlightened cultural policies recognise that heritage and innovation are not rivals: today's experimental work, if it is allowed to emerge at all, may well become tomorrow's museum piece."]]},{"id":"c1w2","part":1,"kind":"essay","t":"An Ageing Population","in":"Your class has attended a lecture on the challenges of an ageing population. Write an essay discussing TWO of the issues in your notes, explaining which you think governments should address first, giving reasons. Write 220–260 words.","mail":"Challenges of an ageing population:\n• healthcare costs\n• pensions\n• loneliness among older people\n\nOpinions expressed:\n\"We can't afford to keep paying for longer retirements.\"\n\"Hospitals are already under pressure.\"\n\"Isolation can be as harmful as smoking.\"","pts":[["İlk konuyu tartış",["healthcare","hospital","pension","retire","lonely","loneliness","isolation"]],["İkinci konuyu tartış",["whereas","on the other hand","by contrast","turning to"]],["Öncelikli olanı gerekçelendir",["first","priority","more urgent","in my view","i would argue"]],["Akademik bağlaçlar",["however","furthermore","moreover","nevertheless","consequently"]]],"min":220,"max":260,"model":"As life expectancy rises and birth rates fall, many countries face the challenge of supporting a growing number of older citizens. Two of the most pressing issues are the rising cost of healthcare and the problem of loneliness.\n\nHealthcare costs are perhaps the most visible concern. Older people are more likely to suffer from long-term conditions, and hospitals in many countries are already struggling to cope. Without reform, governments may have to choose between raising taxes and reducing the quality of care, neither of which is politically attractive.\n\nLoneliness, by contrast, receives far less attention, yet its effects are serious. Research suggests that chronic isolation increases the risk of heart disease and dementia, and some studies have compared its impact to that of smoking. As families become more scattered, many older people spend days without speaking to anyone.\n\nI would argue that governments should address loneliness first, not because it is more important than healthcare, but because the two are closely connected. Older people who remain socially active tend to stay healthier for longer and need less medical treatment. Consequently, relatively inexpensive measures such as community centres, volunteer visiting schemes and intergenerational projects could reduce pressure on hospitals. Furthermore, these measures can be introduced quickly, whereas reforming healthcare systems takes years. Tackling isolation is therefore not only the humane choice but also a practical investment in the sustainability of public services.","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı yöntemleri seçerek","As populations age across much of the world, governments are being forced to rethink how they support older citizens. Of the many challenges this creates, the sustainability of pensions and the rising cost of healthcare are arguably the most urgent.\n\nPension systems in many countries were designed when people lived only a few years beyond retirement. Today, retirement can last two or three decades, and a shrinking working population must fund an ever-growing number of pensioners. Raising the retirement age is the most obvious solution, but it is deeply unpopular and particularly unfair to those in physically demanding jobs.\n\nHealthcare costs present a similar problem. Older people are more likely to live with chronic conditions requiring long-term treatment, and hospitals are already under severe strain. Without reform, waiting times will lengthen and the quality of care is likely to decline.\n\nIn my view, healthcare should be addressed first. While pension reform is undoubtedly necessary, its effects are gradual and can be planned over many years. A failing healthcare system, by contrast, has immediate and sometimes fatal consequences. Moreover, investment in preventive care – encouraging exercise, improving diet and detecting illness early – would allow people to remain healthy and active for longer, reducing costs and enabling many to work later in life if they wish. In this sense, addressing healthcare effectively would also ease the pressure on pensions, making it the more strategic priority."],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (C2'ye yakın)","The demographic shift towards an older population is frequently framed as a looming crisis. While this may be an exaggeration, it undeniably presents governments with difficult choices, of which the funding of pensions and the problem of loneliness are among the most pressing.\n\nThe pensions dilemma is essentially one of arithmetic. As the ratio of workers to retirees declines, maintaining current levels of support will require higher taxes, a later retirement age, or both. Each option carries political risk, which perhaps explains why so many governments have postponed meaningful reform for as long as possible.\n\nLoneliness, though less quantifiable, is no less serious. A growing body of research links chronic isolation to depression, cognitive decline and heart disease, with some studies suggesting its effects are comparable to smoking fifteen cigarettes a day. As families become more geographically dispersed, an increasing number of older people may go days without meaningful human contact.\n\nIt is tempting to prioritise pensions simply because the financial stakes are so visible. I would contend, however, that tackling loneliness should come first, precisely because it is so often neglected and because the solutions are relatively inexpensive. Community centres, befriending schemes and intergenerational housing projects require modest investment yet can transform lives – and, by keeping older people healthier, reduce demands on public services. Pension reform cannot be avoided indefinitely, but a society that ignores the isolation of its older members has, in a very real sense, already failed them."]]},{"id":"c1w3","part":2,"kind":"report","t":"Student Clubs","in":"The director of your college wants to know how the student clubs could be improved. Write a report evaluating the current clubs, identifying any problems and making recommendations. Write 220–260 words.","mail":"REPORT REQUEST\nStudent clubs\nPlease evaluate the current range of student clubs, explain any problems that limit participation, and recommend changes that would encourage more students to take part.","pts":[["Başlıklar kullan (report biçimi)",["introduction","findings","recommendations","conclusion","aim"]],["Mevcut durumu değerlendir",["currently","at present","range","popular","participation"]],["Sorunları belirt",["problem","however","limited","lack","clash"]],["Öneriler sun",["recommend","suggest","should","it would be advisable"]]],"min":220,"max":260,"model":"Report on student clubs\n\nIntroduction\nThe aim of this report is to evaluate the college's current student clubs and to suggest ways of increasing participation. It is based on a questionnaire completed by 85 students and interviews with four club leaders.\n\nCurrent situation\nThe college offers twelve clubs, ranging from debating to photography. The sports clubs and the drama society are well attended and highly rated. However, overall participation is low: only around a third of students belong to a club, and several clubs have fewer than five regular members.\n\nMain problems\nThe most frequently mentioned problem was timing. Most clubs meet immediately after classes, which clashes with part-time jobs and long journeys home. A second issue is awareness; many students, particularly first-years, were unaware that some clubs existed. Finally, a number of respondents felt that the range of clubs did not reflect current interests, with no options related to technology, the environment or mental well-being.\n\nRecommendations\nFirstly, it would be advisable to hold some club meetings at lunchtime, when most students are on site. Secondly, a club fair at the start of each term, together with a regularly updated online page, would raise awareness. Thirdly, students should be invited to propose new clubs, with a small budget available for those that attract sufficient interest.\n\nConclusion\nIf these changes are implemented, I am confident that participation would increase significantly, enriching students' college experience.","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı yöntemleri seçerek","Report on student clubs\n\nPurpose\nThis report assesses the effectiveness of the college's student clubs and proposes measures to increase participation. The findings are drawn from an online survey of 120 students and conversations with club organisers.\n\nOverview of current clubs\nThere are currently fourteen clubs, covering sport, music, drama, debating and languages. The football and music clubs are thriving, with waiting lists for new members. By contrast, the language and chess clubs struggle to attract more than a handful of students each week.\n\nKey problems\nThree main obstacles emerged. Firstly, many students consider the clubs too formal; several respondents described them as \"an extra lesson\" rather than a social activity. Secondly, there is little variety for students who are not interested in sport or performing arts. Thirdly, club information is spread across noticeboards, emails and social media, so students often miss announcements.\n\nRecommendations\nIn order to make clubs more appealing, organisers should be encouraged to include informal, social elements, such as games, trips or shared meals. The college could also introduce a simple process allowing students to start new clubs, perhaps requiring a minimum of eight interested members. Finally, it is recommended that all club information be gathered in a single online calendar.\n\nConclusion\nThe college clearly has a strong foundation of clubs, but several are failing to reach their potential. Implementing the measures above would require minimal expense and, in my view, would lead to a noticeable increase in student involvement."],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (C2'ye yakın)","Student clubs: evaluation and recommendations\n\nIntroduction\nAt the director's request, this report evaluates the college's extracurricular clubs, identifies factors limiting participation and puts forward a number of recommendations. It draws on a questionnaire completed by 96 students, attendance records for the past term and informal interviews with club leaders.\n\nStrengths\nThe college offers a respectable range of clubs, and several – most notably the debating society and the orchestra – have achieved considerable success in regional competitions. Members of these clubs spoke enthusiastically about the friendships and skills they had developed.\n\nAreas of concern\nDespite this, participation is uneven. Attendance records indicate that almost half of all club members belong to just three clubs, while others have dwindled to only a few regular attendees. Survey responses point to two underlying causes: a perception that clubs cater primarily to students who are already highly skilled, and a lack of clubs reflecting newer interests such as coding, sustainability or mental well-being.\n\nRecommendations\nIt would be advisable for established clubs to introduce beginner sessions, making it clear that no prior experience is required. In addition, the college should invite proposals for new clubs at the start of each academic year, supporting promising ideas with a small start-up budget. Finally, a brief \"taster week\", during which students could attend any club without commitment, would lower the barrier to joining.\n\nConclusion\nWhile the existing clubs have much to be proud of, they currently serve a relatively narrow group of students. If adopted, the measures outlined above should broaden participation significantly at minimal cost."]]},{"id":"c1w4","part":2,"kind":"proposal","t":"A Town Festival","in":"Your town council is planning to hold an annual festival and has asked residents for proposals. Write a proposal suggesting what the festival should include, explaining how it would benefit the town and addressing any potential problems. Write 220–260 words.","mail":"PROPOSALS WANTED\nA new town festival\nWhat kind of festival would bring the community together and attract visitors? Explain your ideas, how they would benefit the town, and how any problems could be dealt with.","pts":[["Başlıklar kullan (proposal biçimi)",["introduction","proposal","benefits","potential problems","conclusion"]],["Festival içeriği öner",["include","feature","stalls","music","food","workshop"]],["Faydaları açıkla",["benefit","local businesses","community","attract","boost"]],["Olası sorunlara çözüm",["problem","concern","could be addressed","to avoid","solution"]]],"min":220,"max":260,"model":"Proposal for an annual town festival\n\nIntroduction\nThis proposal outlines a festival celebrating the town's food and craft traditions, which I believe would bring residents together and attract visitors from the surrounding area.\n\nWhat the festival should include\nI propose a two-day weekend event held in the main square and along the riverside. The centrepiece would be a market featuring local food producers, bakers and craftspeople. Alongside this, there could be cooking demonstrations by local restaurants, workshops where children learn traditional crafts such as pottery and weaving, and live music in the evenings performed by local bands and school groups.\n\nBenefits for the town\nThe festival would give local businesses valuable publicity and additional income. It would also strengthen the community by involving schools, clubs and residents of all ages. In the longer term, a successful festival could put the town on the map as a destination, encouraging tourism throughout the year.\n\nPotential problems\nResidents in the centre may be concerned about noise and parking. These concerns could be addressed by ending live music at 10 p.m. and providing a free shuttle bus from car parks on the edge of town. Another risk is bad weather; a covered area near the town hall should therefore be reserved for key events.\n\nConclusion\nWith careful planning, a food and craft festival would be an affordable and inclusive way to celebrate the town's identity. I strongly recommend that the council consider this proposal.","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı yöntemleri seçerek","Proposal: a riverside music and arts festival\n\nAim\nThe purpose of this proposal is to suggest a festival that would celebrate local talent, bring residents of all ages together and attract visitors to the town.\n\nFestival content\nI propose an annual weekend festival along the riverbank each June. The main attraction would be two outdoor stages featuring local bands, choirs and school orchestras, with a well-known headline act on the Saturday evening. Alongside the music, an arts trail could display work by local painters and photographers in shop windows throughout the town centre. For families, a children's area with face painting, storytelling and craft workshops would ensure the event appealed to all ages.\n\nBenefits to the town\nSuch a festival would provide a valuable platform for local performers, many of whom have few opportunities to perform in public. Shops, cafés and hotels would benefit from increased trade, and the arts trail in particular would encourage visitors to explore the centre rather than remaining by the river.\n\nPossible difficulties\nThe most significant concerns are likely to be noise and litter. To address these, music could finish at 10.30 p.m., and volunteers from local schools and clubs could be recruited to keep the site clean in exchange for a donation to their organisation. Funding could be secured through sponsorship from local businesses and a modest charge for the headline concert only.\n\nConclusion\nA riverside festival of this kind would be relatively inexpensive to organise and would give the town an event to be genuinely proud of."],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (C2'ye yakın)","Proposal for a \"Festival of Lights\" in the old town\n\nIntroduction\nIn response to the council's invitation, this proposal sets out plans for an annual winter festival which, I believe, would enliven the town during its quietest months and strengthen community ties.\n\nThe concept\nTaking place over the first weekend of December, the festival would transform the historic streets of the old town with light installations created by local artists, students and community groups. Each evening, a lantern procession – led by children from local schools – would wind its way to the main square, where a programme of acoustic music and storytelling would follow. Stalls selling hot food and handmade crafts would line the route, with priority given to local producers.\n\nAnticipated benefits\nUnlike a summer festival, which would compete with countless similar events, a winter festival would fill a genuine gap, attracting visitors at a time when local businesses most need custom. Furthermore, the collaborative nature of the installations would involve hundreds of residents in the months leading up to the event, fostering a strong sense of ownership.\n\nAddressing potential concerns\nSafety on narrow, crowded streets would naturally be a priority; a one-way pedestrian route and clearly marked exits would help to manage crowds. Concerns about cost could be mitigated by using energy-efficient lighting and by seeking sponsorship from local firms, who would gain considerable visibility.\n\nConclusion\nThe Festival of Lights would offer a distinctive, inclusive celebration of the town's heritage and creativity. I would urge the council to give it serious consideration."]]},{"id":"c1w5","part":2,"kind":"letter","t":"Saving the Library","in":"You have read an article in a local newspaper arguing that the town library should be closed to save money. Write a letter to the editor responding to the article and explaining why the library should remain open. Write 220–260 words.","mail":"From the article:\n\"In an age when information is available online, libraries are an expensive luxury. The council should close the town library and use the money for more urgent needs.\"","pts":[["Uygun hitap ve kapanış",["dear","editor","yours faithfully","yours sincerely"]],["Makaleye yanıt ver",["article","claim","argue","suggests","i was"]],["Kütüphanenin faydalarını açıkla",["library","free","community","access","children","elderly"]],["Güçlü bir sonuç",["urge","strongly","therefore","in conclusion","i hope"]]],"min":220,"max":260,"model":"Dear Editor,\n\nI am writing in response to last week's article suggesting that the town library should be closed to save money. While I understand the pressure on the council's budget, I believe the article seriously underestimates the role the library plays in our community.\n\nThe writer claims that libraries are unnecessary because information is available online. This overlooks the fact that many residents do not have reliable internet access at home. For them, the library's free computers are essential for applying for jobs, accessing government services and helping children with homework.\n\nFurthermore, the library is far more than a collection of books. It hosts reading groups for children, language classes for newcomers and a weekly meeting for elderly residents, many of whom live alone. For these people, the library is one of the few warm, safe and free public spaces left in the town.\n\nThe article also describes the library as an \"expensive luxury\". Yet its annual budget is a tiny fraction of the council's total spending, and the cost of closing it – in terms of increased isolation, reduced literacy and fewer opportunities – would be far greater in the long run.\n\nRather than closing the library, the council could explore ways of making it more financially sustainable, such as renting rooms to local groups or seeking support from businesses.\n\nI therefore urge the council to reject this proposal and to recognise the library as the valuable public service it is.\n\nYours faithfully,\nZeynep Arslan","more":[["Örnek 2 · farklı yöntemleri seçerek","Dear Editor,\n\nI was dismayed to read last week's article calling for the closure of our town library. As someone who has used the library regularly for over twenty years, I would like to offer a rather different perspective.\n\nThe article suggests that, because information is now available online, libraries have become obsolete. This argument fundamentally misunderstands what a library offers. Online information is abundant, but it is also unreliable and often hidden behind paywalls. Librarians help people of all ages find trustworthy sources – a skill that has arguably never been more important.\n\nMoreover, the library serves as a lifeline for groups who are easily overlooked. Job seekers without home computers use it to apply for work; parents bring toddlers to free rhyme-time sessions; and students who lack a quiet place at home rely on it to study for their exams. Closing it would hit precisely those residents who can least afford alternatives.\n\nThe writer also claims that the money could be spent on \"more urgent needs\". Yet the library's running costs amount to a very small proportion of the council's budget, and the savings would be modest compared with the long-term costs to education and community life.\n\nIf the council is genuinely concerned about value for money, it should consider ways of making better use of the building, for instance by sharing it with other public services, rather than closing it altogether.\n\nI hope the council will listen to the many residents who value this vital service.\n\nYours faithfully,\nMehmet Yıldız"],["Örnek 3 · daha zengin dil (C2'ye yakın)","Dear Editor,\n\nYour recent article describing the town library as \"an expensive luxury\" has prompted considerable debate in our household, and I feel compelled to respond.\n\nThe author's central claim – that the internet has rendered libraries redundant – rests on a misconception. A library is not merely a warehouse for books, any more than a school is merely a building containing teachers. It is a public space in the truest sense: free, open to all and requiring no justification for one's presence. In an age when almost every other public place expects us to spend money, its value can hardly be overstated.\n\nConsider who would bear the cost of closure. Not those of us with fast broadband and shelves of books at home, but elderly residents who rely on the library for company and assistance with online forms, newcomers attending its language classes, and teenagers for whom it offers the only quiet place to study. To describe such provision as a luxury is, frankly, to misunderstand who it serves.\n\nI do not dispute that the council faces difficult financial choices. However, closing a library is a decision that is easy to make and almost impossible to reverse. Once the building is sold and the collection dispersed, it will not return when finances improve.\n\nI would therefore urge the council to explore alternatives – partnerships with local businesses, shared use of the building, volunteer support – before taking such an irreversible step.\n\nYours faithfully,\nAyşe Demir"]]}],"S":[{"id":"c1s1","p":1,"t":"Opinions and Experiences","ins":"Doğal ve gelişmiş cevaplar ver.","q":[["What kind of things do you find yourself spending too much time on?",30,["I have to admit","I find myself","far more than"],["I have to admit I find myself scrolling through news websites far more than I should. I tell myself I'm staying informed, but more often than not I'm just reading the same story from slightly different angles. It's become a sort of reflex – I'll pick up my phone to check the time and twenty minutes later I'm still reading.","Probably planning things that never actually happen. I'll spend hours researching a holiday, comparing flights and reading reviews of hotels, and then end up not going because of work. My friends joke that I enjoy the planning more than the trip itself, and they might have a point."]],["How has the area where you live changed in recent years?",30,["it's changed","considerably","for better or worse","these days"],["It's changed considerably, especially over the last five years or so. A lot of the old family-run shops have closed, and there are far more cafés and chain stores now. On the plus side, the area feels livelier and safer at night, but for better or worse, it's lost some of its character and feels much more like any other city.","Surprisingly little, actually. I live in quite a traditional neighbourhood, and many of the same families have been there for generations. The main difference is the traffic – there are far more cars now, and the streets where we used to play football as children are permanently lined with parked vehicles."]],["What's the best piece of advice you've ever been given?",30,["the best advice","stuck with me","it taught me"],["The best advice I've ever been given was from my grandmother, who told me never to make an important decision when I'm angry. It sounds obvious, but it's stuck with me, and it's saved me from a few regrettable emails and arguments. Now, whenever I feel annoyed, I make myself wait until the next morning before responding.","A teacher once told me that it's fine not to know the answer, as long as you know how to find it. At the time I was terrified of making mistakes in class, and that completely changed my attitude. I became much more willing to ask questions, and I think it's made me a better learner ever since."]]]},{"id":"c1s2","p":2,"t":"Learning from Others","ins":"Üç fotoğraftan ikisini seç ve 1 dakika karşılaştır.","img":[["👵📱👦","Fotoğraf 1: Bir genç, büyükannesine akıllı telefon kullanmayı öğretiyor.","c1-phone"],["🧑‍🔧🚗👨","Fotoğraf 2: Bir usta, çırağına araba tamiri gösteriyor.","c1-mechanic"],["👩‍🏫🌍👧","Fotoğraf 3: Bir öğretmen öğrencilere küre üzerinde ülkeleri gösteriyor.","c1-globe"]],"q":[["These pictures show people learning from others. Compare two of the pictures and say what the people might be learning, and how they might be feeling.",60,["whereas","I'd imagine","both","it seems","presumably"],["I'll compare the grandmother and the apprentice. In both pictures, someone is passing on practical knowledge, but the roles are interestingly reversed. In the first, a teenager is presumably teaching his grandmother how to use a smartphone, whereas in the second, an experienced mechanic is showing a younger man how to repair a car. The grandson is probably learning patience, since explaining technology to someone unfamiliar with it can be surprisingly challenging, while his grandmother is learning a skill that could help her stay in touch with family. I'd imagine she might be feeling slightly frustrated, or perhaps a little embarrassed at having to learn from her grandson, although she also seems amused. The apprentice, on the other hand, is probably concentrating hard, aware that he'll soon have to do the job on his own, and perhaps feeling a mixture of curiosity and pressure.","Let me compare the classroom and the car workshop. Both show people learning from someone more experienced, but the type of learning is quite different. In the classroom, the teacher is showing children countries on a globe, so they're presumably acquiring general knowledge about the world, whereas the apprentice is learning a very specific, hands-on skill that will directly affect his career. The children seem fascinated – geography can be quite exciting when it's presented visually – though one or two may simply be waiting for the bell. The apprentice probably feels a greater sense of responsibility, since a mistake on a real car could have serious consequences. I'd also guess the mechanic feels quite proud, passing on skills he's spent years developing."]],["Which situation do you think would be the most rewarding for the person teaching?",30,["I'd say","probably","because"],["I'd say the grandson, probably, because helping a family member become more independent is particularly satisfying. Seeing his grandmother send her first video message would be a lovely moment, and it might bring them closer too. It also reverses the usual dynamic, which I imagine both of them would find rather touching.","I'd argue it's the mechanic. Training an apprentice means seeing someone gradually become competent in a skill you've mastered, and there's a real sense of legacy in that – knowing your knowledge will be carried forward. In a trade like that, skills are often learned by watching rather than reading, so the relationship really matters."]]]},{"id":"c1s3","p":3,"t":"Choosing a Job","ins":"Önce seçenekleri tartış (2 dakika), sonra karar ver (1 dakika).","opts":["💰 salary","⏰ flexible hours","🌱 company values","📈 career progression","🤝 colleagues"],"q":[["Here are some factors people consider when choosing a job. Talk about how important these factors are for young people today.",120,["I'd argue","by contrast","that's a fair point","it depends on","increasingly"],["I'd argue that salary is still important, especially given how expensive housing has become for young people – it's very difficult to feel motivated if you can barely pay the rent. That said, it's no longer the only consideration. Flexible hours have become increasingly important since the pandemic; many young people now see the ability to work from home at least part of the week as essential rather than a perk. Company values are an interesting one. Surveys suggest young people care a lot about working for an ethical company, although I suspect that matters less when jobs are scarce and people simply need an income. Career progression depends very much on the individual – some people are highly ambitious and want to climb quickly, while others prioritise balance and stability. And colleagues are often underestimated. A supportive team can make even a fairly dull job enjoyable, whereas a toxic atmosphere can ruin a job that looks perfect on paper. So I'd say all of these factors matter, but their importance varies hugely from person to person.","Let's start with career progression, since I think that's often what young people focus on first. Many graduates want to know they won't be stuck in the same role for years, so clear opportunities for promotion and training are a major attraction. Salary is obviously important too, but interestingly, studies show that above a certain level it doesn't make people much happier. Company values have become far more prominent – young people are increasingly unwilling to work for organisations that damage the environment or treat workers badly, and they're very aware of a company's reputation online. Flexible hours are, in my view, almost expected nowadays. As for colleagues, I think people only realise how much they matter once they've started a job, so they're rarely the deciding factor when choosing between offers, even though they probably should be. If I had to rank them, I'd probably put flexibility and progression at the top for most people, with salary close behind, but I'd say the balance shifts depending on whether someone has family responsibilities or student debts to pay off."]],["Now decide which factor is the most important for young people when choosing a job.",60,["on balance","if we had to pick","I'd go for","although"],["On balance, I'd go for flexible hours. Although salary is clearly important, flexibility affects every aspect of life, from physical and mental health to relationships and hobbies. Young people seem increasingly unwilling to sacrifice their personal lives for their careers, and a job that allows them to organise their own time is often worth more to them than a higher salary. It also tends to benefit employers, since people who feel trusted are generally more loyal and productive, so it's arguably the factor that makes the biggest difference on both sides.","If I had to pick one, I'd choose colleagues. You spend more time with them than with almost anyone else, and they have an enormous impact on whether you enjoy going to work. A good team will support you, teach you and make difficult periods bearable, which in turn often leads to better progression and opportunities. Salary and flexibility can be negotiated, but you can't negotiate a good atmosphere – either it's there or it isn't – and that's why I think it deserves more attention than it usually gets when people are comparing job offers."]]]},{"id":"c1s4","p":4,"t":"Technology and Society","ins":"Görüşünü gerekçeler ve farklı bakış açılarıyla açıkla.","q":[["Do you think artificial intelligence will create more jobs than it destroys?",45,["it's hard to say","historically","on the other hand","I suspect"],["It's hard to say. Historically, new technologies have created more jobs than they've destroyed – the internet, for example, wiped out some jobs but created entire industries. However, AI is different in that it can perform cognitive tasks, not just physical ones, so it could affect lawyers, translators and programmers as well as factory workers. I suspect the real challenge won't be the total number of jobs but retraining people quickly enough.","I'm rather pessimistic, to be honest. Even if new jobs are created, they may require very different skills and be concentrated in particular cities or sectors. A fifty-year-old administrative worker can't easily become a data scientist. So while the overall number of jobs might recover eventually, many individuals could find themselves left behind in the meantime. Governments would need to invest heavily in lifelong education to prevent a sharp rise in inequality."]],["Is it the responsibility of governments to regulate technology companies?",45,["to a large extent","after all","the risk is","I'd argue"],["To a large extent, yes. After all, these companies now have enormous influence on public life, from what news we read to how our personal data is used, and they're accountable mainly to their shareholders. Governments have a duty to protect citizens. The risk, of course, is that regulation is always one step behind the technology, and poorly designed rules could stifle innovation.","I think regulation is necessary, but it needs to be international to be effective. These companies operate globally, so if one country introduces strict rules, they can simply move their operations elsewhere. That's why agreements between countries, like the European Union's data protection laws, seem to me the most promising approach. Of course, reaching international agreement is notoriously slow, but the alternative is a patchwork of rules that companies can easily avoid."]],["How might our relationship with technology change in the next decade?",45,["I imagine","is likely to","might well","I'd hope"],["I imagine we'll become more selective about the technology we use. There's already a backlash against constant connectivity – some schools have banned phones, and more people are deliberately buying simpler devices. I'd hope that over the next decade technology becomes something we use deliberately rather than compulsively, although the companies behind it obviously have strong incentives to keep us hooked.","I suspect technology will become even more invisible – built into our homes, cars and even clothes – so we'll interact with it constantly without noticing. That could make life more convenient, but it also raises serious questions about privacy, since we'll be generating data about almost everything we do. Who owns that data, and how it's used, is likely to become one of the defining political questions of the next decade."]]]},{"id":"c1s5","p":4,"t":"The Environment","ins":"Görüşünü gerekçeler ve örneklerle açıkla.","q":[["Should individuals or governments take the main responsibility for protecting the environment?",45,["both","ultimately","without","I'd argue"],["I'd argue both have a role, but ultimately governments have far more power. Individuals can recycle, cycle to work and eat less meat, and those choices do add up. But without regulation and investment in things like renewable energy and public transport, large-scale progress simply isn't possible. In fact, I think placing too much emphasis on individual responsibility can distract from the decisions that really matter.","I think individuals have to take the lead, because governments generally follow public opinion. If enough people change their behaviour and demand action, politicians will respond. We've seen this with plastic bags – consumer pressure led to bans in many countries. So individual action isn't just about its direct impact; it also sends a powerful political signal."]],["Do you think people are willing to change their lifestyles to help the environment?",45,["to some extent","as long as","in practice","I suspect"],["To some extent, as long as the changes are convenient and affordable. In practice, I suspect most people support green policies in principle but become far more reluctant when it affects their own habits, like flying on holiday or driving to work. That's why making the sustainable option the easiest and cheapest one is probably more effective than relying on people's goodwill.","More than they used to be, certainly. Younger generations in particular seem genuinely willing to make sacrifices – many of my friends have stopped eating meat or refuse to fly short distances. Whether that commitment will last as they get older and have families is another question, but the shift in attitudes is real. It's also visible in what people buy – second-hand clothes, for instance, have gone from being slightly embarrassing to genuinely fashionable."]],["Is economic growth compatible with protecting the environment?",45,["it's a complex","in theory","arguably","green technology"],["It's a complex question. In theory, green technology can create growth while reducing emissions, and arguably that's already happening in renewable energy, which now employs millions of people worldwide. But it requires a fundamental shift in how we measure success – for instance, accounting for environmental damage rather than simply counting economic output. If pollution and the loss of natural resources were included in calculations of growth, many apparently successful economies would look rather different.","I'm doubtful, if we mean unlimited growth. The planet's resources are finite, and so far, no country has managed to reduce its environmental impact as fast as its economy has grown. Some economists argue we need to focus on well-being rather than growth, and I find that argument increasingly persuasive, even if it's politically very difficult. No government wants to tell voters that their standard of living may not keep rising."]]]}]}}};
